work more efficiently it reminded him of a time&nbsp;
before these fluorescent lights and these same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:30.360
four walls of crusty bread with fresh butter&nbsp;
by the banks of the sin the little slot opened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:35.340
in the door and the tray was shoved through there&nbsp;
was bread just as he hoped a small dish of butter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:41.640
pot of jam and a cup of tea still seeming he&nbsp;
picked up the cup at first taking a deep breath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:48.120
ah an herbal blend with fresh lavender lovely he&nbsp;
couldn't see the guard through the window anymore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:54.060
but he called out to him just the same thank you&nbsp;
for the libations he still had his manners after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:59.580
all even in confinement he wished he could have&nbsp;
gotten a better look at the man seen the power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.580 --> 00:17:05.520
of his complexion a tremor in his hand he thought&nbsp;
he had spotted sweat beating on his forehead could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:10.740
he be ill the case required further examination&nbsp;
to be certain he sighed clutching the cup of tea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:16.500
tighter in frustration why wouldn't they just&nbsp;
let him work why must they scream at the sight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:24.120
of his efforts flee from his instruments it didn't&nbsp;
seem fair still the pursuit of science rarely was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:29.640
a glamorous one he had learned as much over the&nbsp;
centuries one day though history would look back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:35.400
on him kindly of this he could be certain he was&nbsp;
just settling in and beginning to spread butter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:41.220
across the admittedly stale bread when a horrible&nbsp;
sound shook him to attention he had heard the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:48.480
noise before though he had never seen its source&nbsp;
it was an ear splitting scream a whale of pure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:56.040
agony like the sound of a wounded wild animal he&nbsp;
had heard many many screams during his life from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.040 --> 00:18:01.380
patients and those who stood in the way of his&nbsp;
work but until he had been brought into custody of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:08.880
the foundation he had never heard a scream quite&nbsp;
like this one it was pure rage devastation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:15.720
suffering mixed together wet with tears and loud&nbsp;
enough to rip through human vocal folds whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:23.100
was crying out it was no mere man more screams&nbsp;
answered it and these were very much human these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:29.820
sounds were more familiar to him shrieks of pain&nbsp;
a fear of desperate but futile attempts to escape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:36.720
then the meaty thud of bodies falling to the floor&nbsp;
of torn off limbs hitting walls and windows a loud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:42.720
crash and the sound of something large moving&nbsp;
quite quickly through the halls scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:47.220
curiosity got the better of the doctor and he&nbsp;
found himself moving back to his little window&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:52.920
face pressed to the glass so hard his beak nearly&nbsp;
cracked it he couldn't see much of anything just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:58.620
guards running down the hall weapons drawn he saw&nbsp;
one of them fire heard the gunshot ring out but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.620 --> 00:19:05.400
what was he firing at then he saw it a pale blur&nbsp;
darting past the door whatever it was it didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:10.620
so much as flinch when the bullet ricocheted&nbsp;
off its skin a long thin arm crashed against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:16.020
the door knocking the doctor backwards into his&nbsp;
work table he steadied himself and climbed back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.020 --> 00:19:22.140
to his feet taking in the damage done to the door&nbsp;
it was crumpled in on itself nearly ripped off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:28.860
hinges and whatever had plowed into it was already&nbsp;
gone from the sounds of chaos in the distance it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:33.840
had disappeared around the corner with the guards&nbsp;
following it he inspected the ruins of the door&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:40.440
to his containment cell it was useless now hanging&nbsp;
loose and open well that was an invitation he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:46.500
hardly about to decline he grabbed his trusty bag&nbsp;
tossed his scalpel back inside and set off to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:51.780
what all the commotion was it was easy enough&nbsp;
to follow the trail of blood stark and vivid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:58.920
red against the white tile floor and the sound of&nbsp;
gunfire human screams and that loud long painful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.920 --> 00:20:05.460
whale he had heard before he walked at a leisurely&nbsp;
pace taking his time until the sound suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:12.420
stopped he rounded the corner and found a mound of&nbsp;
bodies guards and scientists beaten and bloodied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.420 --> 00:20:20.520
almost beyond recognition it was quiet here save&nbsp;
for one sound the sound of weeping there in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:26.880
corner huddled over with its face to the wall was&nbsp;
a pale thin figure its shoulders heaving with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:34.140
force of its sobs this poor soul was clearly in&nbsp;
great distress it was a peculiar sight hairless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:40.320
and white extended arms wrapped around itself&nbsp;
as it cried excuse me the doctor cried out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:47.340
the pitiful creature are you alright do you need&nbsp;
assistance it didn't answer it just continued to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.340 --> 00:20:53.700
cry had something so despondent been responsible&nbsp;
for this destruction the dozens of corpses the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.700 --> 00:21:00.780
smashed in walls the crumbled doors and shattered&nbsp;
windows it seemed impossible sure it was large and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.780 --> 00:21:07.140
looked strong but he had never seen a monster cry&nbsp;
before this couldn't be a dangerous creature not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.140 --> 00:21:13.500
when it was so sad he would help it but first he&nbsp;
would attend to some of these bodies he sat his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:19.260
bag on the ground and pulled out several vials of&nbsp;
liquid a set of syringes and a variety of other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:19.260 --> 00:21:25.980
surgical tools he might need now after such a long&nbsp;
hiatus he could resume his work in a meaningful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.980 --> 00:21:31.440
way he couldn't be certain how long he worked&nbsp;
reviving these poor souls and reconstructing their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:36.660
bodies as the pale creature wept in the corner&nbsp;
the sobbing faded into the background for a while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.660 --> 00:21:42.900
becoming a kind of white noise as he removed a&nbsp;
liver here placed it in a chest cavity there poked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:49.800
and prodded injected and extracted testing out new&nbsp;
methods alongside tried and true cures one by one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:56.340
the milky eyes fluttered open rigor mortis stiff&nbsp;
joints creaked into motion sallow faces looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.340 --> 00:22:02.460
his with the vacant gratitude he saw in so many&nbsp;
patients over the years he didn't need to thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:09.180
him with their words the work was its own reward&nbsp;
he expected more guards to arrive to attempt to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.180 --> 00:22:15.480
contain the situation but none came even as&nbsp;
the alarm blared overhead as for the morose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:21.060
creature he didn't seem to notice his presence at&nbsp;
all not even when he had brought all of the intact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:27.180
corpses back to life the patient shuffled around&nbsp;
the room aimlessly waiting for orders of some kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.180 --> 00:22:33.900
the doctor tapped one on the shoulder and handed&nbsp;
the reborn man a vial of thick black medicine give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:39.960
this to the poor fellow in the corner please it&nbsp;
wasn't much but it should calm him provide some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:46.500
relief from his suffering the corpse nodded mouth&nbsp;
hanging loose and open an eyeball dangling unseen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:52.260
from the socket he shuffled over to the strange&nbsp;
creature and held out the vial to it it turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.260 --> 00:22:58.680
lifting its head and as it locked eyes with the&nbsp;
cured patient something shifted in its face its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.680 --> 00:23:07.080
mouth opened wide impossibly wide and a treat that&nbsp;
same terrible sound as before tears streamed from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:13.320
its colorless eyes its arms shaking with unbridled&nbsp;
rage has its jaws locked around the patient's head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.320 --> 00:23:19.320
like a boa constrictor in one fluid motion&nbsp;
it swallowed the revived man whole while the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:25.080
doctor watched in shock he had been wrong this&nbsp;
was not an innocent creature caught in the wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.080 --> 00:23:32.040
place at the wrong time this creature whatever&nbsp;
it was was deeply sick he had never seen such an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:37.740
advanced aggressive case of the pestilence he'd&nbsp;
heard rumors of course but never encountered it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.740 --> 00:23:44.160
firsthand as a doctor he had sworn to do no harm&nbsp;
but in a drastic situation drastic measures have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:49.740
to be taken it was well known by himself and the&nbsp;
doctors at this foundation that he could cause any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:55.680
and all biological functions in an organism to&nbsp;
cease with a single touch and so he approached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.680 --> 00:24:01.560
the creature arm outstretched ready to administer&nbsp;
that necessary touch to protect the rest of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.560 --> 00:24:07.200
patients as he approached the creature turned to&nbsp;
him its eyes wide and blank an endless stream of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:12.360
tears pouring from them spilling onto the floor&nbsp;
it shrieked again mouth stretching wide enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.360 --> 00:24:19.680
to engulf his entire head and ran toward him at&nbsp;
a breakneck speed i am so sorry you are not well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:25.620
the doctor said simply as his hand pressed to the&nbsp;
creature's chest as soon as the tough hide of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.620 --> 00:24:31.200
doctor's hand which the uninformed might mistake&nbsp;
for gloves made contact with the unpigmented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.200 --> 00:24:38.400
skin of the beast its eyes closed its muscles went&nbsp;
slack and it collapsed to the ground with a mighty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:44.340
thigh the doctor paced around the fallen creature&nbsp;
taking in the sight then something strange caught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:51.420
his eye the creature's chest still rose and fell&nbsp;
was it his imagination he checked its pulse and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.420 --> 00:24:57.360
thought it was slow and faint and though was&nbsp;
slow and faint it was very much present the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.360 --> 00:25:03.540
creature was still alive it had merely been&nbsp;
rendered unconscious by his touch rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:03.540 --> 00:25:12.120
completely deceased curious very curious indeed he&nbsp;
muttered perhaps there were comorbidities present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:12.120 --> 00:25:18.480
other infections aside from the pestilence which&nbsp;
rendered the creature unnaturally strong resilient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:25.320
to the usual courses of treatment what would cause&nbsp;
these abilities this intense aggression it seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:25.320 --> 00:25:30.720
to be brought on whenever someone looked at the&nbsp;
entity's face if only he studied psychology more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:36.660
the science of the mind and its inner workings&nbsp;
since he had no experience with therapy nor was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:36.660 --> 00:25:41.340
he certain this creature could communicate using&nbsp;
language at all there was only one way to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:48.060
out more about how this creature's brain worked he&nbsp;
would have to take it apart and see for himself it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:52.980
was slow work getting the massive creature back&nbsp;
to the doctor's containment cell he required the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:57.780
help of his cured patients who grasped it&nbsp;
by its massive limbs and dragged the limp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.780 --> 00:26:03.240
body through the halls once back in a familiar&nbsp;
environment his work table ready and waiting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:08.700
doctor instructed his assistants to place the new&nbsp;
patient on the table it was a bit small unable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:08.700 --> 00:26:13.380
accommodate the creature's distended limbs but&nbsp;
if he attempted to use an official foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.380 --> 00:26:19.800
laboratory he risked discovery and subsequent&nbsp;
interruption so it would have to suffice first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.800 --> 00:26:25.800
he set up an iv stand filled with a vivid green&nbsp;
liquid it was easy to find a vein the creature's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:30.060
skin was nearly translucent now that he could&nbsp;
be certain the creature would not wake during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:36.540
surgery he could make the first incision scalpel&nbsp;
he held out a hand and his favorite surgical blade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:36.540 --> 00:26:42.180
was placed in it by one of the helpful patients&nbsp;
thank you he slid the scalpel along the hairline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:47.460
of the creature or where the hairline would be&nbsp;
if it weren't completely bald once the scalp was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:54.720
removed he set it aside for later when it could&nbsp;
be reattached bonsa please he held out his hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:54.720 --> 00:27:01.560
again and again his assistant gave him the proper&nbsp;
tool this however was when things got strange the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.560 --> 00:27:06.540
doctor had always been a deft hand at cutting he'd&nbsp;
once even received tutelage from the great robert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:12.060
liston but no matter how hard he tried to saw it&nbsp;
never left a scratch on the creature's skeleton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:12.060 --> 00:27:18.120
naturally this was somewhat frustrating he wanted&nbsp;
to study the creature's brain tissue to get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:23.160
sense for what was going up there neurologically&nbsp;
and he couldn't do that if it was impossible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.160 --> 00:27:29.040
saw off the creature's cranial cap he blunted two&nbsp;
of his favorite saws while trying thankfully there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.040 --> 00:27:33.900
was still a solution to access the beast's gray&nbsp;
matter a little trick he learned while studying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:40.080
the funerary practices of the ancient egyptians&nbsp;
he produced a long curved hook from his bag and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.080 --> 00:27:45.600
inserted it up the creature's nasal passage with&nbsp;
some fine maneuvering he eventually managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:50.760
remove the brain it was such a terrible shame that&nbsp;
he needed to do it piece by piece via the nasal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:56.640
passage but one makes do all that was left was&nbsp;
to sew the skin of the creature's head back into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:56.640 --> 00:28:02.820
place it was mid-stitch when a voice interrupted&nbsp;
his careful work nearly making him drop the needle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.820 --> 00:28:09.540
hey what are you doing he looked up to find a&nbsp;
guard aiming a gun at his face excuse me i am in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.540 --> 00:28:15.300
surgery at the moment please do not interrupt he&nbsp;
admonished the guard but the man did not listen or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.300 --> 00:28:20.400
lower his weapon in fact he shouted something&nbsp;
into the radio code words the doctor didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:27.540
recognize then he fired a bullet into the skull&nbsp;
of the patient standing at his side how dare you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.540 --> 00:28:33.000
the doctor cried readying himself to confront&nbsp;
the guard but it was too late dozens of other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:38.280
guards were swarming the room and neutralizing&nbsp;
his assistance some in hazmat suits grabbed his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:38.280 --> 00:28:43.740
arms and pulled him away from the creature on the&nbsp;
operating table no matter how hard he fought or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.740 --> 00:28:51.180
how loudly he protested then something incredible&nbsp;
happened something wonderful the creature opened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:51.180 --> 00:28:57.660
its eyes and sat up it looked directly at the&nbsp;
guard closest to it and the two saw one another's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:57.660 --> 00:29:04.620
faces the guard tensed preparing for the worst but&nbsp;
nothing came the creature simply stared placid and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:04.620 --> 00:29:12.120
quiet no screaming no tearing and flesh no mouth&nbsp;
opening wider and wider to swallow the manhole i&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:12.120 --> 00:29:18.600
did it the doctor shouted overcome with elation&nbsp;
i've cured you now begins the rest of your happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.600 --> 00:29:24.000
life he watched as the guards led the shockingly&nbsp;
calm creature away back to its containment cell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:28.920
the doctor's door was repaired and he was&nbsp;
returned to his state of captivity but he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:33.960
never forgot the patient he helped that day&nbsp;
and how marvelous it felt to do such a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:33.960 --> 00:29:40.980
deed meanwhile scp-096's brain regrew within&nbsp;
the hour and caused another massive containment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:40.980 --> 00:29:47.280
breach murdering a variety of researchers and&nbsp;
guards but the staff agreed not to tell scp-049&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:47.280 --> 00:29:52.860
about any of this better to just let him have&nbsp;
this one he really seemed like he needed a win

00:29:52.860 --> 00:29:58.680
A very strange anomaly sits in a humanoid
containment cell in the minimum security wing

00:29:58.680 --> 00:30:00.120
of Site 17.

00:30:00.120 --> 00:30:05.760
He walks, talks, and looks like a man, but
everything else is convoluted in a question

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:06.120
mark.

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:13.140
This is SCP-343 - Who, like his namesake,
God, is likely to cause arguments whenever

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:13.860
he’s brought up.

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:16.380
Is he the creator of all that exists?

00:30:16.380 --> 00:30:18.840
The basis for the Abrahamic Faiths?

00:30:18.840 --> 00:30:24.840
Or is he a pretender - A reality warper with
immense power and a predilection towards delusion?

00:30:24.840 --> 00:30:27.840
A courtesan of the House of Malidraug?

00:30:27.840 --> 00:30:28.500
Matthew?

00:30:28.500 --> 00:30:29.640
Methuselah?

00:30:29.640 --> 00:30:30.540
Yahweh?

00:30:30.540 --> 00:30:31.560
Who knows?

00:30:31.560 --> 00:30:36.240
Really, it depends on who you ask, and which
stories you choose to believe.

00:30:36.240 --> 00:30:41.160
And few people enjoy a good story more than
SCP-343 himself.

00:30:41.160 --> 00:30:46.260
If, one day, you take the time to visit him
in his room, and ask him to reveal a page

00:30:46.260 --> 00:30:50.700
in the long book of his personal history,
he might be kind enough to tell you a story.

00:30:50.700 --> 00:30:58.080
A story like this - Of an encounter with something
monstrous, that few others could hope to survive

00:30:58.080 --> 00:30:59.400
meeting face to face…

00:30:59.400 --> 00:31:01.380
Rewind a few thousand years.

00:31:01.380 --> 00:31:04.020
Nobody knows how many, exactly.

00:31:04.020 --> 00:31:10.140
God - as he chooses to dub himself - walked
across the cracked ground on worn sandals.

00:31:10.140 --> 00:31:14.880
It’d been some time since he’d seen an
animal around here, and even longer since

00:31:14.880 --> 00:31:16.440
he’d seen a human being.

00:31:16.440 --> 00:31:18.060
Not that this bothered him.

00:31:18.060 --> 00:31:21.840
He’d never been bothered by his own company
on a long walk like this.

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:25.080
Of course, he could have&nbsp;
sped up time or teleported,&nbsp;

00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:26.700
but where was the fun in that?

00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:29.280
He was a tourist in the world of sensation.

00:31:29.280 --> 00:31:30.720
Of experience.

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:35.040
Of flesh, bone, dirt, blood, and sand.

00:31:35.040 --> 00:31:39.300
After all, where’s the fun in creating a
whole universe if you can’t drop in now

00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:41.700
and then to visit and do as the Romans do?

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:45.360
Not that the Romans would be around for another
few thousand years.

00:31:45.360 --> 00:31:50.340
Even Atlas must occasionally shift the weight
of the globe from his shoulders for a jaunt

00:31:50.340 --> 00:31:55.380
around the cosmic neighborhood, and whatever
passes for “fresh air” in the vacuum.

00:31:55.380 --> 00:31:57.480
God whistled a tune to himself.

00:31:57.480 --> 00:32:00.840
It was a craggy, mountainous region he’d
found himself in.

00:32:00.840 --> 00:32:03.420
The distant peaks had frosted caps.

00:32:03.420 --> 00:32:07.980
A breathtaking place, where many truly had
their breaths taken away.

00:32:07.980 --> 00:32:12.360
How humans will so happily risk their lives
to do something extraordinary.

00:32:12.360 --> 00:32:14.340
It never ceased to amaze him.

00:32:14.340 --> 00:32:15.660
His stomach rumbled.

00:32:15.660 --> 00:32:18.000
Oh, how he enjoyed that sensation.

00:32:18.000 --> 00:32:22.320
One of the funny little quirks of this human
form that he’d weaved for himself.

00:32:22.320 --> 00:32:27.360
It was no reason to be concerned - If memory
served, from his last trip through the area

00:32:27.360 --> 00:32:31.380
a few decades before, there was a friendly
village not far from here.

00:32:31.380 --> 00:32:36.360
They had always accepted him as a genial stranger,
having no knowledge of his true power.

00:32:36.360 --> 00:32:41.580
God had always believed that a person’s
goodness is defined by how they treat those

00:32:41.580 --> 00:32:43.440
from whom they had nothing to gain.

00:32:43.440 --> 00:32:48.720
So it caused him great concern as he approached
the village, and saw great plumes of smoke

00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:50.220
rising into the sky.

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:54.840
He was so shocked by this that he decided
to break his rule about walking as a man,

00:32:54.840 --> 00:32:57.480
in case there was still some way he could
help.

00:32:57.480 --> 00:33:02.040
With a snap of his fingers, he disappeared
and reappeared in the center of the village’s

00:33:02.040 --> 00:33:02.640
town square.

00:33:02.640 --> 00:33:04.440
Total devastation.

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:07.200
Huts and houses had been torn asunder.

00:33:07.200 --> 00:33:09.480
Broken weapons lay on the ground.

00:33:09.480 --> 00:33:15.300
Some places were on fire, others smeared with
streaks of blood, like some terrible battle

00:33:15.300 --> 00:33:16.560
had occurred here.

00:33:16.560 --> 00:33:18.900
But something was wrong.

00:33:18.900 --> 00:33:20.220
No bodies.

00:33:20.220 --> 00:33:24.000
Not one, from defender or assailant.

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:29.520
How could a thriving village be so thoroughly
destroyed, and not leave a single corpse?

00:33:29.520 --> 00:33:34.740
It was an act so bizarre and depraved that
it even left God puzzling.

00:33:34.740 --> 00:33:40.260
That was another downside of his human form:
Here, on earth, he didn’t have access to

00:33:40.260 --> 00:33:41.280
true omniscience.

00:33:41.280 --> 00:33:47.160
How could a mere human mind, bound by the
constraints of linear time, ever truly comprehend

00:33:47.160 --> 00:33:48.480
the totality of existence?

00:33:48.480 --> 00:33:53.340
Even attempting to do that here would melt
the brain of his human body in its skull,

00:33:53.340 --> 00:33:56.100
and leave it dribbling out of his nose and
ears.

00:33:56.100 --> 00:34:01.260
Instead, he chose to walk around the ruins
of the village and investigate firsthand.

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:04.920
Arrows and broken spears and swords littered
the ground.

00:34:04.920 --> 00:34:09.840
Some buildings were demolished, but there
were no tracks or stray projectiles that could

00:34:09.840 --> 00:34:11.880
suggest the presence of siege weapons.

00:34:11.880 --> 00:34:15.720
No, these buildings looked like they were
ripped apart.

00:34:15.720 --> 00:34:18.120
Some even still had claw marks…

00:34:18.120 --> 00:34:22.140
What terrible beast could have set upon this
town and done this?

00:34:22.140 --> 00:34:27.900
Then, he heard a voice, quiet and pleading
beneath some nearby rubble.

00:34:27.900 --> 00:34:28.860
A survivor!

00:34:28.860 --> 00:34:34.260
He rushed over to the pile and evaporated
it with a thought, Underneath, a feeble old

00:34:34.260 --> 00:34:37.200
man, covered in stone dust, was quivering.

00:34:37.200 --> 00:34:42.180
God helped him up and guided him into one
of the few remaining huts still standing in

00:34:42.180 --> 00:34:42.480
the village.

00:34:42.480 --> 00:34:47.340
They both took seats - God held up two hands,
cradling empty space.

00:34:47.340 --> 00:34:52.800
Two cups suddenly occupied that space, both
filled with warm, healing tea.

00:34:52.800 --> 00:34:56.460
He passed the old man one of the cups while
sipping from his own.

00:34:56.460 --> 00:34:59.220
He asked the old man if he’d seen what had
happened.

00:34:59.220 --> 00:35:03.180
The old man told him, no, he hadn’t seen
anything in decades.

00:35:03.180 --> 00:35:05.700
He’d been rendered blind in his youth.

00:35:05.700 --> 00:35:11.220
Little did either of them know, that very
blindness was the only reason he was the sole

00:35:11.220 --> 00:35:12.900
survivor of the massacre.

00:35:12.900 --> 00:35:17.460
The blind man told God that one of the village’s
scouts had gone up into the mountain with

00:35:17.460 --> 00:35:18.360
a small hunting party.

00:35:18.360 --> 00:35:24.300
The group was gone for days, until one of
the members - the youngest among them - returned,

00:35:24.300 --> 00:35:27.300
weeping, frostbitten, and covered in blood.

00:35:27.300 --> 00:35:30.840
He said that his friends had been killed by
a beast in the mountains.

00:35:30.840 --> 00:35:34.560
Something that looked almost like a man, but
terribly wrong.

00:35:34.560 --> 00:35:35.940
And its face.

00:35:35.940 --> 00:35:38.580
Its awful, awful face.

00:35:38.580 --> 00:35:40.740
He would never forget it.

00:35:40.740 --> 00:35:44.880
He was just lucky to escape with his life
when the others were torn apart.

00:35:44.880 --> 00:35:49.080
But when the young man returned, he’d brought
the shadow of death with him.

00:35:49.080 --> 00:35:55.800
It was a curse that doomed the entire village
- men, women, and children - to a terrible

00:35:55.800 --> 00:35:56.280
fate.

00:35:56.280 --> 00:36:01.140
And that fate was upon them a mere hour after
the survivor had returned.

00:36:01.140 --> 00:36:06.120
Of course, there were gaps in the blind man’s
understanding, given he was lacking one of

00:36:06.120 --> 00:36:07.020
his major senses.

00:36:07.020 --> 00:36:10.260
But the sounds he could describe with perfect
clarity.

00:36:10.260 --> 00:36:12.600
It was faint and distant at first.

00:36:12.600 --> 00:36:15.900
That awful wail, and the galloping.

00:36:15.900 --> 00:36:20.220
Hands and feet thundering against the ground,
faster than any horse could move.

00:36:20.220 --> 00:36:22.680
Getting closer and closer and closer.

00:36:22.680 --> 00:36:27.840
Another villager saw it approaching and screamed,
then it was upon them.

00:36:27.840 --> 00:36:30.420
The villagers screamed but it screamed louder.

00:36:30.420 --> 00:36:36.060
Always wailing and shrieking and sobbing like
a monster crawled straight up from hell.

00:36:36.060 --> 00:36:38.580
People tried to fight it, by the sounds of
it.

00:36:38.580 --> 00:36:45.540
The blind man, with teary, blank eyes, recalled
the sounds of arrows nocking and swords clashing

00:36:45.540 --> 00:36:45.780
against…

00:36:45.780 --> 00:36:46.980
Something.

00:36:46.980 --> 00:36:50.700
But even their greatest warriors had screamed
and died.

00:36:50.700 --> 00:36:55.380
Those who saw it and tried to flee and hide
were slaughtered all the same.

00:36:55.380 --> 00:36:58.740
Soon enough, there were only two sounds left
in the village.

00:36:58.740 --> 00:37:02.880
The monster and the blind man, both weeping.

00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:05.280
He didn’t understand why it never took him.

00:37:05.280 --> 00:37:06.540
It wasn’t fair.

00:37:06.540 --> 00:37:08.220
It took everything else.

00:37:08.220 --> 00:37:14.100
To leave him here, alive, when everyone and
everything he’d ever known was destroyed,

00:37:14.100 --> 00:37:16.800
was a greater punishment than even death.

00:37:16.800 --> 00:37:22.140
After killing all of these innocents, the
monster had simply wandered off to the mountains

00:37:22.140 --> 00:37:28.260
again, the sound of its quiet sobs getting
smaller and smaller until it was gone altogether.

00:37:28.260 --> 00:37:32.940
God comforted the blind man as he wept for
the loss of all his loved ones.

00:37:32.940 --> 00:37:37.260
He told the blind man that he would venture
up to the mountains himself and confront the

00:37:37.260 --> 00:37:42.900
creature on its own territory, and, at the
very least, find out why it had done this

00:37:42.900 --> 00:37:43.560
terrible thing.

00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:47.520
But first, he must relocate the blind man
to a safer place.

00:37:47.520 --> 00:37:51.240
He placed a hand on the blind man’s shoulder
and he vanished.

00:37:51.240 --> 00:37:54.660
He would appear in another friendly village,
miles away.

00:37:54.660 --> 00:38:00.660
God sent a silent message into the minds of
every villager: Take good care of this man.

00:38:00.660 --> 00:38:04.980
He has undergone horrors you can’t even
imagine.

00:38:05.700 --> 00:38:12.060
Your kindness will be rewarded later, for
that, you have my promise.

00:38:12.060 --> 00:38:16.200
God sighed and turned his tired eyes to the
distant mountains.

00:38:16.200 --> 00:38:22.320
A monster lurked up there - Perhaps one of
his own creations, or maybe a corruption of

00:38:22.320 --> 00:38:23.280
one of his creations.

00:38:23.280 --> 00:38:30.000
Either way, whatever existed without his knowledge
existed without his consent, and he intended

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:32.160
to know of the beast in the mountains.

00:38:32.160 --> 00:38:36.480
Though given what he’d seen already, he
didn’t expect to receive a warm welcome

00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:38.220
from this murderous demon.

00:38:38.220 --> 00:38:42.420
Miles away, up in the mountains, the creature
licked the blood from its cracked lips.

00:38:42.420 --> 00:38:47.700
It looked like it might once have been a human
being or something that aspired to humanity,

00:38:47.700 --> 00:38:50.100
or mocked it with its very existence.

00:38:50.100 --> 00:38:58.200
It was a huge, gangling beast, skin alabaster,
eyes empty and soulless, dribbling rivulets

00:38:58.200 --> 00:39:01.140
of burning tears down a hideous, gaunt face.

00:39:01.140 --> 00:39:06.900
It crawled into the frozen mouth of a cave
with great icicle fangs, wheezing and weeping.

00:39:06.900 --> 00:39:10.080
All it ever wanted was to be alone.

00:39:10.080 --> 00:39:12.360
Why did they have to keep interfering?

00:39:12.360 --> 00:39:14.040
Didn’t they know what happened?

00:39:14.040 --> 00:39:16.980
All the terrible things they’d made it do…

00:39:16.980 --> 00:39:22.020
The creature curled its long, gangly body
into the fetal position, scratching great

00:39:22.020 --> 00:39:26.220
ruts into the sides of its bald cranium with
long, sharp fingers.

00:39:26.220 --> 00:39:27.600
Terrible things.

00:39:27.600 --> 00:39:30.060
Terrible, terrible things…

00:39:30.060 --> 00:39:33.720
And then there was a brilliant flash, just
a few feet away.

00:39:33.720 --> 00:39:39.000
The monster was surprised, it turned to see
a figure silhouetted in the mouth of the cave.

00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:41.280
He wore sandals and thin robes.

00:39:41.280 --> 00:39:45.240
His eyes glowed with a kind of power that
the monster didn’t recognize.

00:39:45.240 --> 00:39:50.940
This stranger stared at the monster without
an ounce of fear in his heart: He stared right

00:39:50.940 --> 00:39:52.920
into its eyes, unwavering.

00:39:52.920 --> 00:39:53.640
No!

00:39:53.640 --> 00:39:54.600
No, no, no, no!

00:39:54.600 --> 00:39:56.820
It could feel it again.

00:39:56.820 --> 00:39:59.160
The rage bubbling up, deep down.

00:39:59.160 --> 00:40:01.680
A cauldron of seething anger.

00:40:01.680 --> 00:40:04.980
It hated the feeling, like being lowered into
a vat of molten metal.

00:40:04.980 --> 00:40:08.520
Unspeakable fire and pain coursing through
every vessel.

00:40:08.520 --> 00:40:13.740
It began to weep and scream while the stranger
in the cave mouth just watched, not moving

00:40:13.740 --> 00:40:14.100
a muscle.

00:40:14.100 --> 00:40:16.560
“Do you know who I am?”

00:40:17.520 --> 00:40:22.860
the stranger asked with a deep voice that
betrayed almost infinite power, knowledge,

00:40:22.860 --> 00:40:23.880
and wisdom.

00:40:23.880 --> 00:40:26.160
But it wouldn’t change the outcome here.

00:40:26.160 --> 00:40:30.780
The monster bounded at him at speeds that
wouldn’t be seen again until jet planes

00:40:30.780 --> 00:40:33.540
and bullet trains are invented millennia into
the future.

00:40:33.540 --> 00:40:39.720
Its jaws were hanging impossibly wide, fangs
borne, its arms extended and deadly claws

00:40:39.720 --> 00:40:40.680
ready to strike.

00:40:40.680 --> 00:40:45.360
But before those terrible hands could close
around the stranger, he vanished.

00:40:45.360 --> 00:40:48.480
The monster stumbled and rolled across the
snow, confused.

00:40:48.480 --> 00:40:50.340
What trickery was being used here?

00:40:50.340 --> 00:40:55.200
“I’ll take that as a no,” said the voice
from behind him.

00:40:55.200 --> 00:40:58.200
“You ought to show your father some respect.

00:40:58.200 --> 00:41:02.880
More respect than you gave to those poor people
down in the village, at least.”

00:41:03.780 --> 00:41:07.980
Seething, the creature turned and saw the
stranger standing back in the darkness of

00:41:07.980 --> 00:41:09.540
the cave, staring at him.

00:41:09.540 --> 00:41:13.560
But the beast didn’t have the capacity for
awe or holy terror.

00:41:13.560 --> 00:41:15.300
Only violence.

00:41:15.300 --> 00:41:19.260
Boundless, limitless, unstoppable violence.

00:41:19.260 --> 00:41:22.620
It darted towards the stranger again, trying
to strike at him.

00:41:22.620 --> 00:41:25.560
Somehow, it was like fighting an empty robe.

00:41:25.560 --> 00:41:28.920
Not a single one of its deadly strikes seemed
to hit the stranger.

00:41:28.920 --> 00:41:34.380
The stranger leaped backwards, putting some
space between himself and the monster but

00:41:34.380 --> 00:41:35.760
still not breaking a sweat.

00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:38.400
He breathed in deeply, then exhaled.

00:41:38.400 --> 00:41:43.560
The breath came out like a mighty typhoon,
shocking even the monster with its sudden

00:41:43.560 --> 00:41:43.980
force.

00:41:43.980 --> 00:41:49.680
It was blown backwards, a leaf in the wind,
until its long claws dug into the ground and

00:41:49.680 --> 00:41:50.880
anchored it in place.

00:41:50.880 --> 00:41:53.940
The stranger gave a wry smile at this, impressed.

00:41:53.940 --> 00:41:59.760
“My, my, you’re certainly a tenacious
one, aren’t you?” he said.

00:41:59.760 --> 00:42:03.540
“Perhaps we can talk for a little while
instead of fighting.

00:42:03.540 --> 00:42:06.900
I want to know why you killed all those people.”

00:42:07.740 --> 00:42:08.460
No answer.

00:42:08.460 --> 00:42:13.920
The beast roared, its mighty limbs pounding
into the ground as it closed the gap between

00:42:13.920 --> 00:42:17.160
itself and the stranger in fractions of a
second.

00:42:17.160 --> 00:42:18.960
It would kill him.

00:42:18.960 --> 00:42:19.980
Rend him.

00:42:19.980 --> 00:42:22.920
Destroy him just like all the others.

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:24.900
He’d left it no choice!

00:42:24.900 --> 00:42:27.660
Suddenly, the ground below it seemed to give
away.

00:42:27.660 --> 00:42:29.820
The creature was confused.

00:42:29.820 --> 00:42:34.260
It looked down to see that the ancient stone
below had somehow taken on the properties

00:42:34.260 --> 00:42:36.300
of a liquid, and it was sinking.

00:42:36.300 --> 00:42:38.640
The beast panicked and began to thrash.

00:42:38.640 --> 00:42:43.140
It was a strong swimmer, but it didn’t expect
to need to swim here.

00:42:43.140 --> 00:42:49.260
The shock was too much, and soon, the ground
submerged it entirely, muffling its terrible

00:42:49.260 --> 00:42:50.520
roars and shrieks.

00:42:50.520 --> 00:42:54.960
And just like that, the ground was solid again,
trapping the beast inside.

00:42:54.960 --> 00:42:57.600
The stranger stepped forward and looked at
the ground.

00:42:57.600 --> 00:43:01.560
“A much-needed time out,” he said.

00:43:01.560 --> 00:43:05.220
“You do yourself no good struggling like
this.”

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:11.520
Despite its terrible capacity for evil, God
couldn’t help but admire the beast, at least

00:43:11.520 --> 00:43:12.660
on the level of construction.

00:43:12.660 --> 00:43:16.440
It was so pared down, so unburdened.

00:43:16.440 --> 00:43:19.440
A killer to the core, but seemingly unkillable.

00:43:19.440 --> 00:43:21.420
Had he made this creature?

00:43:21.420 --> 00:43:26.400
Billions of species and the species those,
in turn, had created through billions of years

00:43:26.400 --> 00:43:31.560
of breeding and evolution, and somewhere along
the line, this thing happened.

00:43:31.560 --> 00:43:37.020
It was easy even for the universe’s creator
to lose track of some of the tinier variables.

00:43:37.020 --> 00:43:41.760
And in the grand scheme of things, even this
monster was still a tiny variable.

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:46.860
But right here, right now, it was still one
hell of a problem.

00:43:46.860 --> 00:43:49.020
The ground rumbled below God.

00:43:49.020 --> 00:43:50.340
Cracks formed.

00:43:50.340 --> 00:43:52.080
The mountain peak shook.

00:43:52.080 --> 00:43:57.960
God raised an eyebrow, genuinely impressed,
as the monster ripped free of its stone prison

00:43:57.960 --> 00:43:59.400
and re-entered the fray.

00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:03.720
It roared and screamed still, its blank eyes
fixed on him.

00:44:03.720 --> 00:44:06.840
Its skeletal body throbbed and heaved with
power.

00:44:06.840 --> 00:44:12.000
Unlike any other creature in nature, it was
almost like, the longer their conflict went

00:44:12.000 --> 00:44:14.700
on, the more energized the beast became.

00:44:14.700 --> 00:44:15.900
God sighed.

00:44:15.900 --> 00:44:18.000
All those poor villagers.

00:44:18.000 --> 00:44:20.580
They never stood a chance against this monster.

00:44:20.580 --> 00:44:24.000
It lunged for him, even faster and stronger
than before.

00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:28.860
He teleported out of the way in the nick of
time and the beast’s claws cleaved through

00:44:28.860 --> 00:44:30.960
a nearby cave wall, effortless.

00:44:30.960 --> 00:44:34.500
God materialized nearby, but he didn’t have
time to speak.

00:44:34.500 --> 00:44:37.800
The beast lunged again, and again, and again.

00:44:37.800 --> 00:44:42.960
Every time he reappeared, the beast went for
him with impossible speed.

00:44:42.960 --> 00:44:46.320
Deciding to widen the&nbsp;
playing field, God teleported&nbsp;

00:44:46.320 --> 00:44:47.220
to the top of the mountain.

00:44:47.220 --> 00:44:51.960
The creature, somehow sensing his presence,
vaulted upwards and tunneled through the roof

00:44:51.960 --> 00:44:56.880
of the cave, bursting out of the ground in
front of God, who was floating just slightly

00:44:56.880 --> 00:44:57.480
off the ground.

00:44:57.480 --> 00:45:02.640
“It would be wise of you to stop,” God
carefully intoned.

00:45:02.640 --> 00:45:06.300
“All this time, you know, I’ve been going
easy on you.

00:45:06.300 --> 00:45:09.900
You don’t want to find out what the wrath
of God looks like.”

00:45:10.620 --> 00:45:12.660
Storm clouds were gathering above.

00:45:12.660 --> 00:45:15.240
Mighty thunder roared across the sky.

00:45:15.240 --> 00:45:17.220
The beast was undeterred.

00:45:17.220 --> 00:45:19.320
It roared and galloped towards God.

00:45:19.320 --> 00:45:22.620
And God, in turn, called down a response.

00:45:22.620 --> 00:45:28.080
A volley of lightning the likes of which the
world has never seen before or since struck

00:45:28.080 --> 00:45:29.400
down on the charging monster.

00:45:29.400 --> 00:45:32.880
The sudden white flash could sting the eyes
from miles away.

00:45:32.880 --> 00:45:38.280
The monster shrieked from the blast, feeling
its flesh lift off its bones and atomize in

00:45:38.280 --> 00:45:40.380
the sheer heat of the electricity around it.

00:45:40.380 --> 00:45:42.240
It could smell itself cooking.

00:45:42.240 --> 00:45:47.400
The lightning blasts only lasted for a few
seconds, but for the beast, it felt like an

00:45:47.400 --> 00:45:47.700
eternity.

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:52.080
When the onslaught stopped, the air was still
heavy with electrical potential.

00:45:52.080 --> 00:45:56.640
God stared down at the black scorch mark on
the side of the mountain where the creature

00:45:56.640 --> 00:45:57.480
had been standing.

00:45:57.480 --> 00:46:01.080
All the snow within the mile had been evaporated
by the blast.

00:46:01.080 --> 00:46:07.320
It was a raw display of the power of nature
that would make even Zeus tremble in his sandals.

00:46:07.320 --> 00:46:08.340
And yet…

00:46:08.340 --> 00:46:10.860
There was still movement.

00:46:10.860 --> 00:46:15.120
Something started to get up from the burnt
patch where nothing should be left alive.

00:46:15.120 --> 00:46:21.180
A blackened skeleton, rising shakily from
the ash but still very much alive.

00:46:21.180 --> 00:46:26.100
As it started to rise, new flesh began growing
over the bones, little by little.

00:46:26.100 --> 00:46:28.860
Even God was astonished by the sight of it.

00:46:28.860 --> 00:46:34.500
He’d never seen a creature cling so ardently
to life in spite of having truly unsurmountable

00:46:34.500 --> 00:46:36.120
power amassed against it.

00:46:36.120 --> 00:46:40.140
It was up against God, and still, it fought.

00:46:40.140 --> 00:46:45.960
The monster tottered on its freakishly long
limbs, still disoriented, unusually staggered

00:46:45.960 --> 00:46:49.140
for a creature driven by such single-minded,
violent purpose.

00:46:49.140 --> 00:46:55.320
When enough of its face grew back to do so,
it began to weep and sob again, tears streaking

00:46:55.320 --> 00:46:56.700
down its terrible face.

00:46:56.700 --> 00:47:02.160
Looking at this creature, after all this,
God couldn’t help but feel a new emotion:

00:47:02.160 --> 00:47:02.760
Pity.

00:47:02.760 --> 00:47:05.940
He lowered himself to the ground and approached
the creature.

00:47:05.940 --> 00:47:12.360
Like none had ever done before, he gathered
it up into his arms and held it, feeling its

00:47:12.360 --> 00:47:14.100
heaving, wretched sobs against him.

00:47:14.100 --> 00:47:16.320
This beast was in so much pain.

00:47:16.320 --> 00:47:19.200
He could feel it, radiating from within.

00:47:19.200 --> 00:47:23.940
“Speak, my son,” God said in a soft, fatherly
voice.

00:47:23.940 --> 00:47:28.020
And for the first and only time, the monster
spoke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:36.060 --> 00:47:37.295
“Can’t look.

00:47:37.295 --> 00:47:37.307
Can’t see.

00:47:37.307 --> 00:47:37.920
Make me hurt people.

00:47:37.920 --> 00:47:40.678
Don’t want hurt people.

00:47:40.678 --> 00:47:43.657
Can’t look, can’t look.

00:47:43.657 --> 00:47:44.760
Please.”

00:47:44.760 --> 00:47:50.580
That was all it managed to choke out before
devolving back into unintelligible babble.

00:47:50.580 --> 00:47:52.260
But it was enough.

00:47:52.260 --> 00:47:54.960
Enough for God to understand its pain.

00:47:54.960 --> 00:48:00.060
He did not know if it would be right to change
the monster’s nature - is it ever right

00:48:00.060 --> 00:48:02.040
to truly change anyone’s nature?

00:48:02.040 --> 00:48:07.140
- but it was within his almost limitless power
to grant it one reprieve from its pain.

00:48:07.140 --> 00:48:09.840
He settled the beast in the snow below him.

00:48:09.840 --> 00:48:12.060
It was quiet and still.

00:48:12.060 --> 00:48:16.320
And God said unto the beast, “Rest now,
child.

00:48:16.920 --> 00:48:20.400
Rest for thousands of years, if you must.

00:48:20.400 --> 00:48:25.500
I hope only that when you eventually awaken,
you feel differently…”

00:48:26.160 --> 00:48:29.940
And so another story from the catalog of SCP-343.

00:48:29.940 --> 00:48:35.460
Of course, it leaves us with certain questions,
chief among them being: Is it true?

00:48:35.460 --> 00:48:41.820
Did 343 and 096 have this chance encounter
long ago, or is this just another tall tale

00:48:41.820 --> 00:48:44.460
from an anomaly who fancies himself a deity?

00:48:44.460 --> 00:48:47.700
We have our truth, and you have yours.

00:48:47.700 --> 00:48:50.460
Let us know what you believe down below in
the comments.

00:48:50.460 --> 00:48:53.880
What do you do, when the sun turns wrong?

00:48:53.880 --> 00:48:57.600
When light, which once represented life, joy,&nbsp;

00:48:57.600 --> 00:49:01.080
and warmth becomes a symbol&nbsp;
of death and destruction?

00:49:01.080 --> 00:49:06.720
Do you hide inside, shutter the windows, keep
out the light by any means necessary?

00:49:06.720 --> 00:49:12.480
Do you retreat underground, where the sun’s
rays cannot penetrate, burrowing into the

00:49:12.480 --> 00:49:13.440
dark and the cold?

00:49:13.440 --> 00:49:20.040
Do you simply give yourself to it, accept
that there is no escaping something so vast,

00:49:20.040 --> 00:49:26.040
so far-reaching as sunlight itself, and allow
yourself to be lost in the end of all things?

00:49:26.700 --> 00:49:33.030
We’ve covered this possibility before, SCP-001,
“When Day Breaks”, or S.D.

00:49:33.030 --> 00:49:34.140
Locke’s Proposal.

00:49:34.140 --> 00:49:39.000
It’s difficult to know what any given person
might do in such harrowing circumstances.

00:49:39.000 --> 00:49:43.140
After all, we have always learned to love
the light and fear the darkness.

00:49:43.140 --> 00:49:49.200
It turns our very understanding of reality
upside down to have that paradigm shifted,

00:49:49.200 --> 00:49:53.280
the dark becoming safety and the light becoming
death.

00:49:53.280 --> 00:50:00.840
The source of all life on earth, of all warmth,
transforming in an instant from the sun as

00:50:00.840 --> 00:50:07.560
we know it to an Apollyon Class SCP that melts
all living organisms that cross into its gaze.

00:50:07.560 --> 00:50:10.560
But what about a being that has always feared
the light?

00:50:10.560 --> 00:50:15.660
Something that prioritizes solitude, and never
ever wants to be seen?

00:50:15.660 --> 00:50:18.240
How might it live out those darkened days?

00:50:18.240 --> 00:50:23.640
Today, we’re taking a look at what happens
when Daybreak meets a familiar face, even

00:50:23.640 --> 00:50:28.440
if a constantly hidden one: SCP-096.

00:50:28.440 --> 00:50:33.540
SCP-096, or “The Shy Guy,” is something
of a celebrity around here.

00:50:33.540 --> 00:50:39.840
It is a humanoid creature with long, distended
arms, pale skin, and a jaw that can open four

00:50:39.840 --> 00:50:42.300
times wider than an ordinary human’s.

00:50:42.300 --> 00:50:49.860
There is only one thing, above all else, that
this entity desires: for no one to see its

00:50:49.860 --> 00:50:50.040
face.

00:50:50.040 --> 00:50:55.680
When someone views its face, it flies into
an uncontrollable rage, and seems to have

00:50:55.680 --> 00:50:58.980
no other option but to destroy the person
who has seen it.

00:50:58.980 --> 00:51:04.860
So, it is unlikely that a creature so intent
on solitude would be in much danger from the

00:51:04.860 --> 00:51:05.880
transformed sun.

00:51:05.880 --> 00:51:10.560
In fact, it might even be a positive thing,
and for some time it was.

00:51:10.560 --> 00:51:16.560
With so many human beings gone, reduced to
gelatinous masses with no desire but to slither

00:51:16.560 --> 00:51:21.300
around in the ghoulish sunlight and merge
with one another, there were fewer people

00:51:21.300 --> 00:51:23.340
to observe his face than ever.

00:51:23.340 --> 00:51:29.520
He could stay inside and know, with almost
complete certainty, that no one would come

00:51:29.520 --> 00:51:30.360
to bother him.

00:51:30.360 --> 00:51:36.120
No one was coming to look at him, and there
were no more visitors, researchers poking

00:51:36.120 --> 00:51:38.940
and prodding, that might sneak a look at his
face.

00:51:38.940 --> 00:51:44.640
And so, he stayed there, in the room that
had served as his containment unit before

00:51:44.640 --> 00:51:46.560
the world turned upside down.

00:51:46.560 --> 00:51:51.840
The masses of flesh that once were humans
did not concern him, as they never looked

00:51:51.840 --> 00:51:52.440
at his face.

00:51:52.440 --> 00:51:55.920
All he did was stay in his room, and never
go outside.

00:51:55.920 --> 00:52:01.380
The outside frightened him terribly, though
he was not concerned with the deadly sunlight

00:52:01.380 --> 00:52:04.980
or the possibility of it melting him as it
had so many others.

00:52:04.980 --> 00:52:10.740
He was much more afraid that someone, some
unlikely survivor clinging to their original

00:52:10.740 --> 00:52:12.660
life, might look at him.

00:52:12.660 --> 00:52:15.240
That they might see his face.

00:52:15.240 --> 00:52:18.180
That was simply too distressing to risk.

00:52:18.180 --> 00:52:23.760
He would much rather stay alone, in his old
cell, weeping softly in the shadows.

00:52:23.760 --> 00:52:28.560
One day, though we couldn’t say how many
days after the end of things it had been,

00:52:28.560 --> 00:52:31.080
he was weeping alone in his former cell.

00:52:31.080 --> 00:52:36.600
The days blurred together, spent the same
way, and time had lost much of its meaning.

00:52:36.600 --> 00:52:41.640
Through the echoing sound of his sobs, and
the sloshing sounds of the creatures outside,

00:52:41.640 --> 00:52:43.920
a woman’s voice cut through.

00:52:43.920 --> 00:52:47.340
It was faint, but he could make out what she
was saying.

00:52:47.340 --> 00:52:51.840
She was calling out for help, asking if anyone
had any food or fresh water.

00:52:51.840 --> 00:52:57.900
The sound was getting louder, closer, accompanied
by the disconcerting thump of her footsteps.

00:52:57.900 --> 00:53:02.280
She was coming down the hall, as much as it
could be called a hall anymore.

00:53:02.280 --> 00:53:07.020
She was coming toward his room, to disrupt
his miserable peace.

00:53:07.020 --> 00:53:11.880
He heard her steps cross the threshold of
his room, and her voice broke into a terrified

00:53:11.880 --> 00:53:16.980
scream, no doubt at the unexpected sight of
a pale, naked creature weeping in the corner

00:53:16.980 --> 00:53:19.320
of what she expected to be an empty room.

00:53:19.320 --> 00:53:24.540
Startled by the first loud sound he’d heard
in a long time, in a world that had mostly

00:53:24.540 --> 00:53:29.880
been silent since the initial outpouring of
agonized screams had faded into the soft slap

00:53:29.880 --> 00:53:32.520
of flesh and slime on the ground, he turned
around.

00:53:32.520 --> 00:53:37.080
He shouldn’t, ordinarily he wouldn’t,
but he couldn’t stop himself.

00:53:37.080 --> 00:53:43.860
There, he saw a woman, wide eyes filled with
tears, face streaked with dirt, a small child

00:53:43.860 --> 00:53:46.920
next to her holding her hand and clinging
to her leg.

00:53:46.920 --> 00:53:49.920
Just as he saw the woman, she saw him.

00:53:49.920 --> 00:53:52.140
She saw…his face.

00:53:52.140 --> 00:53:59.460
Anger boiled over inside of him, and he let
out a scream, wild, primal, and filled with

00:53:59.460 --> 00:54:01.020
inhuman rage.

00:54:01.020 --> 00:54:06.240
She could not be looking at him, seeing his
face.

00:54:06.240 --> 00:54:07.740
He couldn’t let her.

00:54:07.740 --> 00:54:12.780
The woman ran with the child in tow, panicked
by the wild roar and the sight of him standing

00:54:12.780 --> 00:54:13.860
upright before her.

00:54:13.860 --> 00:54:18.960
The anger faded as she left his site, making
her way down the hall and to the stairs.

00:54:18.960 --> 00:54:22.800
But then, he remembered: she had seen his
face.

00:54:22.800 --> 00:54:24.480
He could not let her leave.

00:54:24.480 --> 00:54:29.340
It was time to do something he never thought
he’d have to do again: hunt.

00:54:29.340 --> 00:54:33.840
His heightened senses picked up the sound
of muffled footsteps, coming up through the

00:54:33.840 --> 00:54:34.680
floor below him.

00:54:34.680 --> 00:54:38.700
She was downstairs, just beneath the floor
where he was standing.

00:54:38.700 --> 00:54:42.720
His instincts kicked in, and his body moved
almost of its own accord.

00:54:42.720 --> 00:54:48.780
He lifted his long, long arms above his head,
and drove his hands down into the floor.

00:54:48.780 --> 00:54:54.780
It was weakened by the ravages of the elements
and time, and crumbled under his unnatural

00:54:54.780 --> 00:54:55.380
strength.

00:54:55.380 --> 00:55:00.660
He dug his claws into the plaster, tearing
it apart and cracking it into pieces.

00:55:00.660 --> 00:55:05.700
He pushed his claws in, deeper and deeper,
until he could feel them breaking through

00:55:05.700 --> 00:55:06.840
to the other side.

00:55:06.840 --> 00:55:11.760
He pulled the floor apart until the hole was
wide enough to fit through, then dropped down

00:55:11.760 --> 00:55:13.140
to the room below.

00:55:13.140 --> 00:55:17.280
He spotted the woman’s back, fleeing through
the door, just as he landed.

00:55:17.280 --> 00:55:18.600
She was getting away!

00:55:18.600 --> 00:55:24.060
He tore after her, ripping through concrete
and steel, destroying everything in his path

00:55:24.060 --> 00:55:26.820
in a mad dash for the object of his rage.

00:55:26.820 --> 00:55:31.260
He had to catch her, she could not be allowed
to leave.

00:55:31.260 --> 00:55:35.580
She looked over her shoulder at him, her face
a mask of pure terror.

00:55:35.580 --> 00:55:39.420
She knew, just as he knew, that there was
no escape in sight.

00:55:39.420 --> 00:55:42.900
In his single-minded&nbsp;
obsession, and her desperation&nbsp;

00:55:42.900 --> 00:55:45.300
to escape from him, neither of them noticed

00:55:45.300 --> 00:55:49.860
that she had run so far and so carelessly
that she was now outside of the building.

00:55:49.860 --> 00:55:54.540
She had crossed from the shadows, into the
harsh light of the sun.

00:55:54.540 --> 00:55:58.320
The woman’s eyes widened in grim understanding
of her fate.

00:55:58.320 --> 00:56:04.080
He could only watch, helplessly, as her body
began to succumb to the vicious rays.

00:56:04.080 --> 00:56:06.780
Almost instantly, she began to melt.

00:56:06.780 --> 00:56:10.320
As it was the closest to the sun, her head
was the first to go.

00:56:10.320 --> 00:56:16.500
Her nose drooped like hot wax, dripping as
it went until it slumped off her face and

00:56:16.500 --> 00:56:18.900
landed on the ground with a sickening splash.

00:56:18.900 --> 00:56:25.080
Her eyes were next, popping out of their sockets
and hanging, loose and limp, on her cheeks

00:56:25.080 --> 00:56:27.780
and dangling from sinewy strands.

00:56:27.780 --> 00:56:33.000
She tried, in vain, to stuff them back into
place, but they squished in her hands like

00:56:33.000 --> 00:56:33.960
overripe fruit.

00:56:33.960 --> 00:56:38.700
She attempted to run into the comparative
safety of the ruined building, but her feet

00:56:38.700 --> 00:56:43.800
were already beginning to spread out onto
the street, melting into a sticky red smear

00:56:43.800 --> 00:56:44.580
on the pavement.

00:56:44.580 --> 00:56:50.700
Her legs crumbled beneath her, and she collapsed
to the ground, her face leaving a trail of

00:56:50.700 --> 00:56:53.040
gelatinous flesh as it landed.

00:56:53.040 --> 00:56:58.440
She tried, uselessly, to drag herself along
the ground back into the darkness, but her

00:56:58.440 --> 00:57:03.240
arms were melting into the dirt, mixing with
it into a horrible red mud.

00:57:03.240 --> 00:57:09.060
She let out a last scream of agony, but the
sound turned to a thick gurgle as her lips

00:57:09.060 --> 00:57:12.120
fused together and her mouth&nbsp;
melted into nothingness.

00:57:12.120 --> 00:57:16.080
He watched it all, sobbing and roaring in
frustration.

00:57:16.080 --> 00:57:19.800
She was gone, and he would&nbsp;
never get the satisfaction&nbsp;

00:57:19.800 --> 00:57:20.820
of ending her.

00:57:20.820 --> 00:57:24.060
The infernal sun had stolen her from him.

00:57:24.060 --> 00:57:30.660
Now she was nothing but a wet shadow, like
the rest of the humans had become long ago.

00:57:30.660 --> 00:57:36.180
He heard the sound of a soft sob behind him,
a tiny human voice.

00:57:36.180 --> 00:57:38.520
He turned, looking for the source.

00:57:38.520 --> 00:57:40.380
There, was the little girl.

00:57:40.380 --> 00:57:42.240
He had almost forgotten about her.

00:57:42.240 --> 00:57:46.680
She was calling out for her mother and crying,
and she was looking right at him.

00:57:46.680 --> 00:57:48.060
Right at his face.

00:57:48.060 --> 00:57:52.680
He waited for the anger to overtake him, but
instead…there was nothing.

00:57:52.680 --> 00:57:54.300
Why wasn’t he angry?

00:57:54.300 --> 00:57:56.640
She was looking at his face, so why?

00:57:56.640 --> 00:58:01.920
Then he realized: her eyes were on him, but
she did not see him.

00:58:01.920 --> 00:58:03.180
She was blind.

00:58:03.180 --> 00:58:06.360
She approached the creature,&nbsp;
her arms outstretched,&nbsp;

00:58:06.360 --> 00:58:08.160
following the sound of his crying.

00:58:08.160 --> 00:58:14.040
She touched his hand with her own tiny fingers,
so small and fragile, and asked, “Mommy?”

00:58:14.040 --> 00:58:17.820
Then, he felt something he had never felt
before.

00:58:17.820 --> 00:58:23.400
Tenderness, care, a desire to protect this
tiny innocent being.

00:58:23.400 --> 00:58:30.060
It was the first time he had ever felt warm,
ever felt anything but sadness, anger, or

00:58:30.060 --> 00:58:30.420
fear.

00:58:30.420 --> 00:58:35.640
Overcome with this softness, he wrapped his
arms around the child and hugged her close.

00:58:35.640 --> 00:58:41.400
She hugged him back, tightly, and the room
went quiet as they both stopped crying.

00:58:41.400 --> 00:58:44.760
From that day on, they became a family of
sorts.

00:58:44.760 --> 00:58:47.220
They lived together, and he took care of her.

00:58:47.220 --> 00:58:52.260
She didn’t know what he looked like, and
had no idea he might be something to fear.

00:58:52.260 --> 00:58:54.240
To her, he was safety.

00:58:54.240 --> 00:58:55.440
He was home.

00:58:55.440 --> 00:59:01.740
For the first time in his long, awful life,
he felt like a human instead of a monster.

00:59:01.740 --> 00:59:07.200
He would scavenge food for her, bring her
fresh water, lay down stolen blankets and

00:59:07.200 --> 00:59:09.900
pillows so she would have a soft place to
rest.

00:59:09.900 --> 00:59:15.300
He even found her a ratty, worn teddy bear
amongst the rubble and gave it to her as a

00:59:15.300 --> 00:59:15.360
gift.

00:59:15.360 --> 00:59:21.120
She clutched it to her chest and thanked him,
and for once his tears were those of joy.

00:59:21.120 --> 00:59:25.620
In this cursed world, they had found something
like happiness in one another.

00:59:25.620 --> 00:59:29.280
He knew he would protect her no matter what.

00:59:29.280 --> 00:59:32.220
Then, one day, everything changed.

00:59:32.220 --> 00:59:36.960
The creature and the little girl were playing
in the abandoned site, making up little games

00:59:36.960 --> 00:59:41.220
together, when he took a break to look out
the window and monitor their surroundings.

00:59:41.220 --> 00:59:44.340
Outside, the evil red sun waited.

00:59:44.340 --> 00:59:47.040
When he turned back, the little girl was gone.

00:59:47.040 --> 00:59:52.680
One of the shadowy creatures, once human,
was imitating his voice to lure her away.

00:59:52.680 --> 00:59:57.000
He could hear it in the distance, and her
tiny footsteps following it.

00:59:57.000 --> 01:00:03.120
He chased after her, the only thing he had
ever loved, and saw her just as she stepped

01:00:03.120 --> 01:00:04.260
out into the light.

01:00:04.260 --> 01:00:10.560
She screamed as one of her arms began to melt
in the light, and he knew what he had to do.

01:00:10.560 --> 01:00:15.240
He grabbed her and pulled her back inside,
using his body to shield her from the horrible

01:00:15.240 --> 01:00:15.780
rays.

01:00:15.780 --> 01:00:21.120
As he wept over her, his tears stopped her
body from melting any further, but his body

01:00:21.120 --> 01:00:22.740
continued to fall apart.

01:00:22.740 --> 01:00:24.780
It was too late for him.

01:00:24.780 --> 01:00:26.940
He ran, as far away as he could.

01:00:26.940 --> 01:00:30.420
Once he transformed, he did not want to end
up hurting the girl.

01:00:30.420 --> 01:00:34.860
He ran until his legs melted away and he could
not run anymore.

01:00:34.860 --> 01:00:40.740
He had reached the shoreline, and stared out
at the ocean, unable to move any more.

01:00:40.740 --> 01:00:45.240
He screamed, a final time,&nbsp;
in agony, in heartbreak,&nbsp;

01:00:45.240 --> 01:00:48.060
in mourning for the happiness he had finally

01:00:48.060 --> 01:00:50.340
found and now would lose.

01:00:50.340 --> 01:00:57.120
But at least he knew, as his eyes melted away
and he was nearly gone, that the girl would

01:00:57.120 --> 01:00:57.660
be safe.

01:00:57.660 --> 01:01:02.100
And then, he was gone, and the world was quiet.

01:01:02.100 --> 01:01:09.180
The day-to-day routine of Dr. Gears consisted
of a few constants: piping hot cups of black,

01:01:09.180 --> 01:01:14.640
unsweetened coffee, plain dry wheat toast,
the soothing sounds of his favorite white

01:01:14.640 --> 01:01:19.620
noise machine, and the endless carousel of
experiments with SCP-914.

01:01:19.620 --> 01:01:25.440
Not that he was complaining, he was perfectly
content to spend his time supervising one

01:01:25.440 --> 01:01:31.140
of the few anomalies he crossed paths with
on a regular basis that was unlikely to kill

01:01:31.140 --> 01:01:32.700
or maim him in any way.

01:01:32.700 --> 01:01:37.260
Not that the Clockworks hadn't produced its
fair share of unpredictable results over the

01:01:37.260 --> 01:01:38.700
years of extensive testing.

01:01:38.700 --> 01:01:42.540
It had definitely offered up more than a few
surprises.

01:01:42.540 --> 01:01:47.760
And anyone who knew Dr. Gears knew that he
was not especially fond of surprises.

01:01:47.760 --> 01:01:52.020
Dr. Bright had attempted to throw a surprise
birthday party for the man once, but when

01:01:52.020 --> 01:01:56.400
he turned on the lights and fired the confetti
cannon, all Dr. Gears did in response was

01:01:56.400 --> 01:01:58.800
give a deep sigh and say "Really, Jack?

01:01:58.800 --> 01:02:00.960
You're making a spectacle of yourself."

01:02:00.960 --> 01:02:06.300
Still, he had resigned himself long ago to
the fact that supervising the experiments

01:02:06.300 --> 01:02:10.440
with SCP-914 meant witnessing&nbsp;
some truly unpredictable&nbsp;

01:02:10.440 --> 01:02:11.220
outcomes.

01:02:11.220 --> 01:02:16.260
How could he forget the time Researcher Blais
tested an incandescent light bulb on the setting

01:02:16.260 --> 01:02:19.320
"Very Fine," and the machine&nbsp;
spat out an anthropomorphic&nbsp;

01:02:19.320 --> 01:02:22.080
humanoid lightbulb that spoke in German-accented

01:02:22.080 --> 01:02:25.440
English and claimed to be Thomas Edison himself.

01:02:25.440 --> 01:02:30.240
This was, of course, impossible, as historical
records surely would have indicated if Thomas

01:02:30.240 --> 01:02:33.780
Edison was a walking talking lightbulb rather
than a human man.

01:02:33.780 --> 01:02:39.060
The imposter was eventually incinerated after
its presence became too irritating to ignore.

01:02:39.060 --> 01:02:43.680
And then there was the time Researcher Thompson
filled out a Dungeons and Dragons character

01:02:43.680 --> 01:02:46.860
sheet and placed it into the machine on the
setting "Very Fine."

01:02:46.860 --> 01:02:52.380
The output produced was a sheet of paper promoting
the previously nonexistent tabletop roleplaying

01:02:52.380 --> 01:02:54.060
game Fear in the Foundation.

01:02:54.060 --> 01:02:58.560
Whenever a person read the paper, they would
suddenly find themselves in an out-of-body

01:02:58.560 --> 01:03:03.600
experience where they were inside of the game's
world, which contained several characters

01:03:03.600 --> 01:03:06.600
related to the SCP Foundation, as well as&nbsp;

01:03:06.600 --> 01:03:09.780
items and locations based&nbsp;
on real-world counterparts.

01:03:09.780 --> 01:03:14.460
A subject in this state would only snap back
to reality after winning or dying in the game.

01:03:14.460 --> 01:03:20.700
Researcher Jacobson rolled a 1 on stealth
and saw SCP-096's face in the game, and was

01:03:20.700 --> 01:03:23.820
later found dead in the anomalous item storage
wing.

01:03:23.820 --> 01:03:28.200
There was no shortage of Foundation staff
trying to use the machine for personal gain,

01:03:28.200 --> 01:03:28.620
too.

01:03:28.620 --> 01:03:33.840
Dr. Naismith placed his credit card inside
on the setting "Very Fine," using it to produce

01:03:33.840 --> 01:03:36.780
a card covered in unidentified corporate insignias&nbsp;

01:03:36.780 --> 01:03:40.080
and reading “RANK-ALEPH&nbsp;
INFINITE MONEY PRIVILEGES.”

01:03:40.080 --> 01:03:45.480
When Dr. Coltrane issued a written warning,
Dr. Naismith took that warning and then placed

01:03:45.480 --> 01:03:49.980
it into the machine on the same setting, producing
a piece of official documentation from the

01:03:49.980 --> 01:03:53.640
O5 council in support of his Infinite Money
Privileges.

01:03:53.640 --> 01:03:56.520
Junior Researcher Summers attempted to use&nbsp;

01:03:56.520 --> 01:04:00.480
SCP-914 in a misguided&nbsp;
attempt at self-improvement,

01:04:00.480 --> 01:04:06.180
placing not an object, but herself in the
intake booth before running the machine on

01:04:06.180 --> 01:04:07.320
the setting "Very Fine."

01:04:07.320 --> 01:04:11.100
It cleared her skin, lengthened her hair,
and improved her figure.

01:04:11.100 --> 01:04:15.720
This was, of course, in violation of several
employee guidelines, and she was promptly

01:04:15.720 --> 01:04:18.360
dismissed after emerging from SCP-914.

01:04:18.360 --> 01:04:24.000
Dr. Veritas left a note in the experiment
log following this incident, reading: "By

01:04:24.000 --> 01:04:28.080
the time we realized what she was actually
doing, it was too late to stop her.

01:04:28.080 --> 01:04:33.060
Needless to say, she's since been terminated,
and I hope I don't need to tell you all not

01:04:33.060 --> 01:04:34.260
to do it again."

01:04:34.260 --> 01:04:40.740
And with that, the guidance was clear: no
one was permitted to use SCP-914 for personal

01:04:40.740 --> 01:04:43.740
gain, or to change anything about themselves.

01:04:43.740 --> 01:04:48.720
Potential complications were too risky, not
to mention the conflicts of interest that

01:04:48.720 --> 01:04:52.800
would be introduced into what should be an
impartial research process.

01:04:52.800 --> 01:04:57.720
As scientific objectivity's biggest fan, Dr.
Gears couldn't agree more.

01:04:57.720 --> 01:05:02.760
So, as he settled in for the day's round of
tests, he intended to keep a watchful eye

01:05:02.760 --> 01:05:06.540
on things and ensure that no funny business
would take place.

01:05:06.540 --> 01:05:12.240
He didn't have much reason for concern, as
his colleague Dr. Bonita prepped her research

01:05:12.240 --> 01:05:12.900
materials.

01:05:12.900 --> 01:05:18.420
She was working with two items: a small replica
of Michelangelo's sculpture of David, and

01:05:18.420 --> 01:05:23.100
a sealed envelope containing something that
was to be handled with extreme caution, a

01:05:23.100 --> 01:05:26.520
photograph of SCP-096's face.

01:05:26.520 --> 01:05:31.560
She planned to place the items inside on the
"Very Fine" setting, in an attempt to see

01:05:31.560 --> 01:05:36.900
what result might be produced from combining
an ideal of traditional beauty standards with

01:05:36.900 --> 01:05:42.180
the image of a creature that felt such profound
shame and distress at its own appearance that

01:05:42.180 --> 01:05:44.580
it would destroy anyone who looked at its
face.

01:05:44.580 --> 01:05:50.340
Like any good scientist, Dr. Bonita wanted
to remove any unnecessary variables from her

01:05:50.340 --> 01:05:51.000
experiment.

01:05:51.000 --> 01:05:57.540
So, as she placed her items inside the intake
booth, she slowly, delicately, unsealed the

01:05:57.540 --> 01:05:58.080
envelope.

01:05:58.080 --> 01:06:04.080
She wanted to put the picture inside by itself,
without the extra element of the envelope

01:06:04.080 --> 01:06:06.000
potentially complicating things.

01:06:06.000 --> 01:06:09.960
Unfortunately, like Marie&nbsp;
Curie slowly, unintentionally&nbsp;

01:06:09.960 --> 01:06:12.600
poisoning herself with her own research materials,

01:06:12.600 --> 01:06:16.860
she didn't truly understand the danger of
what she held in her hands.

01:06:16.860 --> 01:06:21.180
Just as she was setting the photograph down,
her eyes flickered to the image.

01:06:21.180 --> 01:06:26.100
Before she could even stop herself, before
she could look away or squeeze her eyes shut,

01:06:26.100 --> 01:06:32.800
she caught a glimpse of the one thing she
should never look at: SCP-096's face.

01:06:33.540 --> 01:06:37.796
She gasped and slammed the photograph down,
but she knew it was too late.

01:06:37.796 --> 01:06:42.300
The sound of an inhuman shriek coming from
across the facility signaled that she was

01:06:42.300 --> 01:06:42.540
right.

01:06:42.540 --> 01:06:47.790
It was coming for her, and nothing in the
world could stop it.

01:06:47.790 --> 01:06:51.840
In a containment cell on the other side of
the facility, Foundation staff were horrified

01:06:51.840 --> 01:06:56.100
as they heard the telltale scream of an enraged
SCP-096.

01:06:56.100 --> 01:07:01.500
The pale, thin creature, once huddled in the
corner silently, had stretched to its full

01:07:01.500 --> 01:07:07.860
height of 2.38 meters, and was screaming,
sobbing, wailing in gibberish, and beginning

01:07:07.860 --> 01:07:10.080
to tear its way out of its chamber.

01:07:10.080 --> 01:07:14.760
Guards tried their best to subdue the entity,
firing their weapons at it, but the bullets

01:07:14.760 --> 01:07:18.900
did nothing to damage the creature's pale
flesh or stop its movements.

01:07:18.900 --> 01:07:23.580
It ripped through the steel cube that contained
it, and knocked the guards out of its way

01:07:23.580 --> 01:07:28.920
with one swipe of its unnaturally long arms,
sending them careening into a nearby wall.

01:07:28.920 --> 01:07:33.300
Fortunately for them, SCP-096 only knocked
them unconscious.

01:07:33.300 --> 01:07:39.240
It didn't stop to harm them further, as it
had a more important goal in mind: Find the

01:07:39.240 --> 01:07:42.420
person who had seen its face, and destroy
them.

01:07:42.420 --> 01:07:45.240
As the alarm blared,&nbsp;
signifying a high-threat-level&nbsp;

01:07:45.240 --> 01:07:49.080
containment breach, SCP-096 loped down the

01:07:49.080 --> 01:07:51.880
hall toward Dr. Bonita in SCP-914's room.

01:07:53.040 --> 01:07:58.140
Dr. Gears had not spotted Dr. Bonita's grave
mistake, and had no idea what had triggered

01:07:58.140 --> 01:07:59.460
the alarm he was hearing.

01:07:59.460 --> 01:08:03.900
He stepped away from the observation window,
turning his attention to the crisis that was

01:08:03.900 --> 01:08:06.000
clearly happening somewhere else in the facility.

01:08:06.000 --> 01:08:08.520
Meanwhile, Dr. Bonita was panicking.

01:08:08.520 --> 01:08:13.500
She saw her life flash before her eyes, the
certainty of impending doom that was coming

01:08:13.500 --> 01:08:18.720
for her, and coming fast, all because of one
brief error in judgment.

01:08:18.720 --> 01:08:20.160
What could she do?

01:08:20.160 --> 01:08:24.900
There was nowhere to hide, no way she could
run away fast enough, unless...

01:08:24.900 --> 01:08:30.960
If she managed to lure 096 into the intake
booth, and start the machine while the creature

01:08:30.960 --> 01:08:36.180
was inside, maybe it would transform into
something less intent on tearing her limb

01:08:36.180 --> 01:08:36.840
from limb?

01:08:36.840 --> 01:08:42.300
It was a risky move, and one that could jeopardize
her position at the Foundation, but she couldn't

01:08:42.300 --> 01:08:47.280
very well keep her job if she was dead, so
it seemed like it just might be worth a shot.

01:08:47.280 --> 01:08:54.360
A primal roar of agony and fury interrupted
her thoughts, and she knew that SCP-096 was

01:08:54.360 --> 01:08:58.380
moments away from breaking down the door and
getting its hands on her.

01:08:58.380 --> 01:09:00.420
She would have to move fast.

01:09:00.420 --> 01:09:06.540
With the screech and grind of metal on metal,
SCP-096 wrenched the door off its hinges and

01:09:06.540 --> 01:09:07.560
barreled into the room.

01:09:07.560 --> 01:09:12.480
In its search for the person who had seen
its face, it ran toward the silhouette of

01:09:12.480 --> 01:09:15.600
Dr. Bonita, standing just at the entrance
to the intake booth.

01:09:15.600 --> 01:09:19.320
She tucked and rolled out of the way, just
as the monster entered the booth.

01:09:19.320 --> 01:09:24.840
The door automatically slid shut behind it,
and, as SCP-096 rattled the door and tried

01:09:24.840 --> 01:09:29.820
to free itself, she turned the knob to "Very
Fine" with every ounce of strength and speed

01:09:29.820 --> 01:09:30.420
she had.

01:09:30.420 --> 01:09:35.880
There was the ding of a small bell, and the
machine whirred to life as the objects inside

01:09:35.880 --> 01:09:37.080
were refined.

01:09:37.080 --> 01:09:41.940
Dr. Bonita has no idea what would be waiting
for her in the Output Booth, but she could

01:09:41.940 --> 01:09:45.360
only hope that her last-ditch effort had managed
to save her life.

01:09:45.360 --> 01:09:49.800
In the fog of panic, she briefly felt an itch
of scientific curiosity, too.

01:09:49.800 --> 01:09:57.180
What would become of a being like SCP-096
in a machine as strange and wonderful as SCP-914?

01:09:57.180 --> 01:10:00.360
What would the addition of the statue do to
it?

01:10:00.360 --> 01:10:05.220
As the door to the Outtake Booth slid open,
steam pouring out, it appeared her questions

01:10:05.220 --> 01:10:06.780
would soon be answered.

01:10:06.780 --> 01:10:11.400
Cautiously, in spite of herself, Dr. Bonita
called out: "Hello?"

01:10:11.400 --> 01:10:17.580
No one answered, but she heard the sound of
footsteps, slow and careful, as a figure emerged

01:10:17.580 --> 01:10:18.240
from the mist.

01:10:18.240 --> 01:10:21.180
She covered her mouth in shock, her eyes wide.

01:10:21.180 --> 01:10:22.440
"Dear God..."

01:10:22.440 --> 01:10:24.060
She whispered in awe.

01:10:24.060 --> 01:10:29.460
Standing in front of her, with pale, smooth
skin and the same imposing stature, was the

01:10:29.460 --> 01:10:32.040
most beautiful man she had ever seen.

01:10:32.040 --> 01:10:35.940
Wide dark eyes shone under thick, sculpted
eyebrows.

01:10:35.940 --> 01:10:41.700
Under the eyes, an aquiline nose, full pouty
lips, a strong, sharp jawline.

01:10:41.700 --> 01:10:45.480
His head was topped with a tangle of lustrous
dark curls.

01:10:45.480 --> 01:10:49.740
It was the kind of hair she had only seen
flowing in the wind on the covers of the romance

01:10:49.740 --> 01:10:54.660
novels she wanted desperately to buy, but
was too embarrassed to be seen purchasing.

01:10:54.660 --> 01:11:01.200
His physique was, well, statuesque, like the
build of the very Michelangelo sculpture she

01:11:01.200 --> 01:11:03.720
had placed into the machine just moments ago.

01:11:03.720 --> 01:11:10.380
There was no other way to say it: he was handsome
- Despite still being a little lanky, and

01:11:10.380 --> 01:11:11.280
nine feet tall.

01:11:11.280 --> 01:11:16.860
He peered at her curiously, towering over
her in a way that had been terrifying in his

01:11:16.860 --> 01:11:21.420
former shape, but now made her heart skip
a beat in an entirely different way.

01:11:21.420 --> 01:11:22.500
"Hi..."

01:11:22.500 --> 01:11:23.940
Was all she could think to say.

01:11:23.940 --> 01:11:25.200
Was she blushing?

01:11:25.200 --> 01:11:27.720
She shook her head, snapping herself out of
it.

01:11:27.720 --> 01:11:29.400
She was a scientist, dammit!

01:11:29.400 --> 01:11:32.280
Not some giddy school girl passing notes in
class.

01:11:32.280 --> 01:11:37.140
This was an incredible achievement, something
she would need to study thoroughly.

01:11:37.140 --> 01:11:40.740
And she very much wanted to&nbsp;
study him thoroughly...No!

01:11:41.820 --> 01:11:42.840
No time for that.

01:11:42.840 --> 01:11:48.120
She needed to write up a report, to inform
her superiors, to try her best not to lose

01:11:48.120 --> 01:11:49.020
her job over this.

01:11:49.020 --> 01:11:51.060
She had to remain professional.

01:11:51.060 --> 01:11:57.000
"Hi," the man that had once been, or perhaps
still was, SCP-096 spoke.

01:11:57.000 --> 01:11:57.900
"Oh!

01:11:57.900 --> 01:11:58.560
You can talk!"

01:11:58.560 --> 01:12:00.720
Dr. Bonita laughed in surprise.

01:12:00.720 --> 01:12:02.280
The man's brow furrowed.

01:12:02.280 --> 01:12:05.700
His newfound ability to speak was a surprise
to him too, it seemed.

01:12:05.700 --> 01:12:06.240
"Yes.

01:12:06.240 --> 01:12:07.740
I can.

01:12:07.740 --> 01:12:13.140
What...happened to me?" he asked, stumbling
over his words slightly, getting used to the

01:12:13.140 --> 01:12:13.680
feeling of them.

01:12:13.680 --> 01:12:15.540
"You ran into the machine..."

01:12:15.540 --> 01:12:18.120
She gestured to SCP-914.

01:12:18.120 --> 01:12:20.040
"I'm not exactly sure, to be honest.

01:12:20.040 --> 01:12:22.620
But you're...very different not."

01:12:22.620 --> 01:12:23.520
He nodded.

01:12:23.520 --> 01:12:24.660
"I feel different.

01:12:24.660 --> 01:12:26.880
I feel...calm."

01:12:26.880 --> 01:12:31.680
He sighed, the relief plain on his face, before
a shadow of sadness fell over him.

01:12:31.680 --> 01:12:33.840
"I don't think I have to hurt anymore.

01:12:33.840 --> 01:12:35.880
I'm...I'm sorry.

01:12:35.880 --> 01:12:37.320
For what I did before."

01:12:37.320 --> 01:12:40.080
Dr. Bonita didn't know what to say.

01:12:40.080 --> 01:12:45.120
How do you respond when something you've been
studying from afar, been horrified and fascinated

01:12:45.120 --> 01:12:50.700
by in equal measure, looks at you with a new,
beautiful face and apologizes for all of the

01:12:50.700 --> 01:12:51.720
harm it caused?

01:12:51.720 --> 01:12:56.580
This whole experience was so surreal that
she might think she was dreaming if she didn't

01:12:56.580 --> 01:13:01.800
work at a place that was one long waking dream,
or nightmare depending on the day.

01:13:01.800 --> 01:13:03.120
"Dr. Bonita?

01:13:03.120 --> 01:13:04.920
There's been a containment breach, are you
alright?"

01:13:04.920 --> 01:13:10.200
Dr. Gears had returned to the room, taking
in the sight of the destruction left in 096's

01:13:10.200 --> 01:13:10.440
wake.

01:13:10.440 --> 01:13:11.580
"I'm fine..."

01:13:11.580 --> 01:13:16.740
She called to him, and he followed her voice
into the room, then stopped at the sight of

01:13:16.740 --> 01:13:18.000
the transformed anomaly.

01:13:18.000 --> 01:13:18.600
"Hm.

01:13:18.600 --> 01:13:20.580
I don't have time for whatever this is.

01:13:20.580 --> 01:13:21.960
I trust you'll handle it?"

01:13:21.960 --> 01:13:24.960
Dr. Gears took a long sip of his coffee, and,&nbsp;

01:13:24.960 --> 01:13:27.180
taking Dr. Bonita's shocked&nbsp;
silence as a confirmation,

01:13:27.180 --> 01:13:29.520
had a leisurely stroll back to his office.

01:13:29.520 --> 01:13:34.860
A few moments later, the guards responsible
for containing SCP-096 arrived on the scene,

01:13:34.860 --> 01:13:40.980
expecting to see carnage and find a docile
SCP-096 crouched over a lifeless body, but

01:13:40.980 --> 01:13:45.840
instead they found the same truly bizarre
sight that Dr. Gears had shrugged off, and

01:13:45.840 --> 01:13:48.600
Dr. Bonita was still doing her best to process.

01:13:48.600 --> 01:13:53.100
They entered the room with their weapons drawn,
but quickly lowered them, scratching their

01:13:53.100 --> 01:13:56.400
heads in confusion instead, before radioing&nbsp;

01:13:56.400 --> 01:13:59.040
their supervisors and asking&nbsp;
for further instructions.

01:13:59.040 --> 01:14:04.380
Responses from various Foundation staff who
caught a glimpse of SCP-096's bold new look

01:14:04.380 --> 01:14:06.060
included "Huh, would you look at that?"

01:14:06.060 --> 01:14:07.140
"Who's that guy?

01:14:07.140 --> 01:14:08.400
He's WHAT?!"

01:14:08.400 --> 01:14:12.960
and, in the words of Dr. Jack Bright, "Oh
NO, he's HOT!"

01:14:13.920 --> 01:14:20.580
Dr. Bright also proposed making the new SCP-096
a tiktok account and YouTube channel, seeking

01:14:20.580 --> 01:14:25.020
modeling representation for him, or selling
a novelty calendar filled with pictures of

01:14:25.020 --> 01:14:27.480
096 in various costumes.

01:14:27.480 --> 01:14:32.520
These would be, in his words, "Excellent ways
to increase revenue for the Foundation.

01:14:32.520 --> 01:14:36.360
So really you're the weird ones for thinking
my ideas are weird."

01:14:36.360 --> 01:14:42.120
Dr. Bright was asked to leave SCP-096 alone,
and stop trying to take his headshot.

01:14:42.120 --> 01:14:48.180
In the days that followed the incident with
SCP-914, the SCP Foundation was at a loss

01:14:48.180 --> 01:14:52.920
about what to do with this new, seemingly
harmless version of SCP-096.

01:14:52.920 --> 01:14:57.600
Dozens of D-Class were brought in to look
at his face, and see if the entity would still

01:14:57.600 --> 01:15:02.280
enter one of his rage states after a few days
of getting used to his new form, but he never

01:15:02.280 --> 01:15:02.580
did.

01:15:02.580 --> 01:15:07.380
No screaming, no swallowing people whole,
nothing more than a polite, if somewhat shy,

01:15:07.380 --> 01:15:09.840
greeting, and a courteous "How are you doing
today?"

01:15:09.840 --> 01:15:14.760
The D-Classes were relieved, but confused
about being pulled from their cells just to

01:15:14.760 --> 01:15:16.500
stare at some random handsome man.

01:15:16.500 --> 01:15:22.200
Dr. Clef suggested dissecting SCP-096, to
see what his new body looked like on the inside.

01:15:22.200 --> 01:15:24.180
This request was denied.

01:15:24.180 --> 01:15:29.700
Several interviews were conducted to evaluate
SCP-096's mental and emotional state.

01:15:29.700 --> 01:15:34.800
Now that the anomaly was capable of coherent
speech, it was much simpler to evaluate the

01:15:34.800 --> 01:15:36.540
potential threat level he might pose.

01:15:36.540 --> 01:15:41.640
Every researcher who spoke with him came to
the same conclusion: gone was the danger of

01:15:41.640 --> 01:15:43.500
the old SCP-096.

01:15:43.500 --> 01:15:49.020
He had not just become beautiful in a classical,
superficial sense, but he had become beautiful

01:15:49.020 --> 01:15:51.120
on the inside as well.

01:15:51.120 --> 01:15:56.700
Interviewers reported a warm, friendly demeanor,
a talent for engaging in conversation once

01:15:56.700 --> 01:16:01.980
he was made to feel comfortable, and a sincere
interest in the thoughts, opinions, and feelings

01:16:01.980 --> 01:16:03.420
of those he spoke with.

01:16:03.420 --> 01:16:09.060
There was only one thing left to do, to make
sure that SCP-096 had really changed from

01:16:09.060 --> 01:16:12.780
something deadly to something almost resembling
an ordinary person.

01:16:12.780 --> 01:16:20.760
A photograph of SCP-096's face, of its original
face, was removed from a secure vault by a

01:16:20.760 --> 01:16:21.300
D-Class.

01:16:21.300 --> 01:16:27.720
Then, the D-Class was sent into a room with
SCP-096, and instructed to place the photograph

01:16:27.720 --> 01:16:28.440
on the table.

01:16:28.440 --> 01:16:34.920
SCP-096 looked down at what had once been
his face, and his eyes filled with tears.

01:16:34.920 --> 01:16:40.500
A soft, broken sob left his lips, and he wrapped
his arms around himself, hunching over as

01:16:40.500 --> 01:16:41.580
if in physical pain.

01:16:41.580 --> 01:16:45.720
Outside the room, guards prepared to handle
things if 096 began to attack.

01:16:45.720 --> 01:16:51.000
Instead, he wiped his tears, took a deep,
shuddering breath, and looked at the D-Class

01:16:51.000 --> 01:16:52.560
with a somber expression.

01:16:52.560 --> 01:16:56.820
He picked up the photograph of the table,
and tore it in half, as he finally summoned

01:16:56.820 --> 01:16:57.720
the strength to speak.

01:16:57.720 --> 01:16:59.940
"Please...get rid of these.

01:16:59.940 --> 01:17:02.220
That's not who I am anymore."

01:17:02.220 --> 01:17:07.320
At Dr. Bonita's strong insistence, backed
up by the conclusions of the research staff

01:17:07.320 --> 01:17:13.140
who interviewed SCP-096, a reevaluation of
the entity's containment measures was ordered.

01:17:13.140 --> 01:17:18.060
It seemed cruel, and an unnecessary waste
of resources, to keep 096 trapped in a steel

01:17:18.060 --> 01:17:19.380
cube in its current form.

01:17:19.380 --> 01:17:22.500
He would be moved to a&nbsp;
standard humanoid containment&nbsp;

01:17:22.500 --> 01:17:24.660
cell, and treated as well as other safe-class

01:17:24.660 --> 01:17:31.260
anomalies provided with books, films, food
and drink upon request, and other comforts.

01:17:31.260 --> 01:17:36.180
Of course, the O5 council insisted on evaluating
the entity before any of these changes could

01:17:36.180 --> 01:17:36.840
be approved.

01:17:36.840 --> 01:17:42.960
Dressed in a specially tailored suit, provided
by Dr. Bonita, SCP-096 appeared before the

01:17:42.960 --> 01:17:44.520
council to present his case.

01:17:44.520 --> 01:17:48.060
"I know that I may not have the best record
at the Foundation.

01:17:48.060 --> 01:17:51.060
I've done a lot of damage over the years.

01:17:51.060 --> 01:17:54.780
Though, let's be honest, you all aren't exactly
innocent either.

01:17:54.780 --> 01:17:56.880
Sorry, that was an attempt at a joke.

01:17:56.880 --> 01:17:59.400
I'm still very new to talking.

01:17:59.400 --> 01:18:05.100
All I can say is, please consider giving me
another chance to make a real life here, to

01:18:05.100 --> 01:18:06.840
make this place my home.

01:18:06.840 --> 01:18:08.220
Thank you for your time."

01:18:08.220 --> 01:18:13.380
What SCP-096 didn't know is that the O5 Council
was so flabbergasted by the sight of his new

01:18:13.380 --> 01:18:16.200
face that they didn't retain a single word
he said.

01:18:16.200 --> 01:18:20.820
They had all given their official approval
before he even finished his short presentation.

01:18:20.820 --> 01:18:26.880
Before long, SCP-096 was moved out of his
steel cube and into a new containment chamber

01:18:26.880 --> 01:18:32.400
that resembled a mid-range studio apartment
complete with a bed, a kitchenette, a television,

01:18:32.400 --> 01:18:34.020
and a table and chairs.

01:18:34.020 --> 01:18:39.000
He was provided access to all major streaming
platforms, as well as a large stack of books

01:18:39.000 --> 01:18:44.100
to help him develop his grasp of culture and
language after so very long being isolated

01:18:44.100 --> 01:18:45.360
from human society.

01:18:45.360 --> 01:18:49.920
Though he wasn't exactly human, he was determined
to act like it.

01:18:49.920 --> 01:18:54.720
Word quickly spread around the Foundation
site, and humans and anomalies alike flocked

01:18:54.720 --> 01:19:00.420
to SCP-096's new home to visit him, and see
the miraculous transformation for themselves.

01:19:00.420 --> 01:19:07.200
SCP-999 was the first to come and see the
new and improved 096, chirping excitedly as

01:19:07.200 --> 01:19:08.400
it oozed into his room.

01:19:08.400 --> 01:19:13.800
He pet the slime gently, his face breaking
into a warm smile as its euphoric effect washed

01:19:13.800 --> 01:19:14.640
over him.

01:19:14.640 --> 01:19:19.500
The slime became so excited at meeting this
new friend, someone it had known as a source

01:19:19.500 --> 01:19:24.300
of sadness and hurt for so long, that it tackled
him to the ground and tickled him for several

01:19:24.300 --> 01:19:27.360
minutes, 096 laughing uproariously all the
while.

01:19:27.360 --> 01:19:33.840
SCP-343 stopped by to give 096 his blessing
and wish him well in this new chapter of his

01:19:33.840 --> 01:19:34.080
life.

01:19:34.080 --> 01:19:39.960
A few days later, SCP-507 popped back into
the Site and wanted to see the changes for

01:19:39.960 --> 01:19:40.320
himself.

01:19:40.320 --> 01:19:45.900
He was thoroughly impressed, though privately
confessed to missing SCP-096's more monstrous

01:19:45.900 --> 01:19:49.320
form, which reminded him of some of his favorite
cryptids.

01:19:49.320 --> 01:19:54.900
There was one anomaly that was not thrilled
with the appearance of SCP-096, however.

01:19:54.900 --> 01:20:01.500
SCP-056 was furious upon hearing about the
new beautiful man that everyone just couldn't

01:20:01.500 --> 01:20:02.220
shut up about.

01:20:02.220 --> 01:20:08.040
It demanded a chance to speak to SCP-096,
and to tell him that "This place isn't big

01:20:08.040 --> 01:20:09.000
enough for the both of us.

01:20:09.000 --> 01:20:12.000
I'm the fairest of them all, you sniveling
little worm!"

01:20:12.000 --> 01:20:13.980
But the request was denied.

01:20:13.980 --> 01:20:17.400
SCP-056 sulked about it for several weeks.

01:20:17.400 --> 01:20:22.800
Meanwhile, Dr. Bonita was still intent on
getting to know SCP-096 better.

01:20:22.800 --> 01:20:29.520
During previous testing with SCP-978, the
Desire Camera, a photo taken of SCP-096 revealed

01:20:29.520 --> 01:20:31.800
that his greatest desire was to disappear.

01:20:31.800 --> 01:20:36.840
Curious about what the results would be now,
Dr. Bonita received permission to take another

01:20:36.840 --> 01:20:39.000
picture of SCP-096.

01:20:39.000 --> 01:20:44.340
She snapped the photo while 096 was sitting
in a chair in his new containment chamber,

01:20:44.340 --> 01:20:45.960
looking directly at the camera.

01:20:45.960 --> 01:20:50.880
When the photo developed, the result was simple:
Everything in the picture was exactly the

01:20:50.880 --> 01:20:51.960
same, with one exception.

01:20:51.960 --> 01:20:57.180
Dr. Bonita was pictured sitting next to SCP-096,
her hand clasped in his.

01:20:57.180 --> 01:21:00.060
Both were smiling soft, contented smiles.

01:21:00.060 --> 01:21:03.360
When she showed him the photograph, he smiled
at her and shook his head.

01:21:03.360 --> 01:21:05.280
"It really is an amazing camera."

01:21:05.280 --> 01:21:06.540
She flushed.

01:21:06.540 --> 01:21:09.300
"Doctor...before you go, could I ask your
name?"

01:21:09.300 --> 01:21:11.400
Dr. Bonita smiled, and nodded.

01:21:11.400 --> 01:21:12.240
"It's Isabelle.

01:21:12.240 --> 01:21:13.260
What should I call you?"

01:21:13.260 --> 01:21:16.560
SCP-096 paused thoughtfully for a moment.

01:21:16.560 --> 01:21:21.360
He was giving himself a name for the very
first time, allowing himself an identity other

01:21:21.360 --> 01:21:26.460
than a strange, hollow, pale thing that existed
to cry, and suffer, and hurt.

01:21:26.460 --> 01:21:29.580
Finally, he answered her: "Call me David."

01:21:29.580 --> 01:21:32.760
Dr. Dan anxiously paced his cell.

01:21:32.760 --> 01:21:38.400
It was the only way he’d been able to occupy
himself for the past six months, save for

01:21:38.400 --> 01:21:43.560
poring through files and, when he was lucky,
being able to oversee another failed termination

01:21:43.560 --> 01:21:44.040
attempt.

01:21:44.040 --> 01:21:49.020
He only stopped pacing when, for a brief and
horrible moment, he realized that monster

01:21:49.020 --> 01:21:55.260
was probably doing the exact same thing in
its big, metal cube at this very moment.

01:21:55.260 --> 01:22:01.200
That horrible creature that he’d thrown
away his life for a futile shot at killing.

01:22:01.200 --> 01:22:04.380
Perhaps the whole thing had been a fool’s
errand.

01:22:04.380 --> 01:22:08.580
After all, he was formerly a researcher under
the employ of the SCP Foundation.

01:22:08.580 --> 01:22:11.460
Secure, Contain, Protect.

01:22:11.460 --> 01:22:19.800
They had truly sadistic beasts like SCP-106,
the nightmarish Old Man, and SCP-352, Baba

01:22:19.800 --> 01:22:24.540
Yaga, in containment cells, and there were
no plans to terminate either of them.

01:22:24.540 --> 01:22:28.320
They weren’t the Global Occult Coalition,
for 343’s sake.

01:22:28.320 --> 01:22:33.360
Why did Dr. Dan even want to terminate SCP-096
in the first place?

01:22:33.360 --> 01:22:39.120
He’d tried to explain it to them again and
again, but his pleas had fallen on deaf ears.

01:22:39.120 --> 01:22:43.800
In his mind, the impetus for destroying the
Shy Guy was terrifyingly clear.

01:22:43.800 --> 01:22:50.160
It was a monster that killed anyone or anything
that looked at its face, even a photograph

01:22:50.160 --> 01:22:52.980
of it, even a tiny collection of pixels.

01:22:52.980 --> 01:22:57.660
When its rage state was induced, it seemed
that nothing would stop it.

01:22:57.660 --> 01:23:03.120
It would have an intuitive psychic link to
its victim, charge at them at breakneck speed,

01:23:03.120 --> 01:23:07.200
and only find its zen again once all of them
were dead.

01:23:07.200 --> 01:23:13.140
And if a picture of SCP-096 was leaked onto
the Internet, it would cause a chain reaction

01:23:13.140 --> 01:23:16.620
that triggered an XK-Class End of the World
Scenario.

01:23:16.620 --> 01:23:20.700
There were plenty of creatures and entities
that the Foundation knew about that could

01:23:20.700 --> 01:23:26.280
theoretically bring about the apocalypse,
but to him, 096 was the most realistic.

01:23:26.280 --> 01:23:31.320
It preyed upon the inherent curiosity and
thirst for knowledge seeded in every human

01:23:31.320 --> 01:23:31.740
heart.

01:23:31.740 --> 01:23:37.560
The same drive to simply Know that led to
the creation of the SCP Foundation would be

01:23:37.560 --> 01:23:44.100
humanity’s doom in the face of an SCP-096
outbreak, and if such a thing happened, the

01:23:44.100 --> 01:23:49.320
Foundation would need to break the Masquerade
of secrecy to have any hope of saving humanity.

01:23:49.320 --> 01:23:54.300
In his many feverish nightmares, Dr. Dan had
seen it all so clearly.

01:23:54.300 --> 01:23:58.620
The first photographs of SCP-096’s face
would be leaked online.

01:23:58.620 --> 01:24:04.200
Perhaps a few hundred people across the globe
would look, in disparate enough locations

01:24:04.200 --> 01:24:08.820
that it would be almost impossible for the
Foundation to detect and save all of them

01:24:08.820 --> 01:24:12.780
as SCP-096 went on its bloody rampage.

01:24:12.780 --> 01:24:20.040
Of course, as the image proliferated, as images
on the internet do, the purview of 096’s

01:24:20.040 --> 01:24:21.360
violence would only grow.

01:24:21.360 --> 01:24:24.780
People across the nation would start to take
notice.

01:24:24.780 --> 01:24:27.960
And soon after that, across the world.

01:24:27.960 --> 01:24:33.120
“Mysterious creature causing mass casualties”
would be the headline on every news desk,

01:24:33.120 --> 01:24:36.420
because why would anybody want to talk about
anything else?

01:24:36.420 --> 01:24:40.440
Perhaps some foolish reporters would take
video footage of the creature.

01:24:40.440 --> 01:24:43.080
Everyone has a phone these&nbsp;
days with high-definition&nbsp;

01:24:43.080 --> 01:24:46.440
cameras, pictures would be taken and posted.

01:24:46.440 --> 01:24:49.020
More and more and more of it would flood the
internet.

01:24:49.020 --> 01:24:53.400
Too many simultaneous moles for even the SCP
Foundation to whack.

01:24:53.400 --> 01:24:55.080
The news would spread.

01:24:55.080 --> 01:24:57.660
Panic and hysteria would spread.

01:24:57.660 --> 01:25:01.920
People wouldn’t even know what’s triggering
all this horror if the Foundation didn’t

01:25:01.920 --> 01:25:05.760
go public and tell them that it was the pictures
that was killing everyone.

01:25:05.760 --> 01:25:09.480
And by then, wouldn’t it already be too
late?

01:25:09.480 --> 01:25:12.060
The antidote to fear is knowledge.

01:25:12.060 --> 01:25:17.880
It’s why, when anything goes wrong, we’re
refreshing timelines and feeds and news websites

01:25:17.880 --> 01:25:19.140
for any kind of information.

01:25:19.140 --> 01:25:24.360
And if you were told an unstoppable monster
was killing scores of people and might be

01:25:24.360 --> 01:25:29.280
coming to a neighborhood near you sometime
soon, wouldn’t you want a good look at it?

01:25:29.280 --> 01:25:35.940
Wouldn’t you want to know your enemy, having
no idea that the mere act of knowing was what

01:25:35.940 --> 01:25:37.740
sealed your horrible fate?

01:25:37.740 --> 01:25:42.660
By Dr. Dan’s bleak estimations, if only
several million people died as a result of

01:25:42.660 --> 01:25:46.860
this kind of scenario, it could be considered
a positive outcome.

01:25:46.860 --> 01:25:48.360
He’d seen it all.

01:25:48.360 --> 01:25:51.480
But no matter how many&nbsp;
times he tried to articulate&nbsp;

01:25:51.480 --> 01:25:54.180
this to his superiors, he wasn’t listened

01:25:54.180 --> 01:25:54.240
to.

01:25:54.240 --> 01:25:59.400
They didn’t take him or the threat that
SCP-096 posed seriously.

01:25:59.400 --> 01:26:06.240
Every time he warned of the apocalyptic potential
of SCP-096, he was simply told to get back

01:26:06.240 --> 01:26:07.920
to working on those goggles.

01:26:07.920 --> 01:26:11.580
It was why he needed to give those naysayers
a demonstration.

01:26:11.580 --> 01:26:18.300
It was why a certain photograph needed to
show up in the home of a certain mountaineer.

01:26:18.300 --> 01:26:21.120
It’s why all those people had to die.

01:26:21.120 --> 01:26:23.100
There was no other way.

01:26:23.100 --> 01:26:28.620
The deaths of the civilians and the researchers
and the Mobile Task Force members were, of

01:26:28.620 --> 01:26:33.480
course, regrettable, but it was the only way
to prevent the deaths of so many more.

01:26:33.480 --> 01:26:40.080
They gave him what he wanted: Permission to
put this gangly time bomb in the ground, even

01:26:40.080 --> 01:26:45.660
if the beast’s death was inextricably tied
to his own execution at the hands of the very

01:26:45.660 --> 01:26:47.460
employers who authorized him.

01:26:47.460 --> 01:26:50.700
The world was full of funny little ironies
like that.

01:26:50.700 --> 01:26:56.340
Another one is that, even though Dr. Dan now
had the authorization and funding to terminate

01:26:56.340 --> 01:27:03.060
SCP-096, he was discovering that he lacked
another important factor: The capability.

01:27:03.060 --> 01:27:05.100
They’d tried incinerating it.

01:27:05.100 --> 01:27:07.440
Exposing it to massive amounts of radiation.

01:27:07.440 --> 01:27:12.480
Exposing it to an insane degree of kinetic
trauma - the equivalent of being hit by an

01:27:12.480 --> 01:27:13.680
out-of-control bullet train.

01:27:13.680 --> 01:27:19.440
They’d tested every kind of experimental,
off-the-books weaponry they had access to

01:27:19.440 --> 01:27:21.480
through various research projects.

01:27:21.480 --> 01:27:22.860
Proton blasts.

01:27:22.860 --> 01:27:23.580
Lasers.

01:27:23.580 --> 01:27:25.620
High-intensity energy beams.

01:27:25.620 --> 01:27:26.400
Nothing.

01:27:26.400 --> 01:27:33.000
Even when they fried off every scrap of flesh
on the monster’s body, its unbreakable skeleton

01:27:33.000 --> 01:27:37.920
still remained, and in what seemed like no
time at all, it’d be back in action.

01:27:37.920 --> 01:27:41.220
Its very existence seemed to mock him.

01:27:41.220 --> 01:27:45.900
A guard opened his cell door, staring disdainfully
at the disgraced researcher.

01:27:45.900 --> 01:27:49.980
That same guard had slammed his head against
a wall a month earlier.

01:27:49.980 --> 01:27:55.740
One of his friends had apparently been killed
in the legendary 096 containment breach that

01:27:55.740 --> 01:27:57.840
set this whole sordid thing in motion.

01:27:57.840 --> 01:28:03.540
“Up and at ‘em, scumbag,” he said in
a gruff, surly voice, training a handgun on

01:28:03.540 --> 01:28:05.580
the Doctor he knew wouldn’t fight back.

01:28:05.580 --> 01:28:07.980
“They need you in test chamber six.”

01:28:07.980 --> 01:28:10.860
Dr. Dan allowed himself a small grin.

01:28:10.860 --> 01:28:14.640
Imperceptible to the guard who clearly hated
his guts.

01:28:14.640 --> 01:28:19.020
They’d approved his 096 Vs 682 cross test.

01:28:19.020 --> 01:28:20.220
Marvelous.

01:28:21.900 --> 01:28:27.360
To all the others, it would seem like just
one more entry in a long line of 682 cross

01:28:27.360 --> 01:28:27.900
tests.

01:28:27.900 --> 01:28:32.700
He thought it darkly funny - In his eyes,
the Foundation had no hope of ever killing

01:28:32.700 --> 01:28:34.320
SCP-682.

01:28:34.320 --> 01:28:39.960
Its defining quality was being impossible
to kill, and it didn’t present nearly the

01:28:39.960 --> 01:28:43.140
threat to the entire world that 096 posed.

01:28:43.140 --> 01:28:48.840
Their futile attempts to murder the lizard
were little more than a money sink to justify

01:28:48.840 --> 01:28:50.760
the site’s exorbitant funding.

01:28:50.760 --> 01:28:53.880
But perhaps he could turn this to his benefit.

01:28:53.880 --> 01:28:59.400
Maybe SCP-682 would hold the secret to actually
killing off his nemesis...

01:28:59.400 --> 01:29:03.540
Within 30 hours, Dr. Dan had his disappointing
answer.

01:29:03.540 --> 01:29:09.900
Both anomalies had undeniably done a real
number on each other, and Dr. Dan did take

01:29:09.900 --> 01:29:17.640
a kind of sick amusement in the mental trauma
that SCP-682 had induced in 096, but ultimately

01:29:17.640 --> 01:29:19.260
it was all for nothing.

01:29:19.260 --> 01:29:23.340
682 had skinned 096 alive and melted off its&nbsp;

01:29:23.340 --> 01:29:26.880
flesh with acid, but once&nbsp;
again, that indestructible

01:29:26.880 --> 01:29:28.740
skeleton stood firm.

01:29:28.740 --> 01:29:32.400
This had been the 27th termination attempt
since the incident.

01:29:32.400 --> 01:29:37.800
The 27th failed termination attempt, Dr. Dan
mentally corrected himself.

01:29:37.800 --> 01:29:44.160
096’s skeleton kept defeating them - Even
their most ardent attempts to penetrate the

01:29:44.160 --> 01:29:47.040
bones and destroy the creature within had
failed.

01:29:47.040 --> 01:29:51.180
During a previous attempt, they’d tried
to access the brain by putting a diamond-hard

01:29:51.180 --> 01:29:52.920
drill into its eye sockets.

01:29:52.920 --> 01:29:58.800
Of course, it was the drill that actually
broke, much to Dr. Dan’s seemingly tireless

01:29:58.800 --> 01:29:59.460
frustration.

01:29:59.460 --> 01:30:04.860
Needless to say, in the debriefing interview
with Dr. Carver, a researcher who once would

01:30:04.860 --> 01:30:08.340
have considered him a colleague, he wasn’t
in high spirits.

01:30:08.340 --> 01:30:13.440
If he managed to kill this thing, then he
would always be the hero who made a terrible

01:30:13.440 --> 01:30:16.680
sacrifice in order to save many more lives.

01:30:16.680 --> 01:30:22.860
If he didn’t kill 096, he was little more
than the monster who murdered all those innocent

01:30:22.860 --> 01:30:23.700
people for nothing.

01:30:23.700 --> 01:30:27.120
Though you’d never hear him say it out loud.

01:30:27.120 --> 01:30:32.760
If it wasn’t for that damnable indestructible
skeleton, this monster would be long dead

01:30:32.760 --> 01:30:33.300
already.

01:30:33.300 --> 01:30:37.380
If only there was some way to break the creature’s
bones...

01:30:37.380 --> 01:30:40.980
That’s when it hit him: SCP-173.

01:30:40.980 --> 01:30:42.300
The Sculpture.

01:30:42.300 --> 01:30:48.540
A Site-19 icon often overlooked because of
its more “silent but deadly” style, but

01:30:48.540 --> 01:30:52.020
undeniably one of the most frightening and
dangerous anomalies out there.

01:30:52.020 --> 01:30:57.660
It had racked up a truly shocking body count
since it was first interred at Site-19 in

01:30:57.660 --> 01:31:03.660
1993, one broken neck at a time, and it’s
one of the few creatures out there that even

01:31:03.660 --> 01:31:07.380
the Hard to Destroy Reptile himself is utterly
terrified of.

01:31:07.380 --> 01:31:13.080
Perhaps a monster that practically had a PhD
in breaking bones would be the ideal candidate

01:31:13.080 --> 01:31:15.780
to vandalize the Shy Guy’s vertebrae.

01:31:15.780 --> 01:31:21.240
Once his proposal was greenlit for initial
testing, Dr. Dan arranged for the Shy Guy

01:31:21.240 --> 01:31:25.980
- with a bag over his head, of course - to
be brought into a testing chamber with the

01:31:25.980 --> 01:31:28.440
ever-stoic SCP-173.

01:31:28.440 --> 01:31:33.900
When all of the researchers and guards were
safely outside, all it took was a single blink

01:31:33.900 --> 01:31:37.380
to make all of Dr. Dan’s darkest dreams
come true.

01:31:37.380 --> 01:31:42.360
In the nanoseconds his eyes were closed, he
heard the most beautiful sounds in the world:

01:31:42.360 --> 01:31:47.280
A loud, fleshy crunch, and a pained howl from
096.

01:31:47.280 --> 01:31:54.480
He opened his eyes to an equally wondrous
sight: 096 doubled over at an unnatural angle,

01:31:54.480 --> 01:31:57.600
its spine snapped between the fourth and fifth&nbsp;

01:31:57.600 --> 01:32:00.840
vertebrae, spinal fluid&nbsp;
leaking down the creature’s

01:32:00.840 --> 01:32:01.200
flank.

01:32:01.200 --> 01:32:04.080
That freaky little sculpture had done it.

01:32:04.080 --> 01:32:07.980
It had actually damaged the integrity of the
Shy Guy’s skeleton.

01:32:07.980 --> 01:32:12.480
It was at that moment that Dr. Dan knew he
could kill this thing.

01:32:12.480 --> 01:32:16.740
Admittedly, there was a containment breach
shortly after that when the bag slipped off

01:32:16.740 --> 01:32:21.540
of 096’s head and it charged through the
nearby steel wall, killing multiple people

01:32:21.540 --> 01:32:24.060
before being subdued and recontained.

01:32:24.060 --> 01:32:29.580
But when it comes to projects involving Dr.
Dan, a mere handful of innocent people being

01:32:29.580 --> 01:32:34.080
killed sits comfortably within the acceptable
margin of error.

01:32:34.080 --> 01:32:39.300
A day later, Dr. Dan was standing before a
crowded boardroom, with agents, researchers,

01:32:39.300 --> 01:32:43.080
and even a few members of the O5 Council in
attendance.

01:32:43.080 --> 01:32:48.480
He explained his findings to them, and the
fact that, even more importantly, they may

01:32:48.480 --> 01:32:53.640
have a way to bring the wretched existence
of 096 to an absolute halt.

01:32:53.640 --> 01:32:55.320
His plans were approved.

01:32:55.320 --> 01:32:59.040
Not long after, he was putting them into motion.

01:32:59.040 --> 01:33:05.340
Dr. Dan was surrounded by researchers and
heavily armed guards as 173 was escorted into

01:33:05.340 --> 01:33:09.600
the test chamber with a forklift. 096 was
there, still bagged.

01:33:09.600 --> 01:33:16.260
Off to the side was a giant tub of hydrofluoric
acid connected to a hose and injector attachment.

01:33:16.260 --> 01:33:18.900
If this didn’t work, nothing would.

01:33:18.900 --> 01:33:23.880
Everyone stood back in a safe zone as 173
was released to do its thing.

01:33:23.880 --> 01:33:24.840
Crunch.

01:33:24.840 --> 01:33:30.120
Just like the previous time, 096’s spine
had been successfully snapped.

01:33:30.120 --> 01:33:32.520
Spinal fluid dribbled down its skin.

01:33:32.520 --> 01:33:38.220
Everyone had rushed into the chamber - One
group kept 173 isolated with their stares.

01:33:38.220 --> 01:33:43.380
The other, headed by Dr. Dan, prepared the
acid injector and shoved it into the breach

01:33:43.380 --> 01:33:45.420
in 096’s spine.

01:33:45.420 --> 01:33:51.000
As the acid was pumped by the gallon into
096’s bones, the creature let out the most

01:33:51.000 --> 01:33:52.320
horrifying wail.

01:33:52.320 --> 01:33:58.500
It kicked and bucked as more acid was pumped
in, melting those indestructible bones from

01:33:58.500 --> 01:33:58.920
within.

01:33:58.920 --> 01:34:04.920
The creature even began to vomit acid as it
panicked, melting the bag off of its own head.

01:34:04.920 --> 01:34:07.320
Some panicked and averted their gaze.

01:34:07.320 --> 01:34:09.480
Dr. Dan didn’t.

01:34:09.480 --> 01:34:15.060
He stared directly at the creature’s face
for the first time as it melted in front of

01:34:15.060 --> 01:34:17.520
him with nothing short of pure elation.

01:34:17.520 --> 01:34:18.660
Had he won?

01:34:18.660 --> 01:34:20.280
Had he actually won?

01:34:20.280 --> 01:34:23.760
The creature let out a gurgling shriek and
the guards opened fire.

01:34:23.760 --> 01:34:28.320
Their bullets splattered into the monster’s
melting flesh and bones as it hollered and

01:34:28.320 --> 01:34:28.740
shrieked.

01:34:28.740 --> 01:34:34.140
They didn’t let up, firing up and down its
body, spraying it with white-hot lead.

01:34:34.140 --> 01:34:40.020
It began to melt and bubble away, expanding
into a great, gruesome puddle on the floor

01:34:40.020 --> 01:34:40.920
beneath them.

01:34:40.920 --> 01:34:44.340
Dr. Dan laughed like a madman.

01:34:44.340 --> 01:34:49.620
Even as the guards grabbed him and dragged
him away from the foul-smelling, greasy mess,

01:34:49.620 --> 01:34:53.640
bubbling with chemical life, he just kept
laughing.

01:34:53.640 --> 01:34:59.220
Even though he knew the death of 096 meant
an equally swift death of his own, it didn’t

01:34:59.220 --> 01:34:59.580
matter.

01:34:59.580 --> 01:35:01.680
It had all been for something.

01:35:01.680 --> 01:35:03.960
The world was saved.

01:35:03.960 --> 01:35:04.860
It was over.

01:35:04.860 --> 01:35:07.260
He’d finally won.

01:35:07.260 --> 01:35:13.260
A lot of us wish we could travel the multiverse,&nbsp;
pay a visit to another world and see all the ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:13.260 --> 01:35:18.360
in which it differs from this dimension that we&nbsp;
call home. Maybe there’s a decision that you made&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:18.360 --> 01:35:23.400
that altered the course of your entire life –&nbsp;
what college you went to, asking somebody out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:23.400 --> 01:35:28.800
accepting a job over pursuing a passion.&nbsp;
The question of ‘what if’ might have kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:28.800 --> 01:35:32.760
you wondering what your life could have&nbsp;
looked like if you picked the other path.&nbsp;

01:35:32.760 --> 01:35:39.420
Surely it would save you all that wondering and be&nbsp;
so much easier just to go and find out. Imagine if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:39.420 --> 01:35:45.540
you could simply pick a universe where you chose&nbsp;
differently, then take a quick jaunt over there to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:45.540 --> 01:35:51.060
see how things turned out for your other self.
But what if you couldn’t choose?&nbsp;

01:35:51.060 --> 01:35:56.580
Never mind whatever that decision was; what if&nbsp;
you couldn’t pick where in the infinite multiverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:56.580 --> 01:36:02.400
you ended up? If traveling from world to world&nbsp;
was as random, unpredictable, and instinctive&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:02.400 --> 01:36:08.880
as a sneeze, what then? One moment you’re safe at&nbsp;
home, surrounded by everything you know. It might&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:08.880 --> 01:36:14.040
not be perfect, but at least it's familiar. The&nbsp;
next thing you know, with a blink of your eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:14.040 --> 01:36:19.080
you’re standing in a world that maybe looks&nbsp;
similar, but giant bees are the dominant species&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:19.080 --> 01:36:24.600
instead of humans. Or the world is overrun&nbsp;
by spiders, or everyone is made out of paint.&nbsp;

01:36:24.600 --> 01:36:28.140
Needless to say, you’d be at&nbsp;
least a little disorientated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:28.140 --> 01:36:34.080
at worst absolutely terrified for your life.
That pretty much sums up the experience of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:34.080 --> 01:36:41.040
SCP-507, also known as the Reluctant Dimension&nbsp;
Hopper. From the outside, he looks to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:41.040 --> 01:36:45.960
mostly normal human man, with blonde hair,&nbsp;
green eyes; nothing all that remarkable&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:45.960 --> 01:36:52.320
about him of note save for him having a somewhat&nbsp;
unplaceable accent. Despite having a legal name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:52.320 --> 01:36:58.080
SCP-507 will try to squeeze what little enjoyment&nbsp;
he can out of introducing himself by a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:58.080 --> 01:37:05.460
nickname to everyone he meets; previous favorites&nbsp;
have included Tommy, Steve, Bruto, Guy, Houdini,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:05.460 --> 01:37:11.760
and Grabnok the Destroyer. We’ll stick with Tommy.
The sole most intriguing thing about Tommy is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:11.760 --> 01:37:14.880
that, through a process that happens&nbsp;
completely without his control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:14.880 --> 01:37:21.840
he can spontaneously disappear, only to reappear&nbsp;
after an unspecified amount of time later. During&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:21.840 --> 01:37:28.680
that time, SCP-507 will have been in a completely&nbsp;
different universe to our own. This can happen at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:28.680 --> 01:37:33.900
any time of day or night – and usually at&nbsp;
the most inconvenient moments; including&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:33.900 --> 01:37:40.440
while Tommy is in the middle of a conversation,&nbsp;
trying to sleep, or using the… ‘facilities.’&nbsp;

01:37:40.440 --> 01:37:46.440
He had no control over when it happens, however,&nbsp;
he'll always arrive in an alternate universe in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:46.440 --> 01:37:51.540
the same geographical spot he was in while in&nbsp;
ours. If he moves around in a parallel world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:51.540 --> 01:37:57.480
then when he returns, he will have relocated to&nbsp;
the corresponding place back in this universe.&nbsp;

01:37:57.480 --> 01:38:02.400
And that’s when disaster struck.
Alarms were blaring, declaring to all staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:02.400 --> 01:38:07.140
on-site that there had been a containment breach&nbsp;
in one of the anomaly’s holding cells. All hands&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:07.140 --> 01:38:12.780
were on deck, and security teams were converging,&nbsp;
dashing through the corridors. This was the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:12.780 --> 01:38:19.860
alert today, at first SCP-507 had vanished on one&nbsp;
of his unplanned little excursions, but now it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:19.860 --> 01:38:26.820
seemed that another SCP was trying to do the same.
Dazed, trying to reorientate himself, Tommy rested&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:26.820 --> 01:38:31.680
against a nearby wall. His head was spinning,&nbsp;
and he knew it would for a few more seconds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:31.680 --> 01:38:37.440
He was in a cell, similar to the one he’d just&nbsp;
been in while in another universe. Except here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:37.440 --> 01:38:44.040
back home, there was a key difference: The cell&nbsp;
was occupied. As soon as his vision cleared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:44.040 --> 01:38:49.380
Tommy looked up at the only other creature in the&nbsp;
room with him. It was a tall, wiry humanoid shape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:49.380 --> 01:38:56.400
completely hairless with pale skin, white eyes,&nbsp;
and a large, gaping jaw. Turning to face him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:56.400 --> 01:39:00.540
Tommy instantly recognized the creature from&nbsp;
a description he’d been read by a Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:00.540 --> 01:39:06.600
researcher: it was SCP-096.
Uh oh, spaghetti-o’s.&nbsp;

01:39:06.600 --> 01:39:12.660
Realizing its face was being observed, the Shy Guy&nbsp;
started screeching, first an uncontrollable wail&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:12.660 --> 01:39:18.060
of distress that quickly gave way to a guttural,&nbsp;
animalistic roar of anger. It covered its face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:18.060 --> 01:39:22.680
babbling to itself as Tommy backed away,&nbsp;
banging his hand against the sealed wall&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:22.680 --> 01:39:29.100
of 096’s cell. Then, the creature turned towards&nbsp;
him, and lunged, racing across the room to where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:29.100 --> 01:39:35.280
SCP-507 was trapped. All he could do was close&nbsp;
his eyes tight and think about running away…&nbsp;

01:39:35.280 --> 01:39:41.220
Tommy could feel a headache brewing in his skull,&nbsp;
and caught traces of a familiar flash through his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:41.220 --> 01:39:46.680
closed eyelids. He gently opened them back up.&nbsp;
It had happened again, maybe for the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:46.680 --> 01:39:51.960
ever at a moment when he actually needed to get&nbsp;
away. Staggering to his feet, Tommy looked around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:51.960 --> 01:39:57.660
He was in the cell still, or rather, another&nbsp;
version of it in a different universe yet again.&nbsp;

01:39:57.660 --> 01:40:01.440
Knowing that he wouldn’t be able to&nbsp;
predict when he would next displace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:01.440 --> 01:40:07.680
he didn’t want to reappear right back where he&nbsp;
had started, with SCP-096 poised to kill him. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:07.680 --> 01:40:12.720
relocating was top priority. Fortunately, the&nbsp;
cell actually had a door in this universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:12.720 --> 01:40:18.000
and that door wasn’t locked. In fact, none&nbsp;
of the doors were locked or even guarded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:18.000 --> 01:40:23.640
and the Foundation facility, while looking more&nbsp;
or less the same, was completely abandoned. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:23.640 --> 01:40:28.860
almost looked like it had never even been used.
Stepping out of the entrance, Tommy trekked&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:28.860 --> 01:40:34.260
his way towards the city. He knew it was a short&nbsp;
hike away, and by the time he could feel blisters&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:34.260 --> 01:40:39.480
forming on his feet, he was close enough that it&nbsp;
didn’t matter. Within another few short steps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:39.480 --> 01:40:44.640
he was standing among the tall buildings and&nbsp;
surprisingly empty streets. One of the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:44.640 --> 01:40:49.080
things that struck Tommy about this universe&nbsp;
was that there were hardly any people around&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:49.080 --> 01:40:55.500
outside. On top of that, the roads were devoid&nbsp;
of cars. Everything around was so quiet; the air&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:55.500 --> 01:40:59.880
was still and clean when he breathed it in.
The sound of footsteps against the pavement&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:59.880 --> 01:41:04.500
immediately caught Tommy’s attention, the&nbsp;
first sign of anyone else around other than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:04.500 --> 01:41:09.300
him. Racing towards the noise of another&nbsp;
person, Tommy turned a corner and bumped&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:09.300 --> 01:41:15.000
straight into an old man in a long coat, who had&nbsp;
clearly been in a hurry, and quickly revealed he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:15.000 --> 01:41:19.200
didn’t have the best temperament either.
“Watch where you’re going!” he shouted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:19.200 --> 01:41:22.680
caught off-guard by the sight of another person&nbsp;
put on the streets. “What in the hell are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:22.680 --> 01:41:25.860
you playing at, sneaking up on me like that?&nbsp;
You trying to get yourself killed out here?”&nbsp;

01:41:25.860 --> 01:41:30.960
“Oh, definitely not!” Tommy said, climbing back&nbsp;
to his feet, hands held out apologetically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:30.960 --> 01:41:34.320
“I’m so sorry mister, I was just-”
“You got a death wish or something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:34.320 --> 01:41:40.920
kid?” the old man demanded, marching past him.
“No!” SCP-507 protested, chasing after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:40.920 --> 01:41:45.900
man. “Wait, what makes you say that?”
“As if you don’t know,” he scoffed. “Look&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:45.900 --> 01:41:50.640
around you, look up at the sky! There’s a storm&nbsp;
about to hit, any second now, it’s a-coming. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:50.640 --> 01:41:53.880
if you don’t get inside, you’re a goner.”
“What could be so dangerous about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:53.880 --> 01:41:57.780
a thunderstorm?” Tommy asked.
The old man stared back at him with a look&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:57.780 --> 01:42:03.300
of confusion on his wizened face, looking at Tommy&nbsp;
like he had just said something utterly bizarre.&nbsp;

01:42:03.300 --> 01:42:09.720
“What the hell’s a thunderstorm?” the man asked.
Feeling equally confused, Tommy craned his neck&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:09.720 --> 01:42:15.360
up towards the sky. Looming above were a swirling&nbsp;
mass of clouds, the kind anyone would expect to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:15.360 --> 01:42:21.180
be accompanied by severe winds, and the deafening&nbsp;
boom of thunder. But the wind was still, and there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:21.180 --> 01:42:27.360
was nothing but silence. Until a choking, gurgling&nbsp;
sound came from somewhere behind Tommy’s back.&nbsp;

01:42:27.360 --> 01:42:34.140
He spun back around to see the tall, wiry frame&nbsp;
of SCP-096, its long fingers gripping tightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:34.140 --> 01:42:39.360
around the old man’s throat. It had followed&nbsp;
Tommy, and he knew that anyone who saw its face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:39.360 --> 01:42:44.760
even in a picture, would find themselves chased&nbsp;
down on foot by the creature. But he had no idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:44.760 --> 01:42:49.560
the Shy Guy could travel between dimensions.&nbsp;
He’d foolishly allowed himself to think that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:49.560 --> 01:42:54.900
was safe, out of the monster’s reach, but now it&nbsp;
seemed to be showing him that the opposite was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:54.900 --> 01:43:00.240
true. It hoisted the helpless old man upwards&nbsp;
by his neck, his shoes barely able to graze&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:00.240 --> 01:43:05.460
the pavement as his legs flailed, eyes rolling&nbsp;
back as the Shy Guy clamped his throat shut.&nbsp;

01:43:05.460 --> 01:43:10.560
The very second Tommy turned to run, the clouds&nbsp;
churned above and, without so much as a warning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:10.560 --> 01:43:15.540
the storm hit. Something wasn’t right, Tommy&nbsp;
could feel his feet lifting off the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:15.540 --> 01:43:21.180
immediately panicking as he thought SCP-096&nbsp;
had grabbed him. But, looking over his shoulder&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:21.180 --> 01:43:26.460
showed the creature was still a few feet behind,&nbsp;
and starting to gently float up off the ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:26.460 --> 01:43:32.160
too. Instinctively, Tommy’s legs started thrashing&nbsp;
around, trying to gain a solid footing. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:32.160 --> 01:43:37.380
all his urgent motions did was cause him to&nbsp;
start spinning uncontrollably, as the gravity&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:37.380 --> 01:43:43.080
storm lifted him higher and higher into the air.
Spinning around as he was taken further upwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:43.080 --> 01:43:49.020
Tommy caught sight of SCP-096. The Shy Guy&nbsp;
was staying still, as if it was still in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:49.020 --> 01:43:53.460
control of its body’s motion while he was&nbsp;
wildly flipping over himself. The tall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:53.460 --> 01:43:58.500
pale creature drifted closer to the side of&nbsp;
a nearby building, with Tommy barely able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:58.500 --> 01:44:03.600
reach the top of the streetlights as he was pulled&nbsp;
further away from the safety of having both his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:03.600 --> 01:44:09.780
feet on the ground. Just as he saw SCP-096 push&nbsp;
itself off the edge of the building, drifting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:09.780 --> 01:44:14.880
straight through the air towards him, Tommy shut&nbsp;
his eyes again and prayed he’d displace again.&nbsp;

01:44:14.880 --> 01:44:19.620
Sure enough, a flash of light came bursting&nbsp;
out in time with another headache. Suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:19.620 --> 01:44:23.640
he had appeared in the exact same&nbsp;
spot in another universe. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:23.640 --> 01:44:28.800
here there was no gravity storm in progress&nbsp;
here. In fact, no such phenomena existed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:28.800 --> 01:44:34.560
the laws of physics were much more in keeping with&nbsp;
those in Tommy’s home, our universe. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:34.560 --> 01:44:40.800
that would be more reassuring if he wasn’t already&nbsp;
several feet up in the air. The second he arrived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:40.800 --> 01:44:45.240
Tommy started plummeting back down to&nbsp;
Earth, screaming as he felt himself falling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:45.240 --> 01:44:50.160
keeping his eyes screwed shut as he dared not look&nbsp;
at the ground he was currently hurtling towards.&nbsp;

01:44:50.160 --> 01:44:55.620
His plummet was broken by something, not&nbsp;
particularly soft. Whatever it was seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:55.620 --> 01:45:00.540
to tower high enough that Tommy only picked up&nbsp;
a few bruises as he hit it. But the fall didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:00.540 --> 01:45:06.180
stop instantly, instead, he kept on toppling&nbsp;
through whatever he had landed on. It was some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:06.180 --> 01:45:12.120
kind of mass, piled up almost as tall as a&nbsp;
building, a collection of lots of… things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:12.120 --> 01:45:16.860
that were all brushing past Tommy the more&nbsp;
he fell. He hadn’t yet stopped for long&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:16.860 --> 01:45:21.420
enough to recognize the sensations, that&nbsp;
is, until he landed with a painful thud.&nbsp;

01:45:21.420 --> 01:45:26.700
Slowly opening his eyes back up, Tommy could&nbsp;
barely see anything in front of him. He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:26.700 --> 01:45:31.800
so deep in the mass that the pile was blocking&nbsp;
out the sunlight, with only a few beams bleeding&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:31.800 --> 01:45:36.720
through the gaps, but barely enough to give&nbsp;
him a clear view of where he was. Tommy tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:36.720 --> 01:45:41.340
to crawl forward, squeezed between the heavy&nbsp;
things around him, pushing though whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:41.340 --> 01:45:45.900
tiny little crevice he could find. As he did,&nbsp;
the weight of the pile shifted around him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:45.900 --> 01:45:50.940
and he was worried that any moment he would move&nbsp;
the wrong part of it, bringing the whole structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:50.940 --> 01:45:56.280
collapsing down on top of him like a toppled jenga&nbsp;
tower, only big enough to crush him to death.&nbsp;

01:45:56.280 --> 01:46:01.440
He forced himself not to think about exactly what&nbsp;
the pile was made of either. It was already long&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:01.440 --> 01:46:07.560
past the point where Tommy recognized what each…&nbsp;
thing felt like. Plus, there was the added stench,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:07.560 --> 01:46:13.740
something – or lots of somethings – rotting and&nbsp;
producing a stomach-turning odor as it decayed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:13.740 --> 01:46:18.720
Even when you’re trying not to look, it can be&nbsp;
hard to mistake the feeling of a hand, an arm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:18.720 --> 01:46:24.360
cold skin against his. The light from beyond the&nbsp;
pyramid was getting closer, he could see it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:24.360 --> 01:46:28.860
starting to illuminate more of his surroundings&nbsp;
the close her crawled to the perimeter. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:28.860 --> 01:46:32.700
he could have very easily used the torch&nbsp;
the Foundation let him keep with him. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:32.700 --> 01:46:37.020
Tommy wasn’t quite ready to see what he was&nbsp;
crawling through yet, intentionally shutting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:37.020 --> 01:46:40.920
his eyes and opting instead to follow the&nbsp;
faint glow and distant warmth of the sun.&nbsp;

01:46:40.920 --> 01:46:47.340
Breaching the outer side of the pile, Tommy gulped&nbsp;
down lungfuls of air. It barely tasted or smelled&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:47.340 --> 01:46:52.740
that much cleaner than the air within the mass,&nbsp;
but at least he was free. Clawing himself out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:52.740 --> 01:46:58.800
along the ground, he finally, carefully, stood up,&nbsp;
still keeping both eyes screwed shut. Part of him&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:58.800 --> 01:47:03.420
really didn’t want to take a look around this&nbsp;
new dimension, maybe if he waited long enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:03.420 --> 01:47:10.740
he would displace again and end up somewhere with&nbsp;
less… decomposing. But then he remembered SCP-096&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:10.740 --> 01:47:16.440
was still after him, and he had no idea how&nbsp;
to escape it. He needed to try and figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:16.440 --> 01:47:21.900
what he was going to do, and the only way to&nbsp;
do that… was to look around at where he was.&nbsp;

01:47:21.900 --> 01:47:28.080
Lifting his eyelids, the different sized masses&nbsp;
of all shades, shapes and sizes lay before him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:28.080 --> 01:47:34.260
Each one was coated in one unifying color,&nbsp;
all of them slick with the crimson of blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:34.260 --> 01:47:38.640
against skin. Tommy could feel the urge to&nbsp;
throw up quickly shooting up from the pit&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:38.640 --> 01:47:42.900
of his stomach towards his throat as he looked&nbsp;
at the massive pile of bodies in front of him.&nbsp;

01:47:42.900 --> 01:47:48.480
It was enormous, there were so many dead people&nbsp;
all laying on top of one another that the pile&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:48.480 --> 01:47:53.220
reached up as tall as office buildings. And&nbsp;
the pile that he had fallen into and crawled&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:53.220 --> 01:47:59.400
his way out of wasn’t the only one either. As&nbsp;
far as the eye could see, everyone was dead.&nbsp;

01:47:59.400 --> 01:48:04.440
Feeling a heavy sob at the back of his throat,&nbsp;
Tommy swallowed it when he heard another sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:04.440 --> 01:48:11.160
above the low buzzing of flies over the human&nbsp;
carcasses. Someone… something else was crying&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:11.160 --> 01:48:17.040
nearby, and the moment he turned around, the&nbsp;
familiar figure caught Tommy’s eye. Even without&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:17.040 --> 01:48:22.320
being the only other living thing around for&nbsp;
miles, the Shy Guy still stood out clear as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:22.320 --> 01:48:27.600
day. Its distress at finding itself in this&nbsp;
horrible dimension briefly mirrored Tommy’s own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:27.600 --> 01:48:32.880
until it restablished its psychic link&nbsp;
with its victim and gave chase once more.&nbsp;

01:48:32.880 --> 01:48:39.420
SCP-096 started dashing towards SCP-507. For&nbsp;
a split second, he thought about just letting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:39.420 --> 01:48:44.220
it catch him; what difference would it make in a&nbsp;
place like this? But then, the hallucinations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:44.220 --> 01:48:49.920
bright colors and a stinging deep within his skull&nbsp;
hit Tommy. And just like that, he was gone again.&nbsp;

01:48:49.920 --> 01:48:55.320
It was nighttime in the next dimension Tommy&nbsp;
arrived in. The cold air and dim light of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:55.320 --> 01:48:59.880
street were enough to give that away. But at&nbsp;
least he was away from that last world filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:59.880 --> 01:49:05.400
with discarded and unburied piles of corpses,&nbsp;
he thought to himself. Surely, he couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:05.400 --> 01:49:09.900
possibly be anywhere worse than that now…
The first sight that Tommy was met with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:09.900 --> 01:49:14.940
was a huge, white LCD screen, big enough&nbsp;
to cover the entire side of one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:14.940 --> 01:49:20.100
skyscrapers facing him. Emblazoned against&nbsp;
the bright white glow was a familiar symbol,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:20.100 --> 01:49:26.160
a circle with three arrows crossing its outline&nbsp;
and pointing towards its center. Around that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:26.160 --> 01:49:31.920
a thin frame, another circle, this one with three&nbsp;
protruding rectangles around the circumference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:31.920 --> 01:49:38.700
in line with the arrow tails. Thick lettered text&nbsp;
appeared on the screen too, beneath the emblem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:38.700 --> 01:49:44.340
boldly proclaiming that anyone reading&nbsp;
it should ‘Submit. Comply. Persevere.’&nbsp;

01:49:44.340 --> 01:49:48.900
Tommy blinked in disbelief, looking around.&nbsp;
There was hardly anyone else around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:48.900 --> 01:49:54.180
save for a few people staring at him; had they&nbsp;
seen him appear? Or was it just because he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:54.180 --> 01:49:58.260
dressed so differently to them? Each of&nbsp;
them had on a grimy pair of overalls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:58.260 --> 01:50:03.720
sporting more of that same symbol on the chest&nbsp;
and across the back. Every wall of every nearby&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:03.720 --> 01:50:08.520
building was overfilled with posters featuring&nbsp;
matching imagery, each one posing different&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:08.520 --> 01:50:13.920
questions in the same unforgiving, accusatory&nbsp;
text against its clinical white background.&nbsp;

01:50:13.920 --> 01:50:19.560
One poster read ‘Could you be anomalous?&nbsp;
Submit for testing today.’ Another had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:19.560 --> 01:50:26.040
following notice: ‘Curfew still in effect, 10am&nbsp;
to 8am the following morning. All citizens must&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:26.040 --> 01:50:31.800
Comply.’ And one more touted that ‘Only through&nbsp;
Persevering will we all continue to thrive.’&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:31.800 --> 01:50:37.140
Each of them was signed with the same name and&nbsp;
logo to match, the organization that Tommy had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:37.140 --> 01:50:42.300
come to put some amount of trust in, even though&nbsp;
they weren’t always clear with him. But he knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:42.300 --> 01:50:46.800
they weren’t the bad guys, or at least, the&nbsp;
version that existed in his universe didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:46.800 --> 01:50:53.100
seem to be too bad. They had questionable&nbsp;
methods, but they weren’t evil… weren’t this.&nbsp;

01:50:53.100 --> 01:50:58.800
“By order of the SCP Foundation!” A loud,&nbsp;
militaristic voice barked from behind Tommy. “Get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:58.800 --> 01:51:03.000
down on your knees with your hands behind your&nbsp;
head. You will surrender any anomalous technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:03.000 --> 01:51:07.140
and declare any memetic effects or cognito&nbsp;
hazardous abilities before we approach you.”&nbsp;

01:51:07.140 --> 01:51:11.220
Tommy looked over his shoulder, met with&nbsp;
the barrels of several automatic weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:11.220 --> 01:51:16.380
that were now being trained on him. Instantly&nbsp;
he threw his hands in the air, terrified that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:16.380 --> 01:51:22.260
this alternate SCP Foundation might be far more&nbsp;
trigger-happy than the version of them he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:22.260 --> 01:51:27.540
used to. From the look at the oppressive text&nbsp;
on the huge screen, the propaganda posters and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:27.540 --> 01:51:32.760
the matching uniforms ordinary people seemed&nbsp;
forced to wear, this Foundation seemed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:32.760 --> 01:51:38.520
far more ruthless. They had transformed from&nbsp;
a secretive organization into a full-blown&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:38.520 --> 01:51:45.300
authoritarian regime, maybe even in control&nbsp;
of the entire world, ruling with an iron fist.&nbsp;

01:51:45.300 --> 01:51:49.080
“On your knees!” the commander of&nbsp;
the Mobile Task Force shouted again.&nbsp;

01:51:49.080 --> 01:51:52.920
The aggression in his voice, as well&nbsp;
as the several laser pointers from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:52.920 --> 01:51:57.180
his men’s’ guns that were hovering over his&nbsp;
chest, prompted Tommy to do as he was told.&nbsp;

01:51:57.180 --> 01:52:01.620
“Do you have anything to declare before&nbsp;
we approach?” the MTF leader barked.&nbsp;

01:52:01.620 --> 01:52:08.340
“N-no!” Tommy stammered before realizing that&nbsp;
was probably an inadvisable answer. “I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:08.340 --> 01:52:13.320
yes! I’m… I’m SCP-507, but that number&nbsp;
might refer to someone different here. Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:13.320 --> 01:52:17.220
what I’m saying is, this isn’t my dimension,&nbsp;
okay? I have a condition that sends me to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:17.220 --> 01:52:19.740
different universes at random, I can’t&nbsp;
control it, it just happens. Please,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:19.740 --> 01:52:23.040
for the love of god, don’t shoot!”
“Better radio into command,” the team&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:23.040 --> 01:52:26.700
leader told one of his subordinates. “Tell&nbsp;
them to run that number against the Archives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:26.700 --> 01:52:30.660
then inform Lord Adjudicator Clef that&nbsp;
we may have a containment breach.”&nbsp;

01:52:30.660 --> 01:52:34.980
Overhearing what they were saying instantly&nbsp;
reminded Tommy that he was still being chased.&nbsp;

01:52:34.980 --> 01:52:38.580
“Wait,” he called to the elite operatives.&nbsp;
“There’s something else you need to know!”&nbsp;

01:52:38.580 --> 01:52:42.840
“What’s that, 507?” the commander&nbsp;
sighed, lacking the patience for this.&nbsp;

01:52:42.840 --> 01:52:47.460
“There’s another anomaly after me!” Tommy&nbsp;
explained hurriedly. “I don’t know how long&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:47.460 --> 01:52:51.420
it’ll take to get here, just that when it&nbsp;
does… it’ll kill everyone to get at me.”&nbsp;

01:52:51.420 --> 01:52:54.900
“I’ve heard enough, troopers take&nbsp;
him away,” the commander grunted.&nbsp;

01:52:54.900 --> 01:52:59.100
Two of the MTF team rushed up to Tommy and&nbsp;
grappled either of his arms, not letting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:59.100 --> 01:53:04.020
go despite his protests. They started dragging&nbsp;
him towards their armored vehicle, looking like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:04.020 --> 01:53:07.800
Tommy was about to spend the foreseeable future&nbsp;
in the clutches of one of the worst versions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:07.800 --> 01:53:13.560
of the Foundation. That is, until a tall, pale&nbsp;
creature stepped out from behind the transport.&nbsp;

01:53:13.560 --> 01:53:18.900
The MTFs were instantly frenzied, and yells of&nbsp;
another anomalous contact were quickly drowned&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:18.900 --> 01:53:24.180
out by the noise of gunfire. All the while, the&nbsp;
Shy Guy was casually moving between the amassed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:24.180 --> 01:53:29.340
troops, gripping their heads, twisting them all&nbsp;
the way around until a sickening crunch rang out.&nbsp;

01:53:29.340 --> 01:53:35.700
Dropped back to the ground, SCP-507 started to&nbsp;
feel another headache coming on, knowing that any&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:35.700 --> 01:53:41.700
second he’d displace again and end up in another&nbsp;
alternate universe. And for the first time today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:41.700 --> 01:53:46.860
he was okay with that. It was better&nbsp;
for their chase to go on if it meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:46.860 --> 01:53:51.660
that him and the Shy Guy didn’t have to&nbsp;
spend another minute in this universe.

01:53:51.660 --> 01:53:53.760
He’s waiting for you in the dark.

01:53:53.760 --> 01:54:01.680
He sits, cross-legged, still as a placid lake
and patient as a praying monk six feet deep

01:54:01.680 --> 01:54:02.400
in meditation.

01:54:02.400 --> 01:54:04.740
He’s been like this for so long.

01:54:04.740 --> 01:54:13.320
His body oil-slicked with deadly black grease,
skin ancient and haggard, eyes black and flat

01:54:13.320 --> 01:54:18.840
with the kind of calm you only find in crawling
insects and Great White Sharks.

01:54:18.840 --> 01:54:24.480
His lipless mouth is stretched across his
jawbone in an ugly, black rictus.

01:54:24.480 --> 01:54:30.720
He may be an Old Man, but trust us, he’s
got plenty of new tricks he’d love to show

01:54:30.720 --> 01:54:30.960
you.

01:54:30.960 --> 01:54:37.560
He sat in the loneliest place in the world:
The deep, dark bowels of an SCP Foundation

01:54:37.560 --> 01:54:43.260
containment site, surrounded by a complex
confluence of containment measures: Different

01:54:43.260 --> 01:54:49.140
shapes, running liquids, bright lights, the
kind of steel so thick a nuclear weapon would

01:54:49.140 --> 01:54:50.340
only leave it scuffed.

01:54:50.340 --> 01:54:53.280
All the things that had a tendency to...

01:54:53.280 --> 01:54:54.600
Confuse him.

01:54:54.600 --> 01:54:56.580
Because that’s all they ever did.

01:54:56.580 --> 01:54:59.700
The Old Man laughed to himself, very quietly.

01:54:59.700 --> 01:55:05.580
All the people out there are so scared of
him because of one immutable fact: He isn’t

01:55:05.580 --> 01:55:07.740
trapped here, he’s just resting.

01:55:07.740 --> 01:55:12.960
By virtue of his unique anomalous abilities
- to pass through solids at will and resist

01:55:12.960 --> 01:55:18.180
any form of physical damage they can dole
out - there is no way to really contain him

01:55:18.180 --> 01:55:19.680
like all the others.

01:55:19.680 --> 01:55:25.500
All the SCP Foundation could hope to do was
make leaving inconvenient enough for him to

01:55:25.500 --> 01:55:30.360
make his little adventures on the outside
less appealing than just sitting around, doing

01:55:30.360 --> 01:55:30.780
nothing.

01:55:30.780 --> 01:55:37.020
After all, he had all the time in the world,
and when you always have a “Later”, there’s

01:55:37.020 --> 01:55:39.360
a lot less pressure to do anything now.

01:55:39.360 --> 01:55:46.500
But like all thinking creatures - And please,
be aware that SCP-106, the wretched, terrifying

01:55:46.500 --> 01:55:51.960
Old Man, is very much a thinking creature
- he was subject to occasional boredom.

01:55:51.960 --> 01:55:56.700
And when most people are bored, they might
play a video game, watch a few YouTube videos,

01:55:56.700 --> 01:56:00.120
or rewatch It’s Always Sunny In Philadelphia
for the fifth time.

01:56:00.120 --> 01:56:03.540
But for the Old Man, killing boredom involved...

01:56:03.540 --> 01:56:05.220
Well, killing people.

01:56:05.220 --> 01:56:07.620
But not just killing people, of course.

01:56:07.620 --> 01:56:13.260
Truth be told, for him, killing was really
the least entertaining part of the whole affair.

01:56:13.260 --> 01:56:14.580
The screams?

01:56:14.580 --> 01:56:15.720
The begging?

01:56:15.720 --> 01:56:17.280
The desperate pleas?

01:56:17.280 --> 01:56:20.460
Warm tears on shaking cheeks?

01:56:20.460 --> 01:56:22.260
Convulsions of pain?

01:56:22.260 --> 01:56:23.880
Cold sweats?

01:56:23.880 --> 01:56:27.480
Fear so intense it gives way to gibbering
madness?

01:56:27.480 --> 01:56:32.640
That dull sheen of dead hope in their eyes
when they then realize that their doom is

01:56:32.640 --> 01:56:39.960
inevitable, and that he, their captor, their
torturer, would decide the exact moment they

01:56:39.960 --> 01:56:41.640
slipped into the void?

01:56:41.640 --> 01:56:47.460
Oh, that was what he lived for - If you could
call what the Old Man did Living.

01:56:47.460 --> 01:56:50.160
He cracked his neck and licked his teeth.

01:56:50.160 --> 01:56:51.720
Was he salivating?

01:56:51.720 --> 01:56:55.560
All those thoughts about pain seemed to have
got him worked up.

01:56:55.560 --> 01:56:58.380
He could feel the distant thumping of his
heart.

01:56:58.380 --> 01:57:03.540
Old, black blood pumping through the dusty
corridors of his vascular system.

01:57:03.540 --> 01:57:09.300
Synapses booming and sparking with excitement
at the thought of plumbing new depths of human

01:57:09.300 --> 01:57:14.040
misery, like a scholar of suffering, a pioneer
of pain.

01:57:14.040 --> 01:57:19.080
All those silly Foundation drones, with the
audacity to think they’re in control.

01:57:19.080 --> 01:57:21.480
He’d teach them to scream again...

01:57:21.480 --> 01:57:26.700
Meanwhile, in the control room for the Old
Man’s containment chamber, all hell was

01:57:26.700 --> 01:57:27.600
breaking loose.

01:57:27.600 --> 01:57:32.640
Monitors on the monster’s vitals were all
spiking: Heart and brain activity peaked,

01:57:32.640 --> 01:57:36.120
just as they always did before the Old Man
decided to get active.

01:57:36.120 --> 01:57:41.280
Cameras in his containment chamber showed
him rising steadily to his feet, cracking

01:57:41.280 --> 01:57:43.560
his joints, limbering up.

01:57:43.560 --> 01:57:48.720
His body started to produce that terrible
corrosive mucus at an accelerated rate, pooling

01:57:48.720 --> 01:57:53.700
around his feet and unleashing squalls of
noxious fumes as it melted into the floor.

01:57:53.700 --> 01:57:59.100
Years of Foundation testing, and they still
couldn’t find any material that could withstand

01:57:59.100 --> 01:58:00.960
that vile black slop...

01:58:00.960 --> 01:58:04.980
As the Old Man began strolling towards the
wall, a containment specialist in the control

01:58:04.980 --> 01:58:09.240
room grabbed a nearby phone and sounded the
alarm for the containment breach.

01:58:09.240 --> 01:58:15.000
They needed a Mobile Task Force and every
guard on site mobilized to tackle 106 before

01:58:15.000 --> 01:58:18.660
he got into the facility proper and started
causing havoc.

01:58:18.660 --> 01:58:23.220
They couldn’t risk it escaping, either - The
infamous incident when the Old Man escaped

01:58:23.220 --> 01:58:27.060
for a Halloween massacre still haunted the
nightmares of many a staff member.

01:58:27.060 --> 01:58:30.000
He had to be stopped - Now!

01:58:30.000 --> 01:58:32.640
A small fighting force was quickly armed and&nbsp;

01:58:32.640 --> 01:58:35.700
assembled, along with armor&nbsp;
and several high-intensity

01:58:35.700 --> 01:58:40.740
portable lights to hopefully slow the monster
down in time for them to grab a D-Class and

01:58:40.740 --> 01:58:42.000
set up the femur breaker.

01:58:42.000 --> 01:58:46.320
By the time they all reached the hallway they
intended to use as a choke point for the escape,

01:58:46.320 --> 01:58:51.540
the Old Man was already half way through the
outer layer of defence, phasing through the

01:58:51.540 --> 01:58:57.060
wall with that same terrible, manic grin he
always had when he was about to inflict terrible

01:58:57.060 --> 01:58:58.920
violence for the sheer fun of it.

01:58:58.920 --> 01:59:02.940
The guards and task force members trained
their lights and weapons on him.

01:59:02.940 --> 01:59:07.980
All of them had seen terrible things on the
job - the kind of things that would saddle

01:59:07.980 --> 01:59:13.080
almost anyone with a lifetime of trauma - but
almost nothing compared to the horror of direct

01:59:13.080 --> 01:59:16.200
engagement during a 106 containment breach.

01:59:16.200 --> 01:59:21.180
That terrible old coffin dodger was Hell on
two legs.

01:59:21.180 --> 01:59:25.080
When they discovered, to their horror, that
the lights didn’t seem to be slowing him

01:59:25.080 --> 01:59:31.500
down as much as anticipated, they fell back
on Plan B: That B standing for, “Blast the

01:59:31.500 --> 01:59:34.140
living hell out of him with everything they
had.”

01:59:34.140 --> 01:59:39.300
The personnel opened fire, pumping round after
round into his dark form.

01:59:39.300 --> 01:59:44.280
While each gave a loud, meaty splatter, the
Old Man didn’t even flinch.

01:59:44.280 --> 01:59:50.160
When he was close enough, all those months
of lethargy gave way to sudden, deadly speed.

01:59:50.160 --> 01:59:53.040
In many ways, the Old Man was like a crocodile.

01:59:53.040 --> 01:59:57.720
A fierce and intelligent ambush predator who
conserved his energy wisely.

01:59:57.720 --> 02:00:02.280
He could seem so deceptively languid that
you could easily mistake him for something

02:00:02.280 --> 02:00:03.420
slow and harmless.

02:00:03.420 --> 02:00:08.520
But when you got within arm’s reach, the
sudden quickness and ferocity would take you

02:00:08.520 --> 02:00:09.000
off guard.

02:00:09.000 --> 02:00:11.940
And by then, it’s already too late.

02:00:11.940 --> 02:00:16.200
Within thirty seconds, the Old Man was the
only thing left alive in that hallway.

02:00:16.200 --> 02:00:22.140
All the guards and Task Force operatives sent
to slow him down lay dead, dripping with his

02:00:22.140 --> 02:00:23.400
black mucus.

02:00:23.400 --> 02:00:28.980
There was silence, save for the quiet fizzing
of the mucus eating into their clothes and

02:00:28.980 --> 02:00:29.280
skin.

02:00:29.280 --> 02:00:31.020
The Old Man sighed.

02:00:31.020 --> 02:00:32.520
How boring.

02:00:32.520 --> 02:00:36.660
He’d killed so many people now, it all started
to seem a little...

02:00:36.660 --> 02:00:37.920
Samey.

02:00:37.920 --> 02:00:43.020
How many screams could you hear before they
all just blended into a single shrill whine?

02:00:43.020 --> 02:00:46.860
You can get sick of even your favorite meal
if you eat it too often.

02:00:46.860 --> 02:00:48.840
But that didn’t matter.

02:00:48.840 --> 02:00:54.780
The Old Man had a different order of business
today: He happened to be inside an SCP Foundation

02:00:54.780 --> 02:01:00.180
containment facility, filled with so many
fascinating specimens of non-human entities

02:01:00.180 --> 02:01:05.760
that the one they called SCP-106 simply couldn’t
wait to torture.

02:01:05.760 --> 02:01:10.920
He’d only met a small portion of the ones
they had contained here, but given what a

02:01:10.920 --> 02:01:15.720
fascinating experience his past attack on
his fellow anomalies was, he couldn’t wait

02:01:15.720 --> 02:01:17.040
to meet some more.

02:01:17.040 --> 02:01:21.660
Meanwhile, the site’s top brass did all
they could to try to mobilize a second response

02:01:21.660 --> 02:01:26.340
to the rogue senior citizen from hell currently
strolling through their high-security containment

02:01:26.340 --> 02:01:26.820
facility.

02:01:26.820 --> 02:01:30.360
Could they get in contact with other Mobile
Task Forces deployed in the area?

02:01:30.360 --> 02:01:35.340
Maybe they could get Hammer Down or Samsara
in on this mess before anything got really

02:01:35.340 --> 02:01:35.940
out of hand?

02:01:35.940 --> 02:01:41.700
The Old Man seemed more intent on something
this time, they suspected even the femur breaker

02:01:41.700 --> 02:01:46.380
might not work in luring him back until he
claimed whatever he deemed to be his prize.

02:01:46.380 --> 02:01:49.080
Maybe they should contact the O5 Council...

02:01:49.080 --> 02:01:54.300
Security guards were tracking 106’s progress
through the facility via their vast networks

02:01:54.300 --> 02:01:55.500
of security cameras.

02:01:55.500 --> 02:02:00.060
They warned any research, administration,
and janitorial staff to clear out of his path,

02:02:00.060 --> 02:02:02.520
unless they felt like being tortured to death.

02:02:02.520 --> 02:02:07.560
He hadn’t dragged anyone into his pocket
dimension yet, so whoever that privilege was

02:02:07.560 --> 02:02:10.680
reserved for would probably&nbsp;
be getting a particular&nbsp;

02:02:10.680 --> 02:02:12.300
sticky experience.

02:02:12.300 --> 02:02:16.260
They could only speculate as to the silent
beast’s intentions.

02:02:16.260 --> 02:02:18.180
One guard noticed something.

02:02:18.180 --> 02:02:21.060
When cross-referencing 106’s movements with&nbsp;

02:02:21.060 --> 02:02:23.880
a map of the facility, they&nbsp;
were able to triangulate

02:02:23.880 --> 02:02:29.940
where he was heading: Straight for the containment
chamber of SCP-096.

02:02:29.940 --> 02:02:35.100
The guard’s lips curled into a slight smile,
thinking, “If one of you freaks can actually

02:02:35.100 --> 02:02:38.700
kill the other, maybe something positive will
come out of this disaster...”

02:02:38.700 --> 02:02:42.060
The Old Man was having a stroll down memory
lane.

02:02:42.060 --> 02:02:46.680
The time prior, when he broke out of his cell
and decided to hassle his fellow intimates

02:02:46.680 --> 02:02:51.840
was certainly eventful - He wound up in the
company of some cantankerous reptile and a

02:02:51.840 --> 02:02:55.200
Korean fox woman, an unlikely pair if ever
there was one.

02:02:55.200 --> 02:02:59.460
After finding himself in a room with them,
he decided that they should have a little

02:02:59.460 --> 02:03:04.020
more privacy, and dragged them back into his
pocket dimension for further fun.

02:03:04.020 --> 02:03:08.160
Of course, with two at once, his attention
had been a little split.

02:03:08.160 --> 02:03:12.840
They spent as much time tussling with each
other as resisting his attempts to torment

02:03:12.840 --> 02:03:13.140
them.

02:03:13.140 --> 02:03:18.720
They’d definitely put him through his paces
that day, and he gave far better than he got:

02:03:18.720 --> 02:03:24.300
After so many hours of brutal mutilation,
there was barely any of the lizard left, despite

02:03:24.300 --> 02:03:28.500
its frustrating insistence on quickly healing
from so many of its wounds.

02:03:28.500 --> 02:03:34.680
Still, it was good exercise, even if the results
had been less than satisfying.

02:03:34.680 --> 02:03:40.560
If he could get another anomaly alone, though,
he’d be able to take his time, and really

02:03:40.560 --> 02:03:42.900
savor the pain he caused.

02:03:42.900 --> 02:03:45.660
Oh, that sounded heavenly...

02:03:45.660 --> 02:03:49.200
Soon, he came upon an&nbsp;
incredibly strange containment&nbsp;

02:03:49.200 --> 02:03:51.780
chamber that quickly caught his eye: It looked

02:03:51.780 --> 02:03:55.380
like a huge, metal cube, with no way in or
out.

02:03:55.380 --> 02:03:57.060
Not even a seam.

02:03:57.060 --> 02:04:01.440
Whatever was trapped in there, the Old Man
thought, it must be something that even the

02:04:01.440 --> 02:04:05.820
fools at the SCP Foundation really, truly
wanted nothing to do with.

02:04:05.820 --> 02:04:12.420
But even more so than the strange sight, it
was the sound that drew the Old Man in, tempting

02:04:12.420 --> 02:04:13.920
him like a siren song.

02:04:13.920 --> 02:04:15.240
It was weeping.

02:04:15.240 --> 02:04:19.920
A soft, sobbing keen that betrayed a bottomless
well of misery.

02:04:19.920 --> 02:04:23.820
Whatever was making that crying sound had
known true despair.

02:04:23.820 --> 02:04:28.200
Or, whatever passes for true despair before
you’ve had a close encounter with the Old

02:04:28.200 --> 02:04:29.820
Man in his pocket dimension.

02:04:29.820 --> 02:04:36.120
The Old Man grinned, and thought, “Oh, sonny,
I’ll give you something to really cry about...”

02:04:36.120 --> 02:04:40.200
He stepped forward and phased his way into
the metal cube.

02:04:40.200 --> 02:04:41.880
The creature was in the corner.

02:04:41.880 --> 02:04:48.000
In contrast to that stupid lizard, this monster
was pleasingly humanoid, with, presumably,

02:04:48.000 --> 02:04:50.100
all the familiar pain points.

02:04:50.100 --> 02:04:51.360
Splendid.

02:04:51.360 --> 02:04:56.760
The only real difference was color and proportion:
It was far longer and ganglier than an average

02:04:56.760 --> 02:05:00.240
human, with greyish skin and no hair anywhere
on its body.

02:05:00.240 --> 02:05:03.120
Still, it was something the Old Man could
work with.

02:05:03.120 --> 02:05:07.860
The creature didn’t even seem to register
his presence, it just sat crumpled in the

02:05:07.860 --> 02:05:09.060
corner, weeping.

02:05:09.060 --> 02:05:14.100
Still grinning his lunatic grin, the Old Man
approached and rested a moist hand on the

02:05:14.100 --> 02:05:14.880
creature’s shoulder.

02:05:14.880 --> 02:05:18.480
Its pale skin began to fizzle from the intruder’s
corrosive touch.

02:05:18.480 --> 02:05:23.340
A pool of vile sludge began spreading out
beneath his feet until the other creature

02:05:23.340 --> 02:05:24.480
was encompassed too.

02:05:24.480 --> 02:05:30.360
Little by little, they sank into the Old Man’s
favorite place out of the world: The Pocket

02:05:30.360 --> 02:05:37.020
Dimension, his own personal, eternal, infinite
dungeon, where the only laws are pain and

02:05:37.020 --> 02:05:40.020
terror for all but the Old Man himself.

02:05:40.020 --> 02:05:46.080
Little did he know, this time, the scores
may be a little more even on that front.

02:05:46.080 --> 02:05:51.060
There they both stood, in that terrible labyrinth
of hallways and chambers where the Old Man

02:05:51.060 --> 02:05:56.160
had made countless lives infinitely worse
than any terrible afterlife that could follow

02:05:56.160 --> 02:05:59.700
them, and still, this creature didn’t seem
to change its reaction.

02:05:59.700 --> 02:06:01.920
He was almost irritated by it.

02:06:01.920 --> 02:06:06.720
And what’s more, this whole time, the creature
hadn’t even turned to look at him.

02:06:06.720 --> 02:06:08.700
He’d have to fix that, too.

02:06:08.700 --> 02:06:13.860
The Old Man, with his shocking strength, gripped
the monster’s shoulder and whipped him around,

02:06:13.860 --> 02:06:18.780
staring into his face, bearing his black eyes
and manic smile.

02:06:18.780 --> 02:06:21.960
But he couldn’t help find what looked back
at him...

02:06:21.960 --> 02:06:23.580
Disconcerting.

02:06:23.580 --> 02:06:25.740
Its eyes were blank and white.

02:06:25.740 --> 02:06:31.860
Its face, stretched and warped into a permanent
grimace, like Munch’s old painting, The

02:06:31.860 --> 02:06:32.340
Scream.

02:06:32.340 --> 02:06:36.840
And the second the Old Man laid eyes on it,
that’s exactly what it did.

02:06:36.840 --> 02:06:41.700
Its gaping mouth let out the most terrible
wail the Old Man had ever heard in all his

02:06:41.700 --> 02:06:42.780
years of torturing.

02:06:42.780 --> 02:06:46.020
It was a scream of unknowable sadness and
fear.

02:06:46.020 --> 02:06:49.800
But somehow, the Old Man sensed that this
creature didn’t fear him.

02:06:49.800 --> 02:06:51.840
It feared itself.

02:06:51.840 --> 02:06:53.760
Feared what it was about to do.

02:06:53.760 --> 02:06:57.240
What the hell had he dragged down here with
him?

02:06:57.240 --> 02:07:02.340
Without warning, the terrible wails soon gave
way to sudden and ferocious aggression - Just

02:07:02.340 --> 02:07:06.420
like the Old Man had dealt out to a group
of Foundation guards and Mobile Task Force

02:07:06.420 --> 02:07:07.980
agents earlier that same night.

02:07:07.980 --> 02:07:13.680
It was only out of a mix of pure luck and
his own finely-tuned animal instinct that

02:07:13.680 --> 02:07:19.260
the Old Man was able to dodge the first strike
from the creature’s long, lithe forelimb.

02:07:19.260 --> 02:07:20.580
He couldn’t believe it.

02:07:20.580 --> 02:07:26.040
They were in his special place, and this victim
of his had the audacity to fight back?

02:07:26.040 --> 02:07:28.740
Well, he’d soon put this monster in its
place...

02:07:28.740 --> 02:07:33.600
As the monster continued roaring and swiping
for him, the Old Man simply walked backwards

02:07:33.600 --> 02:07:37.380
into a nearby wall, disappearing into a black
stain.

02:07:37.380 --> 02:07:43.320
Of course, the monster - better known to the
SCP Foundation As SCP-096, or The Shy Guy,

02:07:43.320 --> 02:07:45.540
simply crashed through the wall to give chase.

02:07:45.540 --> 02:07:47.040
But on the other side...

02:07:47.040 --> 02:07:48.780
Just another wall.

02:07:48.780 --> 02:07:50.100
He’d disappeared.

02:07:50.100 --> 02:07:50.880
How?

02:07:50.880 --> 02:07:55.140
Typically, when something sees the face of
the Shy Guy, he immediately has a kind of

02:07:55.140 --> 02:07:56.760
intuitive connection to them.

02:07:56.760 --> 02:08:03.000
He can sense their presence, it’s burned
into his mind, a homing beacon, an unstoppable

02:08:03.000 --> 02:08:04.080
imperative.

02:08:04.080 --> 02:08:07.020
He knows exactly where they are and how to
get to them.

02:08:07.020 --> 02:08:09.420
But nothing about this situation was typical.

02:08:09.420 --> 02:08:15.060
The Shy Guy felt all the murderous rage he
normally did, but the rest was just static.

02:08:15.060 --> 02:08:18.240
Where was the life he needed to abolish hiding?

02:08:18.240 --> 02:08:23.400
Suddenly, the Old Man emerged behind him,
and forced his hand into the Shy Guy’s back.

02:08:23.400 --> 02:08:27.780
His fist melted straight through flesh and
skin, causing the creature to let out another

02:08:27.780 --> 02:08:32.400
terrible wail, but as could be expected, they
didn’t leave a scratch on the Shy Guy’s

02:08:32.400 --> 02:08:33.900
indestructible skeleton.

02:08:33.900 --> 02:08:38.040
The Shy Guy pulled a one-eighty and swung
for the Old Man again, but he was already

02:08:38.040 --> 02:08:38.400
gone.

02:08:38.400 --> 02:08:43.260
Here in the Pocket Dimension, the Old Man
had the ultimate home-turf advantage.

02:08:43.260 --> 02:08:45.900
Here, space and time were his playthings.

02:08:45.900 --> 02:08:51.180
In the infinite twisting, labyrinthine walls
of the Pocket Dimension, the Old Man wasn’t

02:08:51.180 --> 02:08:53.760
just the Devil, he was God, too.

02:08:53.760 --> 02:08:57.960
But it would take far more than some cheap
tricks and bodily harm to dissuade the Shy

02:08:57.960 --> 02:08:59.760
Guy from his sole purpose in life.

02:08:59.760 --> 02:09:04.800
It began running through the Old Man’s personal
maze like a crazed lab rat, tearing through

02:09:04.800 --> 02:09:08.340
wall after wall, forever searching for the
monster that trapped it here.

02:09:08.340 --> 02:09:13.680
It would see the smiling face of that rotten
old man every so often, the flash of black

02:09:13.680 --> 02:09:18.600
eyes and dirty teeth in shadows, but every
time it tried to strike the shadows, there

02:09:18.600 --> 02:09:19.620
would be nothing there.

02:09:19.620 --> 02:09:24.240
And every time he got a chance, the Old Man
would emerge from the shadows behind the Shy

02:09:24.240 --> 02:09:29.220
Guy to strike back, clawing into his flesh
and vanishing again before the trapped beast

02:09:29.220 --> 02:09:30.000
could retaliate.

02:09:30.000 --> 02:09:36.780
The nasty old sadist was now starting to have
some fun: It was a reflex test, a classic

02:09:36.780 --> 02:09:38.880
game of high-stakes Whack A Mole.

02:09:38.880 --> 02:09:43.920
And every time the creature failed to strike
him, its distress only seemed to grow.

02:09:43.920 --> 02:09:46.560
This was perfectly fine for the Old Man.

02:09:46.560 --> 02:09:51.480
After all, he enjoyed a little mental torment
as much as he loved physical torture, and

02:09:51.480 --> 02:09:55.140
a human would have died or given in to despair
long before now.

02:09:55.140 --> 02:10:00.300
This monster’s wonderful mix of resilience
and pain made it a great chew toy.

02:10:00.300 --> 02:10:05.400
The Old Man kept up this routine, baiting
and striking, until something unanticipated

02:10:05.400 --> 02:10:05.880
happened.

02:10:05.880 --> 02:10:11.520
He appeared, waited for the swing, disappeared
as it approached, then reappeared behind the

02:10:11.520 --> 02:10:13.560
Shy Guy to deliver another painful strike.

02:10:13.560 --> 02:10:18.240
This time, however, it seemed his victim had
anticipated his pattern.

02:10:18.240 --> 02:10:22.200
Before he could hit the creature, the Shy
Guy turned, quicker than anything the Old

02:10:22.200 --> 02:10:27.420
Man had ever seen, and hit him with such force
he flew through several walls in his own maze,

02:10:27.420 --> 02:10:28.980
shattering them as he went.

02:10:28.980 --> 02:10:34.500
He hit the ground, stunned by the fact the
Shy Guy had actually landed a hit, but he

02:10:34.500 --> 02:10:35.100
wasn’t done.

02:10:35.100 --> 02:10:37.920
The shrieking monster came bounding after
him.

02:10:37.920 --> 02:10:43.620
Before the Old Man could stand, it struck
him again, and again, and again, pounding

02:10:43.620 --> 02:10:47.520
his vile body into the ground, which cracked
and crumbled.

02:10:47.520 --> 02:10:51.000
The Old Man sneered and sank into a puddle
of his mucus on the ground.

02:10:51.000 --> 02:10:53.040
The Old Man was gone again.

02:10:53.040 --> 02:10:59.880
SCP-096 screamed in a mix of rage, despair,
and frustration, so consumed with its hunt

02:10:59.880 --> 02:11:03.420
that it didn’t notice the pool of corrosive
mucus was slowly growing.

02:11:03.420 --> 02:11:07.860
It began to rise, bubbling and hissing at
SCP-096’s feet.

02:11:07.860 --> 02:11:13.920
Getting deeper and deeper and deeper, as the
walls rose taller and taller around it.

02:11:13.920 --> 02:11:19.440
The creature wailed, until a tide of the awful
stuff reached its face and cascaded down its

02:11:19.440 --> 02:11:21.840
throat, melting all the organs within.

02:11:21.840 --> 02:11:27.780
As the creature was fully submerged, SCP-106
manifested atop the wall, and looked down,

02:11:27.780 --> 02:11:29.220
smiling spitefully.

02:11:29.220 --> 02:11:36.660
He thought, oh, you may think you had me there,
boy-o, but in the end, I always win.

02:11:36.660 --> 02:11:39.840
Nothing could survive that!

02:11:39.840 --> 02:11:43.020
And yet, bubbles still rose from the slop
below.

02:11:43.020 --> 02:11:47.400
The Old Man gritted his rotten teeth and balled
his fists in quiet anger.

02:11:47.400 --> 02:11:53.400
Moments later, back in our dimension, a portal
opened in the ground of SCP-096’s containment

02:11:53.400 --> 02:11:53.820
chamber.

02:11:53.820 --> 02:12:01.200
It vomited out SCP-096’s skeleton, smothered
in bubbling black goo, and SCP-106.

02:12:01.200 --> 02:12:07.020
096 would probably be fully regenerated within
a couple of hours - and if 682’s encounter

02:12:07.020 --> 02:12:12.240
with him was anything to go by, probably extremely
unwilling to interface with the Old Man.

02:12:12.240 --> 02:12:16.620
And the Old Man himself, who couldn’t help
but feel a little humiliated by the whole

02:12:16.620 --> 02:12:19.800
experience, was glad the two of them would
probably never meet again.

02:12:19.800 --> 02:12:25.140
He left the regenerating 096 and commenced
the walk of shame back to his containment

02:12:25.140 --> 02:12:25.620
chamber.

02:12:25.620 --> 02:12:30.660
They didn’t even need the femur breaker,
he just felt like having a sit-down and processing

02:12:30.660 --> 02:12:31.020
it all.

02:12:31.020 --> 02:12:35.460
Nobody else would ever know what happened
in the pocket dimension that day, and for

02:12:35.460 --> 02:12:38.160
the Old Man’s pride, that was probably for
the best.

02:12:38.160 --> 02:12:39.300
But he’d know.

02:12:39.300 --> 02:12:40.620
He’d always know.

02:12:40.620 --> 02:12:45.780
By the night’s end, he was sitting in his
containment chamber again, forlornly wondering,

02:12:45.780 --> 02:12:49.560
“Goddamn it, old-timer, have you lost your
edge?”

02:12:49.560 --> 02:12:55.140
A group of well-armed Mobile Task Force members&nbsp;
entered the containment chamber, armed to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:55.140 --> 02:13:01.020
teeth. Some faced the ground, droplets of sweat&nbsp;
dripping onto their visors, some wore Dr. Dan’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:01.020 --> 02:13:07.020
infamous SCRAMBLE goggles, and one particularly&nbsp;
brave member of personnel wielded only a bag.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:07.020 --> 02:13:11.700
The room they were currently standing in was&nbsp;
perhaps the most dangerous place in the world.

02:13:11.700 --> 02:13:12.600
Why?

02:13:12.600 --> 02:13:18.240
Because it contained two terrifying&nbsp;
titans of the anomalous world: SCP-682,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:18.240 --> 02:13:25.560
the infinitely hateful Hard to Destroy Reptile,&nbsp;
and SCP-096, the Shy Guy, the ultimate mindless&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:25.560 --> 02:13:30.660
killing machine. And if the very idea of having&nbsp;
them in the same place doesn’t feel risky enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:30.660 --> 02:13:36.120
this deadly duo had just spent 27 hours in&nbsp;
one of the most brutal brawls imaginable.

02:13:36.120 --> 02:13:42.120
And incidentally, fights like this are exactly&nbsp;
why we’re here today. We cover a lot of SCPs on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:42.120 --> 02:13:46.380
this channel, and we’ve seen the way some of the&nbsp;
more dangerous anomalies out there can tear their&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:46.380 --> 02:13:51.660
way through civilians and trained Foundation MTF&nbsp;
operatives with ease. But how do they fare against&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:51.660 --> 02:13:58.020
monsters, maniacs, and reality benders their own&nbsp;
size? With the help of the SCP Explained audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:58.020 --> 02:14:03.240
we’re going to explore theoretical fights between&nbsp;
some of the anomalous world’s biggest badasses.

02:14:03.240 --> 02:14:08.580
And who better to get this show on the road than&nbsp;
two of the most iconic and deadly monsters that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:08.580 --> 02:14:13.140
haunt the halls of a Foundation containment&nbsp;
site near you? The Hard to Destroy Reptile&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:13.140 --> 02:14:17.700
and the Shy Guy are household names for&nbsp;
anyone familiar with the SCP Foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:17.700 --> 02:14:21.840
and before we get to the gruesome results&nbsp;
of their first fight - and your theories&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:21.840 --> 02:14:26.400
about how a potential rematch might play out&nbsp;
- let’s get to know the combat prowess of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:26.400 --> 02:14:31.260
pair of nasty customers. What kind of damage&nbsp;
are they capable of taking and inflicting?

02:14:31.260 --> 02:14:33.900
Spoiler Alert: It’s a hell of a lot.

02:14:33.900 --> 02:14:40.140
In this corner, we have SCP-682: A monster&nbsp;
whose ability to survive seemingly anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:40.140 --> 02:14:44.460
is only equaled by its terrible attitude&nbsp;
toward pretty much everyone and everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:44.460 --> 02:14:49.560
around it. Though to be fair, you’d probably&nbsp;
be a little grumpy too if the SCP Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:49.560 --> 02:14:53.580
was constantly trying to murder you in&nbsp;
different ways rather than just keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:53.580 --> 02:14:58.920
you contained. 682’s basic offensive power&nbsp;
set involves impressive strength, speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:58.920 --> 02:15:05.580
intelligence, and the aid of its deadly fangs,&nbsp;
claws, and tail. However, 682 is considerably&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:05.580 --> 02:15:09.660
stronger after it’s just survived and&nbsp;
regenerated from a termination attempt.

02:15:09.660 --> 02:15:17.100
That’s because the true combat asset of 682 is and&nbsp;
always has been its adaptability. Specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:17.100 --> 02:15:22.680
if some kind of attack brings 682 into a state&nbsp;
of near-death but doesn’t finish the job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:22.680 --> 02:15:27.360
682 will generally either develop a&nbsp;
perfect defense or counter-offensive for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:27.360 --> 02:15:31.860
that attack or somehow add a version&nbsp;
of the attack to its own arsenal.

02:15:31.860 --> 02:15:36.540
This has ranged from super-pressurized jets&nbsp;
of its own blood, dangerously radioactive&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:36.540 --> 02:15:42.120
banana beams fired out of its mouth, and even&nbsp;
an incapacitating laugher blast gained from an&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:42.120 --> 02:15:47.700
encounter with the adorable SCP-999.&nbsp;
It’s an absolute swiss army knife of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:47.700 --> 02:15:51.960
dangerous anomalous powers and abilities -&nbsp;
And if you need any further proof of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:51.960 --> 02:15:57.180
check out our multiple videos on all the ludicrous&nbsp;
failed attempts to kill this nightmare lizard.

02:15:57.180 --> 02:16:03.840
And in this corner, we’ve got SCP-096, the&nbsp;
Shy Guy. While 682 is freakishly adaptable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:03.840 --> 02:16:10.200
in most situations, SCP-096 is pretty much&nbsp;
unstoppable. This long, gangly creep will&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:10.200 --> 02:16:15.840
murder anyone who sees even the tiniest glimpse&nbsp;
of its face with such ferocity that not even a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:15.840 --> 02:16:20.580
trace of the body will remain. It does this&nbsp;
by entering what many call its “Rage State”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:20.580 --> 02:16:26.040
activated by the sudden immense distress of&nbsp;
its face being seen. When it enters this state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:26.040 --> 02:16:31.680
the SCP Foundation has only one prerogative:&nbsp;
Get themselves and anyone else out of its way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:31.680 --> 02:16:35.160
because you will not stop this&nbsp;
thing until it’s killed its target.

02:16:35.160 --> 02:16:40.920
All the SCP Foundation has ever been able to do is&nbsp;
wait until after the target has been obliterated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:40.920 --> 02:16:46.740
and then come in to bag its head when it enters&nbsp;
its docile state. Almost nothing else has even&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:46.740 --> 02:16:51.240
successfully slowed it down. While they&nbsp;
have been capable of damaging its flesh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:51.240 --> 02:16:55.979
the monster’s skeleton seems to be utterly&nbsp;
indestructible, and its damaged flesh has&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:55.979 --> 02:17:00.660
proven consistently able to grow back. The&nbsp;
monster seems to be immune to everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:00.660 --> 02:17:06.180
from pain to cold, and moves at such a speed that&nbsp;
it’s pretty much impossible to escape for long.

02:17:06.180 --> 02:17:10.920
So when you factor in an indestructible&nbsp;
body, immense strength, immense speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:10.920 --> 02:17:17.040
and an unstoppable urge to kill anyone who sees&nbsp;
its face, you’re looking at a formidable opponent.

02:17:17.040 --> 02:17:22.740
It’s only natural that the Foundation would&nbsp;
want to use 096 in their 682 cross tests, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:22.740 --> 02:17:27.420
what exactly happened when the Mobile Task Force&nbsp;
entered the room after their first epic battle?&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:27.420 --> 02:17:30.480
What did they find inside the&nbsp;
chamber in the grisly aftermath?

02:17:30.479 --> 02:17:37.859
SCP-096 lay crumpled in the corner, wheezing,&nbsp;
scarred, covered in its own blood. SCP-682 was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:37.859 --> 02:17:42.599
barely recognizable - SCP-096 had reduced&nbsp;
it to a quarter of its original size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:42.600 --> 02:17:48.900
the rest having been utterly torn to shreds. Both&nbsp;
had narrowly survived the encounter, but it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:48.899 --> 02:17:55.439
an epic battle neither of them would ever forget&nbsp;
- Though this was especially true for SCP-096.

02:17:55.439 --> 02:18:01.439
You see, as we alluded to earlier, SCP-682&nbsp;
is used to taking a kicking at this point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:01.439 --> 02:18:05.879
It hasn’t ever truly been killed,&nbsp;
but it’s been beaten, eaten, burned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:05.880 --> 02:18:10.859
zapped, and smashed within an inch of its&nbsp;
life more times than you can count. 096,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:10.859 --> 02:18:15.059
on the other hand, was not used to not&nbsp;
being able to utterly eliminate its&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:15.060 --> 02:18:20.640
prey. While it’s impossible to speculate&nbsp;
what’s going on inside SCP-096’s mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:20.640 --> 02:18:27.420
all evidence points towards getting a draw in this&nbsp;
battle, seriously damaging SCP-096’s confidence.

02:18:27.420 --> 02:18:33.120
Now, every time Foundation personnel tries&nbsp;
to bring 096 into 682’s containment chamber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:33.120 --> 02:18:40.019
he begins crying and scratching at his own face.&nbsp;
Why? Is he scared of 682? Is he humiliated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:40.020 --> 02:18:45.720
thinking of the fight he wasn’t able to win?&nbsp;
Or is the very idea of someone seeing his face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:45.720 --> 02:18:51.480
and him not being able to immediately kill&nbsp;
them, just too unbearably stressful? Seeing as&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:51.479 --> 02:18:56.459
the Shy Guy’s mouth is only good for two things&nbsp;
- and neither of them are talking - we’ll never&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:56.460 --> 02:19:01.439
truly know. But either way, it’s clear&nbsp;
that 096 has no interest in a rematch…

02:19:01.439 --> 02:19:04.320
...But what if that changed?

02:19:04.320 --> 02:19:10.320
What if 096 realized that it was wasting&nbsp;
its potential. It managed to get SCP-682&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:10.320 --> 02:19:14.280
three-quarters of the way to death last time,&nbsp;
what could stop it from getting that last&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:14.280 --> 02:19:20.160
quarter? Sure, nothing has ever killed SCP-682&nbsp;
before, but there’s a first time for everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:20.160 --> 02:19:23.700
right? Maybe 096 would have a&nbsp;
Rocky-style training montage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:23.700 --> 02:19:28.740
where he rebuilds his confidence and becomes&nbsp;
the underdog he was always meant to be. He’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:28.740 --> 02:19:32.880
walk right up to that big, nasty reptile,&nbsp;
and give him the rematch of the century.

02:19:32.880 --> 02:19:34.320
What would happen then?

02:19:34.320 --> 02:19:40.679
Now, we give the floor to you, the loyal viewers&nbsp;
of SCP Explained. Here are some of your thoughts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:40.680 --> 02:19:45.180
theories, and opinions on a potential&nbsp;
historic rematch between these two creeps.

02:19:45.180 --> 02:19:47.400
Let’s get ready to rumble!

02:19:53.520 --> 02:19:58.621
PinguWithAnAxe said, “If 682's adaptability&nbsp;
is based around his ability to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:58.620 --> 02:20:03.000
countermeasures to any damage inflicted&nbsp;
(or in the case of 173, a countermeasure&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:03.000 --> 02:20:08.100
to the cause of said damage) I imagine 682&nbsp;
would turn his skin entirely reflective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:08.100 --> 02:20:12.600
causing 096 to see his own face and the&nbsp;
feedback loop would break his mind.”

02:20:13.439 --> 02:20:19.500
Interesting. Whether this would work is all&nbsp;
dependent on whether SCP-096 can actually see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:19.500 --> 02:20:24.060
or if it is merely capable of psychically&nbsp;
intuiting when someone else sees its face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:24.060 --> 02:20:30.360
If the latter is true, then this particular&nbsp;
adaptation wouldn’t do much good for SCP-682.

02:20:30.359 --> 02:20:34.740
Random User said, “If this is after&nbsp;
their first cross test and they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:34.740 --> 02:20:37.559
forced to interact again, they&nbsp;
wouldn’t attempt to fight each&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:37.560 --> 02:20:41.220
other. I feel like they’d form a truce&nbsp;
and take out the researchers on-site.”

02:20:41.880 --> 02:20:47.160
This would be one of the worst-case scenarios.&nbsp;
Whether even the SCP Foundation could handle&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:47.160 --> 02:20:52.620
a simultaneous 682 and 096 breach without&nbsp;
bringing in Hammer Down is an open question.

02:20:52.620 --> 02:20:58.620
Ramon Voncken said, “Simple, after the first&nbsp;
encounter, when 096 stopped attacking 682,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:58.620 --> 02:21:02.760
682 will grow eyes that filter&nbsp;
out 096s face the next time.”

02:21:02.760 --> 02:21:07.200
Organic SCRAMBLE goggles. Dr. Dan&nbsp;
would be fuming with jealousy.

02:21:07.200 --> 02:21:11.880
Koopsader said, “I really want&nbsp;
SCP-096 to win so I’m sending&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:11.880 --> 02:21:16.020
him a blind personal trainer so he&nbsp;
can beat the snot out of SCP-682.”

02:21:16.020 --> 02:21:22.800
This is exactly the kind of help SCP-096 needs.&nbsp;
He’s got the rage, endurance, and strength, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:22.800 --> 02:21:27.480
a little help getting his form and technique in&nbsp;
order, he’d be an even more formidable opponent.

02:21:27.479 --> 02:21:32.639
Kytoe Ryu said, “I'd like to imagine&nbsp;
096, after playing with 682 for so long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:32.640 --> 02:21:39.000
would try to eat 682 in a vain attempt to kill&nbsp;
it, only to gain 682’s abilities, and vice versa.”

02:21:39.000 --> 02:21:44.700
Either of these options would be&nbsp;
horrifying. SCP-096 with 682’s abilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:44.700 --> 02:21:50.580
or 682 with 096’s abilities, would likely&nbsp;
be a truly perfect killing machine.

02:21:52.439 --> 02:21:55.919
Autumn said, “What If they just shared a&nbsp;
nice cold drink while playing Mario Kart?&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:55.920 --> 02:21:59.280
I’m sure 096 would be the white&nbsp;
shy guy character in every race.”

02:21:59.280 --> 02:22:03.300
Personally, we love this idea, and&nbsp;
we think SCP-682 would go for Bowser.

02:22:05.220 --> 02:22:09.840
Ibraheem Temur said, “682 would win&nbsp;
eventually. It’s way more intelligent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:09.840 --> 02:22:14.580
adaptive, and overall malicious&nbsp;
while 096 acts just on instinct.”

02:22:14.580 --> 02:22:20.399
This is an undeniably good point. What 682&nbsp;
lacks in sheer savagery, it makes up for in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:20.399 --> 02:22:24.719
its ruthless, calculating intelligence.&nbsp;
Could this give it the edge over 096?

02:22:24.720 --> 02:22:28.860
Monomoy Mukherjee said, “That fight&nbsp;
will be interesting because both of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:28.859 --> 02:22:31.439
them now know how they attack&nbsp;
and what their attacks are.”

02:22:31.439 --> 02:22:37.019
Nice segue. Due to the relatively mindless&nbsp;
nature of 096, as far as we’re aware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:37.020 --> 02:22:41.460
it’s probably unable to learn&nbsp;
from experience. 682, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:41.460 --> 02:22:45.359
would have the advantage of knowing&nbsp;
how 096 operates in the second battle.

02:22:45.359 --> 02:22:49.139
Devan Smith said, “As much as I'd&nbsp;
like to see how this would play out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:49.140 --> 02:22:55.260
I imagine 096 can't manage more than other&nbsp;
SCP's have in the past. Not to downplay him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:55.260 --> 02:22:58.680
but he might need some extra friends&nbsp;
to do a whole lot more damage.”

02:22:58.680 --> 02:23:03.660
It’s undeniable that going in with tag team&nbsp;
partners would better 096’s chances of taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:03.660 --> 02:23:09.720
the lizard down. However, due to SCP-096’s&nbsp;
shyness, and tendency to kill everyone and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:09.720 --> 02:23:14.281
everything that looks at him, he doesn’t&nbsp;
have many friends looking to volunteer.

02:23:14.280 --> 02:23:19.200
Nyan Tails said, “096 will cry in the&nbsp;
corner while 682 starts attacking it&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:19.200 --> 02:23:22.800
eventually it gets confused that&nbsp;
096 isn't fighting back after an&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:22.800 --> 02:23:25.920
hour and begins to adapt to it&nbsp;
and becomes docile forever.”

02:23:26.580 --> 02:23:31.680
Look, while it’s unlikely that this would&nbsp;
happen, it’s certainly nice to imagine, isn’t it?

02:23:31.680 --> 02:23:35.701
Adam Britton said, “Narratively it&nbsp;
needs to conclude with a reason to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:35.700 --> 02:23:40.260
never try it again. So I'm going to say&nbsp;
that 682 will telepathically project the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:40.260 --> 02:23:44.281
image of 096 into the site staff&nbsp;
or the entire human pychospace.”

02:23:44.280 --> 02:23:49.859
Yikes. This is a possibility&nbsp;
so terrifying - And honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:49.859 --> 02:23:54.059
plausible - that it’s making us regret&nbsp;
wanting to see this rematch altogether.

02:23:54.060 --> 02:23:58.980
Chaos Insurgency said, “I think 682 would just&nbsp;
think "For damn’s sake, just leave me alone!"

02:23:58.979 --> 02:24:03.540
Given he’s been through more termination&nbsp;
attempts than SCP-5031 can count on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:03.540 --> 02:24:06.720
its fingers, who could blame him&nbsp;
for getting a little frustrated?

02:24:06.720 --> 02:24:12.241
Michael Porter said, “I think that 682 and 096&nbsp;
would take each other to court for aggravated&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:12.240 --> 02:24:17.040
assault against the other. Either one of them will&nbsp;
get sentenced to death and will unsurprisingly not&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:17.040 --> 02:24:21.899
die or the 2 team up to kill the entire courtroom&nbsp;
and they walk out as a new serial killing team.”

02:24:21.899 --> 02:24:25.439
The real question is what kind of lawyers&nbsp;
would represent a pair of murderous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:25.439 --> 02:24:28.259
anomalous monsters in court. Better call Saul.

02:24:28.260 --> 02:24:31.980
Ari Wertman said, “Maybe if we&nbsp;
try being nice to them for once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:31.979 --> 02:24:36.359
we can offer them a cup of tea and&nbsp;
get them both a therapist. 682 will&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:36.359 --> 02:24:39.960
talk all his hate away and then realize&nbsp;
his wrongdoing and become an activist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:39.960 --> 02:24:45.359
and 096 would become very confident about his&nbsp;
face and become a model. There problem solved.”

02:24:45.359 --> 02:24:49.019
Hey, when people strive towards&nbsp;
self-improvement, we’re all winners.

02:24:49.020 --> 02:24:55.741
But we don’t know if we can say the same for 682&nbsp;
and 096. Even if this rematch ever did happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:55.740 --> 02:25:00.599
perhaps it’d just be another draw - An&nbsp;
unstoppable force against an immovable&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:00.600 --> 02:25:05.460
object. But other match-ups may be more&nbsp;
clear-cut. What kind of Vs battles would&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:05.460 --> 02:25:09.840
you like to see us cover in the future? What&nbsp;
SCPs would you really love to see duke it out?

02:25:09.840 --> 02:25:13.680
Let us know in the comments, and&nbsp;
keep an eye on the community posts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:13.680 --> 02:25:17.571
in order to throw in your anomalous&nbsp;
two cents for our next Vs battle!

02:25:17.571 --> 02:25:17.640
A person is chased down a dark
hallway by a monster with large claws.]

02:25:17.640 --> 02:25:21.360
What does a monster look like to you?

02:25:21.359 --> 02:25:27.839
What figures slither and claw their way into
your nightmares, chasing you down endless

02:25:27.840 --> 02:25:33.300
halls and stalking you through the dark until
you wake up screaming?

02:25:33.300 --> 02:25:40.380
Maybe you imagine something tall and lean,
bony arms reaching for you from atop impossibly

02:25:40.380 --> 02:25:45.240
long, slender legs, its featureless face showing
no mercy

02:25:45.240 --> 02:25:51.660
Maybe you think of a man in a striped sweater
with knives for fingers , or a serial killer

02:25:51.660 --> 02:25:54.420
in a hockey mask, wielding a machete.

02:25:54.420 --> 02:26:01.380
Maybe it’s something more inhuman, a cosmic
horror of tentacles and eyes that can see

02:26:01.380 --> 02:26:02.880
into your very soul.

02:26:02.880 --> 02:26:08.160
You probably don’t think of something with
no arms, no legs, no body at all.

02:26:08.160 --> 02:26:09.599
Just a face.

02:26:09.600 --> 02:26:11.760
What’s so scary about that?

02:26:11.760 --> 02:26:17.040
A face can’t run after you, can’t grab
you by the ankles and pull you under the bed.

02:26:17.040 --> 02:26:20.040
A face can only look.

02:26:20.040 --> 02:26:25.859
It may be unsettling to behold, it might send
a chill down your spine, but the worst it

02:26:25.859 --> 02:26:28.500
can do is make you a little uncomfortable.

02:26:28.500 --> 02:26:34.319
And if you can’t stand it any longer, you
could always just close your eyes, or walk

02:26:34.319 --> 02:26:36.059
away and be done with it.

02:26:36.060 --> 02:26:36.720
Right?

02:26:36.720 --> 02:26:41.760
If that’s what you think, if you don’t
believe in monsters that can hurt you without

02:26:41.760 --> 02:26:48.480
lifting a finger, then you’re the type to
fall victim to a very special, very intelligent

02:26:48.479 --> 02:26:49.679
mask.

02:26:49.680 --> 02:26:56.520
In the hallowed halls of the SCP Foundation,
there is a containment cell outfitted with

02:26:56.520 --> 02:27:02.041
a hermetically sealed glass case, surrounded
by steel, iron, and lead.

02:27:02.040 --> 02:27:06.240
There are guards posted outside, along with
a trained psychologist.

02:27:06.240 --> 02:27:12.599
If you are ever brave, and foolish, enough
to enter that room, you’ll see a simple

02:27:12.600 --> 02:27:18.781
white porcelain comedy mask, with a peculiar
black substance leaking from its eyes and

02:27:18.780 --> 02:27:19.080
mouth.

02:27:19.080 --> 02:27:26.040
Wherever this slime touches, surfaces begin
to corrode, to rot away into puddles of black

02:27:26.040 --> 02:27:26.519
liquid.

02:27:26.520 --> 02:27:31.380
You might notice the same liquid trickling
slowly down the walls of the room, corrupting

02:27:31.380 --> 02:27:32.819
everything in its path.

02:27:32.819 --> 02:27:39.420
As unsettling of a sight as it is, if you
approach the mask to take a closer look, you

02:27:39.420 --> 02:27:45.540
will find yourself overcome by an intense,
nearly irresistible urge to pick it up, and

02:27:45.540 --> 02:27:46.380
put it on.

02:27:46.380 --> 02:27:53.280
To wear it, to let it consume you from the
inside out and puppet your body while your

02:27:53.280 --> 02:27:56.219
brain simply…turns off.

02:27:56.220 --> 02:28:00.660
Like an extinguished flame, you’ll simply
be gone.

02:28:00.660 --> 02:28:03.899
Then, who knows what the mask will do?

02:28:03.899 --> 02:28:07.080
It won’t be your concern anymore, that’s
for sure.

02:28:07.080 --> 02:28:11.399
But thankfully, you haven’t gone into the
room with the porcelain mask.

02:28:11.399 --> 02:28:14.099
You haven’t let it cast its spell on you.

02:28:14.100 --> 02:28:15.600
Not yet, at least.

02:28:15.600 --> 02:28:21.600
It’s waiting for you there, in the room
with black slime oozing down its walls.

02:28:21.600 --> 02:28:26.640
And it will wait patiently, for as long as
it needs to.

02:28:26.640 --> 02:28:30.960
After all, what’s a mask without a face
to wear it?

02:28:30.960 --> 02:28:38.700
SCP-035, better known as the Possessive Mask,
sat in its containment cell, immobile as always.

02:28:38.700 --> 02:28:41.819
It didn’t really have much of a choice in
the matter.

02:28:41.819 --> 02:28:46.979
The Foundation had chosen, selfishly, to revoke
its host privileges.

02:28:46.979 --> 02:28:53.040
Once upon a time, they had offered it bodies
to choose from, mannequins, dummies, and wooden

02:28:53.040 --> 02:28:54.899
dolls it could adorn and pilot.

02:28:54.899 --> 02:28:58.620
They didn’t last as long as an organic,
living host, of course.

02:28:58.620 --> 02:29:00.660
But it was something.

02:29:00.660 --> 02:29:03.420
It was a taste of freedom.

02:29:08.939 --> 02:29:14.580
This dreadful new situation was almost enough
to make the mask want to change its

02:29:14.580 --> 02:29:20.760
expression from comedy to tragedy, but it
was determined to still find something to

02:29:20.760 --> 02:29:21.240
laugh about.

02:29:21.240 --> 02:29:27.359
Even without a body, trapped in this infernal
box, there had been some delicious opportunities

02:29:27.359 --> 02:29:28.559
for entertainment.

02:29:28.560 --> 02:29:31.440
Human minds were fragile things.

02:29:31.439 --> 02:29:37.979
The mask had learned this over the infinite
years of its life, if one could call it a

02:29:37.979 --> 02:29:38.160
life.

02:29:38.160 --> 02:29:44.340
Apply the right kind of pressure to the right
weak points, whisper an enticing word or two,

02:29:44.340 --> 02:29:50.580
find the right emotional wound to sprinkle
a pinch of salt into, and humans would buckle

02:29:50.580 --> 02:29:53.340
completely in almost no time at all.

02:29:53.340 --> 02:29:58.800
It had tried all sorts of methods since being
confined to this boring little box.

02:29:58.800 --> 02:30:05.040
Sometimes it would charm someone, pour honeyed
flattery into their heads until the person

02:30:05.040 --> 02:30:08.460
felt like the mask was a&nbsp;
dear friend, a confidante.

02:30:08.460 --> 02:30:13.680
Once suitable trust had been built up, the
mask could persuade the person to bring it

02:30:13.680 --> 02:30:17.760
a host, or perhaps even offer up themselves
in sacrifice.

02:30:17.760 --> 02:30:22.500
If flattery didn’t work, there were other
potent approaches to take.

02:30:22.500 --> 02:30:26.160
For a being without ears, the mask was a good
listener.

02:30:26.160 --> 02:30:32.580
It picked up on things that no human ever
could, the darkest secrets buried in a person’s

02:30:32.580 --> 02:30:33.000
mind.

02:30:33.000 --> 02:30:38.760
If it caught wind of something especially
juicy and ruinous, it could leverage that.

02:30:38.760 --> 02:30:45.120
Threaten to expose an affair, a crime, or
perhaps something even worse.

02:30:45.120 --> 02:30:47.880
Something unspeakable.

02:30:47.880 --> 02:30:55.140
If praise failed, and so did blackmail, there
was always good old supernatural intimidation.

02:30:55.140 --> 02:31:00.241
All the mask needed was for someone to be
left alone with it for a long enough period

02:31:00.240 --> 02:31:00.840
of time.

02:31:00.840 --> 02:31:09.000
Then, its invisible tendrils could snake out
into their defenseless mind, weaving and poking

02:31:09.000 --> 02:31:18.000
around, leaving a lingering sense of cold,
dread, of incomprehensible whispers in long-dead

02:31:18.000 --> 02:31:18.899
tongues.

02:31:18.899 --> 02:31:24.420
What a delight containment had been in the
early days, when the Foundation had not yet

02:31:24.420 --> 02:31:26.760
figured out the mask’s true capabilities.

02:31:26.760 --> 02:31:32.640
When they would leave security personnel with
weak wills and minimal training standing guard

02:31:32.640 --> 02:31:38.760
in the mask’s field of influence for hours
at a time, as the entity played with their

02:31:38.760 --> 02:31:41.602
thoughts and chipped away at their free will.

02:31:41.602 --> 02:31:45.000
It had almost escaped more than once, thanks
to the helpers it had been able to mold out

02:31:45.000 --> 02:31:46.500
of those hapless victims.

02:31:46.500 --> 02:31:52.020
They had been there to break open its case
and carry it to freedom.

02:31:52.020 --> 02:31:58.081
But every time, the other guards thwarted
the attempts, shooting its helpers and rendering

02:31:58.080 --> 02:31:59.519
them utterly useless.

02:31:59.520 --> 02:32:02.640
Then, the Foundation increased its security.

02:32:02.640 --> 02:32:07.320
Something about the unacceptably high suicide
and homicide rates among staff assigned to

02:32:07.319 --> 02:32:09.120
SCP-035.

02:32:09.120 --> 02:32:10.979
How utterly boring.

02:32:10.979 --> 02:32:13.859
How truly…pathetic.

02:32:13.859 --> 02:32:19.200
Still, the mask had found ways to occupy itself
even without any more playthings.

02:32:19.200 --> 02:32:25.800
It had grown stronger with its boredom, stretching
its influence beyond organic beings and into

02:32:25.800 --> 02:32:33.660
the very room itself, its evil enriching and
deepening like a fine wine in the depths of

02:32:33.660 --> 02:32:34.979
a cold cellar.

02:32:34.979 --> 02:32:40.559
Over the months, the walls of SCP-035’s
containment cell had begun to secrete the

02:32:40.560 --> 02:32:45.661
same black, slimy substance that would pour
from the mask’s eyes and mouth.

02:32:45.660 --> 02:32:51.300
The liquid dripped down the walls in deliberate
formations, patterns that became increasingly

02:32:51.300 --> 02:32:52.980
easy to recognize.

02:32:52.979 --> 02:33:00.419
Phrases in Italian, Latin, Ancient Greek,
all detailing ritual sacrifices and mutilations.

02:33:00.420 --> 02:33:07.740
The mask took time to describe the sacrificial
victims in great detail, borrowing identifying

02:33:07.740 --> 02:33:12.240
traits from various staff members that it
knew would read the translations.

02:33:12.240 --> 02:33:19.200
The walls were slick with blood and harrowing
imagery, and the glass case around the mask

02:33:19.200 --> 02:33:22.620
was growing more and more fragile by the day.

02:33:22.620 --> 02:33:27.120
Anyone within 10 meters of the mask could
feel this strength, too.

02:33:27.120 --> 02:33:30.540
They would leave the area&nbsp;
complaining of unintelligible&nbsp;

02:33:30.540 --> 02:33:34.859
whispers, of loud, cruel laughter, and a lingering

02:33:34.859 --> 02:33:37.799
sense of nearly insurmountable despair.

02:33:37.800 --> 02:33:43.920
It was as if they knew, on some level, that
no one was truly safe.

02:33:43.920 --> 02:33:47.700
Eventually, the mask would find a way to come
for them all.

02:33:47.700 --> 02:33:53.939
The glass was weakening, and soon the mere
thought of escape would make it shatter into

02:33:53.939 --> 02:33:54.540
pieces.

02:33:54.540 --> 02:34:01.500
Then, perhaps, the mask could finally get
its deepest desire: revenge.

02:34:01.500 --> 02:34:08.160
It wanted nothing more than to make the Foundation
pay for imprisoning it, for taking away its

02:34:08.160 --> 02:34:13.920
host privileges, for trying harder and harder
to contain the kind of power that should have

02:34:13.920 --> 02:34:16.440
had them falling to their knees in worship.

02:34:16.439 --> 02:34:22.080
The mask seethed with hatred day in and day
out.

02:34:22.080 --> 02:34:29.580
It had seen the crumbling of the Roman Empire,
the beheading of kings, the decimation of

02:34:29.580 --> 02:34:30.359
armies.

02:34:30.359 --> 02:34:37.139
It was not going to be captured by a bunch
of rats in lab coats without dire consequences

02:34:37.140 --> 02:34:38.460
befalling them.

02:34:38.460 --> 02:34:44.100
Maybe it couldn’t move from its prison cell
at the moment, but it also knew that it was

02:34:44.100 --> 02:34:50.640
surrounded on all sides by dangerous beasts
capable of reducing the site, and all who

02:34:50.640 --> 02:34:55.320
had dared to oppose the mask, to a pile of
smoldering rubble.

02:34:55.319 --> 02:35:01.080
If it could only find its way onto one of
their faces…it would show them all just

02:35:01.080 --> 02:35:02.519
what it was capable of.

02:35:02.520 --> 02:35:08.460
As the piercing sound of an alarm echoed down
the hall, the sound of screams and chaos following

02:35:08.460 --> 02:35:15.480
shortly after, the mask’s frowning face
curved into a broad, menacing smile.

02:35:15.479 --> 02:35:17.639
What was it that had escaped?

02:35:17.640 --> 02:35:18.900
The lizard, perhaps?

02:35:18.899 --> 02:35:20.759
The giant, grinning man?

02:35:20.760 --> 02:35:26.700
Whatever it was, it seemed that the action
was heading right toward 035’s containment

02:35:26.700 --> 02:35:27.000
cell.

02:35:27.000 --> 02:35:30.240
Perhaps today was the day.

02:35:30.240 --> 02:35:35.819
Finally, the SCP Foundation would fall.

02:35:35.819 --> 02:35:40.740
Outside the mask’s cell, Security Officer
Harper was running for dear life.

02:35:40.740 --> 02:35:46.019
Though his more rational mind knew he was
living on seconds, not even minutes, of borrowed

02:35:46.020 --> 02:35:51.180
time, his animal brain kept his legs pumping
- desperately trying to avoid the screaming,

02:35:51.180 --> 02:35:53.520
howling predator hot on his heels.

02:35:53.520 --> 02:35:58.741
Harper looked over his shoulder and screamed
as a long, white arm reached for him.

02:35:58.740 --> 02:36:05.939
SCP-096: The Shy Guy, its tooth-lined jaw
hanging low and foaming with spittle.

02:36:05.939 --> 02:36:12.299
That face, that terrible, terrible face - an
absolute death sentence to all who saw it.

02:36:12.300 --> 02:36:17.760
He’d seen what he thought was a crack in
the otherwise perfect seal of 096’s containment

02:36:17.760 --> 02:36:21.120
chamber, but it could have just as easily
been a trick of the light.

02:36:21.120 --> 02:36:25.200
Not even thinking, he’d stepped forward
and looked at the vulnerability in the chamber.

02:36:25.200 --> 02:36:31.380
All it took was one misplaced ray of light,
and he made out the vague shape of a face

02:36:31.380 --> 02:36:32.280
in the darkness.

02:36:32.280 --> 02:36:34.319
That’s when the weeping started.

02:36:34.319 --> 02:36:37.559
Harper knew, in that moment, his life was
over.

02:36:37.560 --> 02:36:41.640
The correct thing to do would have been to
order everyone else in the room to close their

02:36:41.640 --> 02:36:46.201
eyes while he stood there and accepted his
fate, minimizing the risk of spreading the

02:36:46.200 --> 02:36:46.920
damage further.

02:36:46.920 --> 02:36:52.080
But humans rarely have perfect reasoning,
even less so when facing mortality.

02:36:52.080 --> 02:36:57.420
Back in the present, the Shy Guy made a perfect
lunge, grabbing Harper in its iron clutches

02:36:57.420 --> 02:36:59.580
and barreling through the adjoining wall.

02:36:59.580 --> 02:37:03.899
The nearby guards scattered, terrified, keeping
their eyes on the floor.

02:37:03.899 --> 02:37:08.580
They might get a slap on the wrist for temporarily
abandoning their posts, but they weren’t

02:37:08.580 --> 02:37:11.460
going to die guarding some stupid evil mask.

02:37:11.460 --> 02:37:18.600
Speaking of, the possessive mask was surprised
to feel two new presences enter its chamber

02:37:18.600 --> 02:37:20.520
- through the now-destroyed wall.

02:37:20.520 --> 02:37:28.621
These two presences soon became just one,
as SCP-096 quickly and totally annihilated

02:37:28.620 --> 02:37:31.859
Security Officer Harper, leaving nothing left.

02:37:31.859 --> 02:37:38.099
The mask couldn’t see, per se, seeing as
it had no actual sensory organs, but it felt

02:37:38.100 --> 02:37:44.640
around this new guest with its many psychic
tendrils, taking in its strange totality.

02:37:44.640 --> 02:37:51.060
The creature was powerful, no doubt about
that, and it elicited fear from those fools

02:37:51.060 --> 02:37:57.121
at the SCP Foundation, but the mask noticed
its brainwaves were extraordinarily muted.

02:37:57.120 --> 02:38:02.640
Humans, to the mask’s vast and malicious
consciousness, weren’t the sharpest knives

02:38:02.640 --> 02:38:08.161
in the drawer, but compared to this thing’s
mind, they were a pile of tempered katanas.

02:38:08.160 --> 02:38:09.899
It barely thought at all.

02:38:09.899 --> 02:38:14.399
The mask would have to dig deeper to find
anything it could use…

02:38:14.399 --> 02:38:18.479
Meanwhile, SCP-096 finally began to calm down.

02:38:18.479 --> 02:38:21.059
The one who had seen it had been annihilated.

02:38:21.060 --> 02:38:26.880
Bubbling rage was slowly siphoned out and
replaced by the standard low but constant

02:38:26.880 --> 02:38:29.340
hum of anxiety and despair.

02:38:29.340 --> 02:38:33.780
It would simply wait until its head was bagged
and it was dragged back to the dark.

02:38:33.780 --> 02:38:37.620
Same old, same old, all the way to the end
of time.

02:38:37.620 --> 02:38:40.500
That’s when it felt something else.

02:38:40.500 --> 02:38:44.939
It started as a faint buzz, an unintelligible
whisper.

02:38:44.939 --> 02:38:49.259
Then, it was almost like a door opened in
the back of its head, Something stepped in

02:38:49.260 --> 02:38:50.580
and took a seat.

02:38:51.300 --> 02:38:54.300
Can you hear me, stranger?

02:38:55.020 --> 02:38:57.281
Knock knock.

02:38:57.840 --> 02:39:00.960
I want to speak to you…

02:39:01.560 --> 02:39:07.621
Something about the voice frightened and comforted
SCP-096 at the same time.

02:39:07.620 --> 02:39:13.740
It spoke with a greater degree of sympathy
than the creature had heard in a long time,

02:39:13.740 --> 02:39:19.679
and yet, something about the way it spoke
implied evil in its intent.

02:39:19.680 --> 02:39:23.820
I know what you want.

02:39:23.819 --> 02:39:26.880
I know what you fear.

02:39:26.880 --> 02:39:31.980
Wouldn’t it be nice if they could never
look at you again?

02:39:33.120 --> 02:39:37.140
If you could cover that face of yours?

02:39:37.140 --> 02:39:40.260
I can help you.

02:39:40.260 --> 02:39:46.711
It will be so simple.

02:39:46.711 --> 02:39:47.940
All&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:39:47.939 --> 02:39:50.939
you need to do is put me on…

02:39:50.939 --> 02:39:57.660
Little by little, 096 felt more of these strange
thoughts filling up the emptiness in its head,

02:39:57.660 --> 02:40:01.559
replacing the few thoughts the creature itself
had.

02:40:01.560 --> 02:40:07.860
It felt itself lifting its hands from the
ground, lifting them and reaching towards

02:40:07.859 --> 02:40:10.439
something - A glass box.

02:40:10.439 --> 02:40:16.799
The glass shattered and those long, white
fingers reached for something within.

02:40:16.800 --> 02:40:17.940
A mask.

02:40:17.939 --> 02:40:20.099
Just like the voice had said.

02:40:20.100 --> 02:40:24.661
A mask to stop people from looking at its
face…

02:40:25.740 --> 02:40:29.710
Yes, yes, you’re doing so well.

02:40:29.710 --> 02:40:31.737
You’re so close.

02:40:31.737 --> 02:40:34.440
Just a little further…

02:40:34.439 --> 02:40:44.280
SCP-096 lifted the mask to its face, feeling
black liquid that burned its skin dripping

02:40:44.280 --> 02:40:46.380
from the porcelain, and put it on.

02:40:46.380 --> 02:40:50.220
And in that moment, everything changed.

02:40:50.220 --> 02:40:56.340
The Shy Guy’s body began to seize up, rattling
as the mask unleashed a web of psychic tendrils

02:40:56.340 --> 02:41:02.760
through its body, mapping out across every
inch like a new nervous system, taking control.

02:41:02.760 --> 02:41:08.281
The Possessive Mask had never experienced
a host like this before - That incredible,

02:41:08.280 --> 02:41:15.509
perfect mix of physical durability and a mind
so insubstantial that it was easy to sublimate.

02:41:15.509 --> 02:41:15.540
Oh, this was going to be fun…

02:41:15.540 --> 02:41:27.300
For the first time, the Shy Guy - now under
the full control of the Possessive Mask - stood

02:41:27.300 --> 02:41:33.540
at full height on its hind legs, its spine
and shoulders clicking into place for its

02:41:33.540 --> 02:41:34.319
new stance.

02:41:34.319 --> 02:41:39.059
The Mask cracked its neck, getting used to
the new dimensions of its physicality.

02:41:39.060 --> 02:41:41.340
Its indestructible bones.

02:41:41.340 --> 02:41:43.980
Its long, grasping limbs.

02:41:43.979 --> 02:41:50.339
Its skin, burning and fizzling with the gooey
black secretions, but growing back just as

02:41:50.340 --> 02:41:50.700
quickly.

02:41:50.700 --> 02:41:54.300
The Foundation had every reason to fear it
now.

02:41:54.300 --> 02:41:58.800
A group of security personnel had gathered
in the ragged hole where the chamber’s north

02:41:58.800 --> 02:41:59.700
wall used to be.

02:41:59.700 --> 02:42:04.620
Some were wielding light firearms; the guard
at the front was carrying an opaque black

02:42:04.620 --> 02:42:04.979
bag.

02:42:04.979 --> 02:42:09.479
The Mask laughed with its new body and turned
to the crowd.

02:42:09.479 --> 02:42:13.679
The second they saw it on 096’s body, their
faces fell.

02:42:13.680 --> 02:42:16.860
For a moment, their bodies went slack with
terror.

02:42:16.859 --> 02:42:19.500
This situation was unprecedented.

02:42:19.500 --> 02:42:22.979
What course of action could they possibly
take at a time like this?

02:42:22.979 --> 02:42:27.959
It looked at the bag held by the leader of
the security force and projected a single

02:42:27.960 --> 02:42:30.720
thought into his mind: You won’t be needing
that.

02:42:30.720 --> 02:42:39.720
Before any of the guards even had a chance
to open fire, the Mask lunged forwards, using

02:42:39.720 --> 02:42:43.560
the long, terrible arms of the Shy Guy to
tear through the guards.

02:42:43.560 --> 02:42:47.701
They were dead in seconds, their bodies strewn
about the hallway.

02:42:47.700 --> 02:42:52.740
The mask’s porcelain was twisted into a
wide, sadistic grin.

02:42:52.740 --> 02:42:58.800
It could tell that it was about to have some
real fun around here - and once it’d slaughtered

02:42:58.800 --> 02:43:03.480
everyone here, it could finally stretch its
legs out in the open again.

02:43:03.479 --> 02:43:09.959
True freedom to spread misery, fear, and pain
everywhere it went.

02:43:09.960 --> 02:43:12.960
There were just a few&nbsp;
hundred members of Foundation&nbsp;

02:43:12.960 --> 02:43:15.780
personnel it needed to turn into corpses,

02:43:15.780 --> 02:43:16.200
first.

02:43:16.200 --> 02:43:21.840
More containment breach alarms sounded around
the site as the Mask began its rampage, running

02:43:21.840 --> 02:43:26.520
through the hallways and tearing apart any
unfortunate Foundation personnel it could

02:43:26.520 --> 02:43:28.380
get 096’s hands on.

02:43:28.380 --> 02:43:36.060
Guards, researchers, administrative staff,
and even one extremely unlucky janitor in

02:43:36.060 --> 02:43:37.320
Hallway C6.

02:43:37.319 --> 02:43:44.160
It was having the time of its long and terrible
life, and much to its glee, it seemed that

02:43:44.160 --> 02:43:46.859
this new host’s body was still holding up.

02:43:46.859 --> 02:43:54.599
It was perfect symbiosis - A twisted, brilliant
mind and a body that could forever support

02:43:54.600 --> 02:43:54.900
it.

02:43:54.899 --> 02:43:59.879
There would be no stopping it, a conclusion
that the hapless guards posted around the

02:43:59.880 --> 02:44:02.220
site soon realized on their own terms.

02:44:02.220 --> 02:44:08.760
096 was indestructible, but it was dumb as
a brick and had an easily exploitable weakness:

02:44:08.760 --> 02:44:11.700
Get the bag on its head and you’re home
free.

02:44:11.700 --> 02:44:15.479
This new composite creature was a different
story.

02:44:15.479 --> 02:44:20.939
It could think tactically, avoiding head-on
confrontations in favor of sneak attacks,

02:44:20.939 --> 02:44:25.379
and the monster had as much psychic combat
potential as physical.

02:44:25.380 --> 02:44:30.660
Guards roving the building in teams, heavily
armed, with anti-memetic protective gear,

02:44:30.660 --> 02:44:36.000
still reported feeling immense feelings of
psychological dread over comms.

02:44:36.000 --> 02:44:41.520
That was the greatest sign that The Mask would
come bursting through the wall moments later

02:44:41.520 --> 02:44:43.980
and tear them to shreds.

02:44:43.979 --> 02:44:48.599
The Site Director put out an urgent call for
all nearby Mobile Task Forces to intercept

02:44:48.600 --> 02:44:51.241
and help them take care of&nbsp;
the unfolding situation.

02:44:51.240 --> 02:44:57.599
Thankfully, a detachment of MTF Eta-10, also
known as "See No Evil", was operating on a

02:44:57.600 --> 02:44:58.980
case in close proximity.

02:44:58.979 --> 02:45:04.259
Given their specialization in anomalies with
a memetic visual property, many on the team

02:45:04.260 --> 02:45:06.600
had dealt with 096 breaches before.

02:45:06.600 --> 02:45:10.680
That at least gave them experience in half
of this situation.

02:45:10.680 --> 02:45:17.460
And one operative among them, Sergeant Henrique
Ramirez, would be the one to bring this nightmare

02:45:17.460 --> 02:45:18.120
to an end.

02:45:18.120 --> 02:45:21.479
But it would cost him his life in the process.

02:45:21.479 --> 02:45:26.339
The Mask was still using its new indestructible
body to wreak havoc on the containment site.

02:45:26.340 --> 02:45:32.160
Once it had taken out the primary contingents
of guards, it was free to have its fun with

02:45:32.160 --> 02:45:37.800
the rest: Stalking defenseless researchers
through the halls, making sure to induce maximum

02:45:37.800 --> 02:45:40.980
terror before finally striking the killing
blow.

02:45:40.979 --> 02:45:46.740
Every single one of them died with a head
full of demonic whispers - it told them of

02:45:46.740 --> 02:45:48.599
the Mask’s eternal dominion.

02:45:48.600 --> 02:45:53.220
Now, it had found the perfect host, nothing
on earth would stop it.

02:45:53.220 --> 02:45:56.700
Humans would be mere ants under its feet.

02:45:57.300 --> 02:45:59.160
Eta-10 touched down and entered the building.

02:45:59.160 --> 02:46:04.200
Ramirez was point man, leading the others
into the bowels of the blood-drenched containment

02:46:04.200 --> 02:46:04.559
site.

02:46:04.560 --> 02:46:10.560
They’d been briefed on what they were heading
in for: 096 and 035 had reached symbiosis

02:46:10.560 --> 02:46:13.800
and were displaying unprecedented anomalous
effects.

02:46:13.800 --> 02:46:19.860
Enter with extreme caution - They’re beyond
dangerous, even more so together than alone.

02:46:19.859 --> 02:46:25.139
Ideally, Ramirez would have wanted to go in
with a comprehensive plan, but lives were

02:46:25.140 --> 02:46:25.860
on the line here.

02:46:25.859 --> 02:46:29.700
They needed to leap off the cliff and build
their wings on the way down.

02:46:29.700 --> 02:46:35.220
It was only when they finally encountered
the monster that they realized just how outmatched

02:46:35.220 --> 02:46:35.820
they were.

02:46:35.819 --> 02:46:41.519
Despite their best efforts, the combined speed,
intelligence, and ferocity of the Mask’s

02:46:41.520 --> 02:46:45.000
new form allowed it to make short work of
Eta-10.

02:46:45.000 --> 02:46:48.540
Only Ramirez was left, heavily injured.

02:46:48.540 --> 02:46:52.859
Even if a miracle happened, he knew he wasn’t
getting out of here alive.

02:46:52.859 --> 02:46:56.939
The Mask grabbed him with one of 096’s claws
and lifted him up.

02:46:56.939 --> 02:46:59.160
It would take its time with this one.

02:46:59.160 --> 02:47:00.479
Make him suffer.

02:47:00.479 --> 02:47:02.099
Watch him squirm.

02:47:02.100 --> 02:47:04.260
Destroy his mind.

02:47:04.260 --> 02:47:08.640
Ramirez felt the Mask’s psychic tendrils
penetrate the membrane of his mind.

02:47:08.640 --> 02:47:15.480
Those whispers, those terrible whispers, reciting
all his worst fears with terrible glee.

02:47:15.479 --> 02:47:16.979
His gun was out of ammo.

02:47:16.979 --> 02:47:18.359
His knife was broken.

02:47:18.359 --> 02:47:21.719
All he had left on him was a pocket mirror.

02:47:21.720 --> 02:47:24.241
And that was his eureka moment.

02:47:24.240 --> 02:47:27.899
It was a long shot, but it was also his only
shot.

02:47:27.899 --> 02:47:31.920
He reached out and grabbed the bottom of the
mask, pulling for dear life.

02:47:31.920 --> 02:47:37.620
His other hand shot into his pocket and grabbed
the mirror, opening its lid with a deft flick

02:47:37.620 --> 02:47:38.099
of his thumb.

02:47:38.100 --> 02:47:41.281
It was all too fast for the Mask to even register
what was going on.

02:47:41.280 --> 02:47:44.700
Ramirez forced his eyes shut and lifted up
the mirror.

02:47:44.700 --> 02:47:51.120
The Mask saw its own reflection in the glass,
as the bottom of its face came loose, revealing

02:47:51.120 --> 02:47:53.640
the reflection of the face underneath.

02:47:53.640 --> 02:47:57.600
The Mask squeezed, killing Ramirez, but it
was already too late.

02:47:57.600 --> 02:48:03.241
It had finally seen the face of its host,
and that would cost it dearly.

02:48:03.240 --> 02:48:08.939
The Mask felt a sudden and tremendous pushback
to its psychic forces - A blind despair and

02:48:08.939 --> 02:48:10.979
then rage that choked out everything.

02:48:10.979 --> 02:48:15.120
SCP-096 began to sob and howl.

02:48:15.120 --> 02:48:18.000
Somehow, the Mask was no longer in control.

02:48:18.000 --> 02:48:23.939
Despite its psychic protests, 096 reached
up and tore the terrible mask from its face,

02:48:23.939 --> 02:48:28.379
tossing it against the wall with such force
that it was embedded in the brickwork.

02:48:28.380 --> 02:48:34.020
Its last thoughts, as other Mobile Task Force
operators descended on the area to bag 096

02:48:34.020 --> 02:48:39.541
and return it to its containment chamber,
were: What the hell just happened?

02:48:39.540 --> 02:48:44.340
And the next thought that crossed the mind
of the Site Director was, “Request site

02:48:44.340 --> 02:48:46.800
transfer for 035 as soon as possible.

02:48:46.800 --> 02:48:49.740
Don’t want a repeat of this incident any
time soon…”

02:48:49.740 --> 02:48:53.939
Well hello there, SCP Fans! Let’s get this out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:53.939 --> 02:48:57.899
the way first - You’ve seen the title and&nbsp;
thumbnail, and you’re probably wondering:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:48:57.899 --> 02:49:04.859
Wait, is Siren Head an SCP? And the&nbsp;
answer is No. Siren Head is not an SCP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:04.859 --> 02:49:10.200
it’s a freaky internet monster created by&nbsp;
renowned Canadian Horror Artist Trevor Henderson.

02:49:10.200 --> 02:49:15.720
Your next question is probably: What’s with&nbsp;
this crazy-looking server bank behind me?

02:49:15.720 --> 02:49:22.500
Allow me to introduce you to the Anom-O-Tron 6000,&nbsp;
the latest advancement of supercomputing owned and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:22.500 --> 02:49:28.020
operated exclusively by the SCP Foundation.&nbsp;
It’s capable of a whole bunch of interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:28.020 --> 02:49:33.360
features - calculating ideal containment methods,&nbsp;
running state of the art Foundation webcrawler&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:33.359 --> 02:49:38.759
software, making and editing fun SCP YouTube&nbsp;
videos - but the most interesting feature that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:38.760 --> 02:49:44.580
Anom-O-Tron has to offer is running hyper-advanced&nbsp;
simulations regarding all things anomalous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:44.580 --> 02:49:50.340
with data fed into it by thousands of real-life&nbsp;
experiments conducted by the SCP Foundation.

02:49:50.340 --> 02:49:54.360
It’s going to save countless dollars&nbsp;
and lives for Foundation researchers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:54.359 --> 02:49:58.620
but way more importantly, it’s going&nbsp;
to let us explore all kinds of weird&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:49:58.620 --> 02:50:04.920
and wacky What If scenarios that would never&nbsp;
happen organically. Could SCP-343 beat Goku,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:04.920 --> 02:50:11.940
or Superman? Who’s a deadlier supernatural killer,&nbsp;
SCP-106 or Freddy Kreuger? Would the world simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:11.939 --> 02:50:18.719
explode from adorableness if SCP-999 and Baby Yoda&nbsp;
ever crossed those multiversal barriers and met?

02:50:18.720 --> 02:50:23.940
We can explore all of these questions -&nbsp;
Along with any others you may have down&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:23.939 --> 02:50:28.319
in the comments. Seriously, comment it.&nbsp;
We dare you! - but to get things started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:28.319 --> 02:50:32.639
we figured we’d make the Anom-O-Tron pit&nbsp;
the Foundation’s lankiest nightmare monster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:32.640 --> 02:50:38.281
SCP-096, against the internet’s&nbsp;
- Trevor Henderson’s Siren Head.

02:50:38.280 --> 02:50:41.160
Sorry, Slender Man, you’re just not cool anymore.

02:50:41.160 --> 02:50:45.840
So, without further adieu, let’s kick&nbsp;
the Anom-O-Tron into gear and see how&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:45.840 --> 02:50:51.540
this wild battle plays out. It’s&nbsp;
time for SCP-096 Vs Siren Head!

02:50:51.540 --> 02:50:55.319
Ricky and Eliot, a pair of aspiring&nbsp;
travel YouTubers from California,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:50:55.319 --> 02:51:00.719
were taking a vacation to the Pacific Northwest&nbsp;
to spend some time in the area’s beautiful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:00.720 --> 02:51:05.460
lush forests. However, while the duo were&nbsp;
both big fans of travel for its own sake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:05.460 --> 02:51:10.680
they weren’t just here for the trees and fresh&nbsp;
air. You see, despite their jetsetting lifestyle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:10.680 --> 02:51:16.080
Ricky and Eliot’s subscribers and views hadn’t&nbsp;
exactly been phenomenal lately. Turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:16.080 --> 02:51:20.160
hiking vlogs and GoPro footage of the two&nbsp;
of them jumping into absurdly blue water in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:20.160 --> 02:51:24.000
Bali weren’t exactly that interesting&nbsp;
to people who weren’t actually there.

02:51:24.000 --> 02:51:28.680
But Ricky had formulated an ingenious&nbsp;
plan to pull the two of them out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:28.680 --> 02:51:34.080
obscurity. They would hunt down and film&nbsp;
real footage of the legendary Siren Head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:34.080 --> 02:51:40.200
a supernatural monster said to haunt forests and&nbsp;
isolated graveyards. There were plenty of fakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:40.200 --> 02:51:44.819
out there on YouTube, but if the two of them&nbsp;
could get real footage of Siren Head - and also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:44.819 --> 02:51:50.099
preferably, survive the experience - they’d be&nbsp;
framing their Golden Play Buttons in no time.

02:51:50.100 --> 02:51:54.000
But they were already encountering&nbsp;
certain issues. For starters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:54.000 --> 02:51:58.380
the two Californians had severely underestimated&nbsp;
just how cold it was in the Pacific Northwest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:51:58.380 --> 02:52:04.560
and as such, were already shivering from&nbsp;
bone-deep cold. The second issue was food:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:04.560 --> 02:52:08.940
While the two of them had thought ahead and&nbsp;
brought a selection of tasty protein bars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:08.939 --> 02:52:13.500
they’d also gotten hungry during the early&nbsp;
hours of their walk, and eaten all of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:13.500 --> 02:52:17.520
leaving them feeling severely out of&nbsp;
luck by the time dinner rolled around.

02:52:17.520 --> 02:52:22.380
And the third and perhaps most problematic&nbsp;
issue of all was the fact that Ricky and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:22.380 --> 02:52:27.300
Eliot didn’t exactly have a great sense of&nbsp;
direction. During all of their previous trips,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:27.300 --> 02:52:31.260
they’d been on guided tours with&nbsp;
local experts. In this instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:31.260 --> 02:52:36.780
wanting to take all the Siren Head glory and clout&nbsp;
for themselves, they’d decided to come in alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:36.779 --> 02:52:41.219
with the incredibly janky Google&nbsp;
Maps app to guide them. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:41.220 --> 02:52:46.920
as the night was creeping in, the two aspiring&nbsp;
YouTube celebrities had no idea where they were.

02:52:46.920 --> 02:52:50.580
Needless to say, it wasn’t&nbsp;
exactly an ideal situation.

02:52:50.580 --> 02:52:55.740
And to make matters worse, Ricky and Eliot&nbsp;
had been experiencing some strange feelings&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:52:55.740 --> 02:53:00.479
for the last couple of hours, when they’d&nbsp;
gotten particularly deep into the forest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:00.479 --> 02:53:06.660
That dread-inducing sensation of being watched&nbsp;
by something lurking just out of sight. A gaze&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:06.660 --> 02:53:12.240
with some unknowable and darkly malicious&nbsp;
intent boiling behind it. These two poor&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:12.240 --> 02:53:16.740
saps had no idea what was in store for&nbsp;
them tonight, and wouldn’t you know it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:16.740 --> 02:53:21.300
the one watching them right now wouldn’t&nbsp;
even be the only monster after them.

02:53:21.300 --> 02:53:27.120
After an expectable amount of bumbling and&nbsp;
buffoonery, Ricky and Eliot managed to gather up&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:27.120 --> 02:53:33.300
enough tinder to make a semi-respectable campfire.&nbsp;
They could sleep out here tonight, then set off in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:33.300 --> 02:53:39.540
search of Siren Head or civilization - whichever&nbsp;
came first - in the daylight the next day. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:39.540 --> 02:53:44.939
duo did what they could to remain chipper, in&nbsp;
spite of the ominous circumstances. Sure, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:44.939 --> 02:53:49.319
were trapped in the depths of the woods, alone,&nbsp;
but think of the amazing vlog footage they’d get!

02:53:49.319 --> 02:53:53.519
They’d be like the next Blair Witch&nbsp;
crew, except they’d live, hopefully!

02:53:53.520 --> 02:53:59.760
But as the duo sat next to their campfire, they&nbsp;
felt that ominous sensation again. The hairs&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:53:59.760 --> 02:54:04.140
on the back of their necks started standing&nbsp;
up. They felt shivers that didn’t just come&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:04.140 --> 02:54:09.060
from the cold. More and more, they started to&nbsp;
feel certain that something in this forest was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:09.060 --> 02:54:15.241
watching them. Something ancient and powerful&nbsp;
and evil. That’s when one of them heard the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:15.240 --> 02:54:20.040
telltale crackle of radio static, and their&nbsp;
skeletons almost jumped out of their skin.

02:54:20.040 --> 02:54:24.120
Could it be him? The Patron Saint&nbsp;
of Going Missing Without a Trace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:24.120 --> 02:54:29.460
of Creeping Dread, of Bad Things&nbsp;
Coming? Siren Head himself?

02:54:29.460 --> 02:54:35.520
Both men fell silent. All they could hear was&nbsp;
the crackling of their own fire. Had the sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:35.520 --> 02:54:41.041
before just been an illusion? A product of&nbsp;
a pair of paranoid minds also predisposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:41.040 --> 02:54:45.840
sense Siren-Headed monsters lurking amongst the&nbsp;
trees? They’d been reading about a higher rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:45.840 --> 02:54:50.400
of disappearances in this area, along with&nbsp;
a number of frightening sightings of tall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:50.399 --> 02:54:56.519
thin beings and radio static that seemed perfectly&nbsp;
consistent with Siren Head activity. That’s why&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:54:56.520 --> 02:55:00.840
they’d come here in the first place.&nbsp;
But had they come here to their deaths?

02:55:00.840 --> 02:55:06.300
The two needed to distract themselves or they’d&nbsp;
go mad from the paranoia. They decided to share&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:06.300 --> 02:55:10.980
a few fun little anecdotes of the travels&nbsp;
they’d had before meeting one another, showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:10.979 --> 02:55:16.740
the photos they’d saved on their smartphones.&nbsp;
Ricky had been to Hawaii, Tahiti, Bora Bora,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:16.740 --> 02:55:23.219
Fiji, St. Lucia, and the Maldives. Clearly,&nbsp;
he liked his vacations warm and sandy. Eliot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:23.220 --> 02:55:28.560
on the other hand, tended to like his trips to be&nbsp;
a little more adventurous. He’d seen volcanoes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:28.560 --> 02:55:34.621
waterfalls, deep and treacherous cave systems,&nbsp;
and of course, a little guided mountaineering.

02:55:34.620 --> 02:55:38.220
One photo that Eliot showed Ricky&nbsp;
was particularly breathtaking:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:38.220 --> 02:55:43.560
He was standing in the foreground of a snowy&nbsp;
landscape, with an incredible mountain range&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:43.560 --> 02:55:48.900
behind him. When Ricky asked Eliot where exactly&nbsp;
he’d taken this picture, strangely enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:48.899 --> 02:55:54.240
he drew a complete blank. Maybe somewhere&nbsp;
in Canada, or Siberia, or something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:55:54.240 --> 02:55:59.160
that… But it was a hell of a trip, whatever&nbsp;
the case, he remembered having a great time.

02:55:59.160 --> 02:56:02.460
It was such a tiny detail,&nbsp;
but when looking at the photo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:02.460 --> 02:56:08.100
Ricky couldn’t help but notice something&nbsp;
in the background. A speck of white. Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:08.100 --> 02:56:13.860
not exactly newsworthy in a snowy mountain&nbsp;
range, but this wasn’t snow. It was a speck&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:13.859 --> 02:56:19.019
of white that seemed to be a slightly different&nbsp;
shade than the rest of the mountain. Strange.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:19.020 --> 02:56:23.220
Perhaps it was just some tiny particle stuck&nbsp;
on the lens whenever the photo was taken.

02:56:23.220 --> 02:56:26.280
The two continued to chat and&nbsp;
warm their hands against the fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:26.279 --> 02:56:30.059
having no idea of the horror&nbsp;
they’d just awoken in doing so.

02:56:30.060 --> 02:56:34.500
Over a hundred miles away, at an&nbsp;
SCP Foundation containment site,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:34.500 --> 02:56:38.819
researchers and guards walked around the&nbsp;
perimeter of a huge, airtight metal cube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:38.819 --> 02:56:43.799
reading off diagnostics and audio recordings.&nbsp;
For reasons that should be obvious to everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:43.800 --> 02:56:47.220
who’s a fan of this channel, there&nbsp;
were no camera feeds coming from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:47.220 --> 02:56:53.280
inside of the great metal cube. Only a faint&nbsp;
whimpering sound, like a sad, wounded animal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:56:53.279 --> 02:56:59.219
and the clanking of pacing feet moving back and&nbsp;
forth over the metal, dragging forlorn knuckles.

02:56:59.220 --> 02:57:03.660
It turned every head in the room when&nbsp;
the crying started to get louder. Soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:03.660 --> 02:57:08.700
it wasn’t just quiet weeping, it was a&nbsp;
series of horrendous, slobbering sobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:08.700 --> 02:57:13.740
wails of pure pain and despair. Someone in&nbsp;
the room screamed “Close your eyes and hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:13.740 --> 02:57:18.240
the deck!”, knowing exactly what had happened.&nbsp;
They’d learned well enough from the infamous&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:18.240 --> 02:57:24.000
Incident 096-1-A Containment Breach that there&nbsp;
was no way of stopping this thing until it’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:24.000 --> 02:57:28.979
run its course. The best they could do was&nbsp;
minimize their own fatalities in the process.

02:57:28.979 --> 02:57:33.959
Everyone in the room did as they were told,&nbsp;
hitting the ground, closing their eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:33.960 --> 02:57:38.880
and covering them with their hands, just&nbsp;
to be safe. That’s when the solid steel&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:38.880 --> 02:57:44.880
of the cube was torn open like crepe paper,&nbsp;
and the howling, raging SCP-096 broke free,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:44.880 --> 02:57:50.460
galloping on all fours through the nearest wall,&nbsp;
shattering it and spraying debris everywhere.

02:57:50.460 --> 02:57:55.800
As Mobile Task Forces were dispatched to track&nbsp;
the rampaging abomination and try their best to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:55.800 --> 02:57:59.580
clear the path ahead as it sought out its&nbsp;
target, one thought was going through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:57:59.580 --> 02:58:05.460
minds of everyone in the room: “Woe betide&nbsp;
the poor fools who saw that monster’s face…”

02:58:05.460 --> 02:58:10.980
Back in the deep, dark forests of the Pacific&nbsp;
Northwest, the presence of some unnerving force&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:10.979 --> 02:58:16.259
was becoming far harder to deny for Ricky and&nbsp;
Eliot. They heard the sounds of garbled voices out&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:16.260 --> 02:58:22.020
in the woods. The squeals and crackles of radio&nbsp;
static. They saw shapes moving amongst the trees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:22.020 --> 02:58:27.900
tall and thin, like long, spindly limbs.&nbsp;
While neither wanted to voice their fear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:27.899 --> 02:58:32.580
they were both thinking similar things:&nbsp;
If there really was a monster out here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:32.580 --> 02:58:36.960
why hadn’t it killed them? It had&nbsp;
all the time in the world to do it.

02:58:36.960 --> 02:58:43.200
But that was just it, wasn’t it? It had all&nbsp;
the time in the world, so why rush? It had&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:43.200 --> 02:58:49.319
every advantage. It could take its time, and&nbsp;
do this exactly how it wanted to. Ricky and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:49.319 --> 02:58:54.479
Eliot had made themselves the perfect prey.&nbsp;
And as they saw a huge, lithe figure suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:54.479 --> 02:58:58.919
separate itself from the trees, they began&nbsp;
to wonder if it was really worth it to risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:58:58.920 --> 02:59:05.040
both of their lives for YouTube clout. Either&nbsp;
way, it was now probably too late for regrets.

02:59:05.040 --> 02:59:09.720
Ricky and Eliot’s jaws fell in horror as&nbsp;
the monster emerged from the darkness before&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:09.720 --> 02:59:15.060
them. They recognized the awful, mottled skin&nbsp;
stretched over the monster’s forty-foot frame.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:15.060 --> 02:59:21.180
They saw the grasping hands, and up above, the&nbsp;
nightmarish, faceless head, split into a pair of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:21.180 --> 02:59:26.760
sirens pointing in different directions, each&nbsp;
one filled with gnashing teeth and slithering&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:26.760 --> 02:59:31.080
tongues. It was even more terrifying&nbsp;
than they possibly could have imagined.

02:59:31.080 --> 02:59:33.240
Siren Head’s speakers crackled into life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:33.240 --> 02:59:42.840
and hissed out, “NINE. EIGHTEEN.&nbsp;
ONE. CHILD. SEVENTEEN. REMOVE. VILE.”

02:59:42.840 --> 02:59:46.260
Ricky and Eliot stumbled&nbsp;
backwards as Siren Head advanced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:46.260 --> 02:59:51.060
its speakers still bleeding hateful&nbsp;
static. Neither could move or speak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:59:51.060 --> 02:59:55.440
only watch as its long, gnarled fingers&nbsp;
reached from the dark to grasp them.

02:59:55.439 --> 03:00:00.719
There was a terrible pained wail in the woods&nbsp;
after that. But it wasn’t coming from Ricky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:00.720 --> 03:00:03.960
Eliot, or Siren Head. It was distant at first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:03.960 --> 03:00:09.660
but soon became louder and louder as the force’s&nbsp;
point of origin came barreling towards them.

03:00:09.660 --> 03:00:16.319
Even Siren Head couldn’t help but turn to&nbsp;
see SCP-096, a flash of terrible white fury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:16.319 --> 03:00:21.059
come charging towards them in the dark,&nbsp;
knowing down all the trees in its path.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:21.060 --> 03:00:26.640
Leaves and pine needles scattered. Trunks&nbsp;
exploded into splinters. Nothing would stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:26.640 --> 03:00:32.460
the Shy Guy from claiming its prey. Nothing&nbsp;
would stop it from destroying Ricky, and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:32.460 --> 03:00:37.260
as it approached, Eliot made the fatal&nbsp;
mistake of looking at its face, too.

03:00:37.260 --> 03:00:42.180
096 lunged, ready to go in for the kill, when&nbsp;
Siren Head’s hands closed around the Shy Guy’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:42.180 --> 03:00:46.920
slender torso. In one fluid motion, with&nbsp;
surprising strength for its scrawny build,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:46.920 --> 03:00:51.960
it hurled the Shy Guy into the distance,&nbsp;
smashing another several trees in its path.

03:00:51.960 --> 03:00:58.620
The words, “MY PREY. MY PREY. MINE. MINE. MINE,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:00:58.620 --> 03:01:00.240
hissed out of its speakers.

03:01:00.240 --> 03:01:06.960
But 096 was only down for mere moments. It&nbsp;
sprung back up on its hind and forelegs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:06.960 --> 03:01:12.060
started galloping towards Ricky and Eliot again,&nbsp;
who were still frozen in place. This time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:12.060 --> 03:01:16.500
just before the Shy Guy’s terrible claws could&nbsp;
close around the two hapless wannabe YouTubers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:16.500 --> 03:01:21.779
down came Siren Head’s clenched fist,&nbsp;
pounding 096’s head into the dirt.

03:01:21.779 --> 03:01:25.920
Overhead, Mobile Task Force helicopters&nbsp;
circled, wearing their SCRAMBLE goggles&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:25.920 --> 03:01:30.120
and trying to get a visual on the situation&nbsp;
unfolding below. They could hardly believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:30.120 --> 03:01:34.800
what they were seeing down there. Was some&nbsp;
other unclassified anomaly managing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:34.800 --> 03:01:40.020
intercept SCP-096 in its rage state? Only a&nbsp;
handful of anomalies on record had ever been&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:40.020 --> 03:01:45.541
shown as being capable of such a feat. It seemed&nbsp;
they were dealing with something serious here.

03:01:45.540 --> 03:01:50.460
Down below, the battle continued. Siren&nbsp;
Head had a unique advantage over most&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:50.460 --> 03:01:56.640
other combatants facing SCP-096: It was&nbsp;
an extradimensional being with no eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:01:56.640 --> 03:02:03.000
merely an innate psychic awareness of its&nbsp;
victims around it - not unlike how SCP-096&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:03.000 --> 03:02:08.160
detects people seeing its face in photographs&nbsp;
or videos without need for any kind of direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:08.160 --> 03:02:12.660
contact. As such, it was impossible for&nbsp;
Siren Head to see the Shy Guy’s face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:12.660 --> 03:02:17.580
and thus, impossible for the Shy Guy to ever&nbsp;
actually direct its aggression against it.

03:02:17.580 --> 03:02:23.819
Whether or not 096 would kill-steal Siren Head’s&nbsp;
intended prey was another matter entirely.

03:02:23.819 --> 03:02:28.080
In the night’s first minor miracle, Ricky&nbsp;
and Eliot remembered that they’d been born&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:28.080 --> 03:02:33.000
with working legs, and decided that right now&nbsp;
would be an excellent time to use them. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:33.000 --> 03:02:38.580
turned and began to run while Siren Head continued&nbsp;
beating the Shy Guy into the dirt, sprinting with&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:38.580 --> 03:02:43.080
all their might. However, the growing distance&nbsp;
between them only aggravated the Shy Guy further.

03:02:43.080 --> 03:02:48.420
It sprang out from the dirt with unstoppable&nbsp;
force and broke away from Siren Head’s grip.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:48.420 --> 03:02:53.880
The Shy Guy never wasted time, ever, it always&nbsp;
made a beeline for its intended victim with&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:02:53.880 --> 03:03:00.300
one directive in mind: Total annihilation.&nbsp;
Tonight is no different. Ricky and Eliot&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:00.300 --> 03:03:03.180
screamed as they heard its pounding&nbsp;
gallop on the ground behind them.

03:03:03.180 --> 03:03:06.180
Closer, closer, closer…

03:03:06.180 --> 03:03:08.880
Then suddenly, defying all spatial reasoning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:08.880 --> 03:03:14.400
Siren Head emerged out of the darkness between&nbsp;
Ricky, Eliot, and 096. Being forty feet tall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:14.399 --> 03:03:19.200
Siren Head dwarfed even the Shy Guy. It lunged&nbsp;
and grabbed the wailing white monstrosity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:19.200 --> 03:03:24.720
holding it as one would a particulary vicious&nbsp;
cat and trying to keep it in place. Siren&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:24.720 --> 03:03:29.700
Head wanted to crush this monster’s bones and&nbsp;
carry on stalking its feeble human prey - who&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:29.700 --> 03:03:34.260
were currently in the process of getting&nbsp;
away - but somehow, that was impossible.

03:03:34.260 --> 03:03:38.280
It was almost like the monster&nbsp;
had an indestructible skeleton…

03:03:38.279 --> 03:03:42.960
Up above, the Mobile Task Forces continued&nbsp;
observing this clash of the titans from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:42.960 --> 03:03:48.660
two helicopters. Seeing how Siren Head was somehow&nbsp;
doing a serviceable job of subduing the Shy Guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:03:48.660 --> 03:03:52.319
one of the task force members joked,&nbsp;
“Maybe we should hire this guy.”

03:03:52.319 --> 03:03:56.580
Another chimed in, “I don’t think we&nbsp;
have any ballistic vests in his size.”

03:03:56.580 --> 03:04:01.800
The laughter soon turned to screams when Siren&nbsp;
Head hurled SCP-096’s flailing body up at one&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:01.800 --> 03:04:07.080
of the helicopters with incredible force, knocking&nbsp;
it out of the sky. It careened down to the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:07.080 --> 03:04:11.580
a ball of fear and violence, before&nbsp;
exploding. Anyone who survived the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:11.580 --> 03:04:16.200
initial blast was unlucky enough to have seen&nbsp;
the Shy Guy’s face. Their screams rang out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:16.200 --> 03:04:21.720
briefly, before being abruptly silenced by the&nbsp;
monster. Helicopter Alpha had no survivors.

03:04:21.720 --> 03:04:26.040
Meanwhile, Ricky and Eliot had kept running&nbsp;
until their lungs burned and their muscles&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:26.040 --> 03:04:31.439
pumped thumbtacks. Seeing that the chaos was so&nbsp;
far behind them now, they took a moment to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:31.439 --> 03:04:36.960
and breathe, recomposing themselves. In the second&nbsp;
minor miracle of the night, it looked like the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:36.960 --> 03:04:42.359
of them had actually survived this ordeal. Those&nbsp;
two horrific monsters had canceled each other out.

03:04:42.359 --> 03:04:47.040
But Ricky and Eliot were about to learn a&nbsp;
harsh lesson: They really weren’t that lucky.

03:04:47.040 --> 03:04:50.580
The last thing the two ever saw&nbsp;
was Siren Head emerging from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:50.580 --> 03:04:54.240
darkness once more. The last thing they&nbsp;
ever felt was the monster’s terrible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:54.240 --> 03:04:59.160
gnarled fingers curling around their bodies,&nbsp;
too fast for them to escape. And the last thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:04:59.160 --> 03:05:04.620
they ever heard was each other’s screams,&nbsp;
followed by a sickeningly meaty crunch.

03:05:04.620 --> 03:05:09.899
Elsewhere in the forest, SCP-096’s&nbsp;
wails died down to its usual maudlin&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:09.899 --> 03:05:14.040
sob. The rage state had ended; all&nbsp;
of its targets had been destroyed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:14.040 --> 03:05:17.519
It just hadn’t been the one to&nbsp;
do all of the destroying, per se.

03:05:17.520 --> 03:05:22.920
Not long after that, Helicopter Bravo descended&nbsp;
safely and the remaining MTF members closed around&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:22.920 --> 03:05:27.060
the blubbering beast. Someone black bagged&nbsp;
it and everyone breathed a sigh of relief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:27.060 --> 03:05:30.300
knowing that was at least one&nbsp;
monster taken care of tonight.

03:05:30.300 --> 03:05:33.300
“What about the other one?”&nbsp;
the MTF Lieutenant asked.

03:05:33.300 --> 03:05:37.440
The team’s commander shook his head and&nbsp;
said, “Let’s worry about that freak later&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:05:37.439 --> 03:05:41.399
and get 096 back to containment.&nbsp;
This place gives me the creeps…”

03:05:41.399 --> 03:05:47.040
It happened in 1981, in a small, rural village
in West Germany.

03:05:47.040 --> 03:05:53.939
Siegfried Geise, nine years old, ran across
the bitter snow, tears freezing to his cheeks.

03:05:53.939 --> 03:05:58.679
It was Christmas Day, and not long ago, he’d
been so happy.

03:05:58.680 --> 03:06:05.820
He was celebrating with his family - his mother,
his father, and his younger sister, the centers

03:06:05.819 --> 03:06:12.059
of his universe - when that monster had attacked
and destroyed everything he’d ever known.

03:06:12.060 --> 03:06:13.680
The white terror.

03:06:13.680 --> 03:06:16.320
The destroyer of Christmas cheer.

03:06:16.319 --> 03:06:20.399
The Yule Man, SCP-4666.

03:06:20.399 --> 03:06:24.540
Though of course, young Siegfried had no idea.

03:06:24.540 --> 03:06:29.279
In hindsight, Siegfried’s parents had been
noticing strange things in the lead up to

03:06:29.279 --> 03:06:30.120
Christmas Day.

03:06:30.120 --> 03:06:32.640
Unfamiliar bumps and knocks.

03:06:32.640 --> 03:06:35.040
Strange smells coming from the attic.

03:06:35.040 --> 03:06:40.560
The occasional tall dark silhouette on the
horizon outside their isolated old farmhouse.

03:06:40.560 --> 03:06:46.260
But in the hustle and bustle of the season,
nobody really paid the proper attention to

03:06:46.260 --> 03:06:49.560
all the signs of an incoming Weissnacht event.

03:06:49.560 --> 03:06:54.900
Every eerie feeling was just written off as
stress, or overactive imaginations.

03:06:54.899 --> 03:07:00.479
None of the Geise family knew about the true
danger they were facing until they woke up

03:07:00.479 --> 03:07:06.299
to see the Yule Man standing over them, sharpening
a rusty blade and staring at them with hungry,

03:07:06.300 --> 03:07:07.860
sadistic eyes.

03:07:07.859 --> 03:07:09.660
“Merry Christmas...”

03:07:09.660 --> 03:07:12.540
the creature rasped, and chuckled.

03:07:12.540 --> 03:07:17.939
Siegfried was lucky to escape alive, but of
course, he would never see his family again.

03:07:17.939 --> 03:07:23.879
He’d be picked up a day or so later, traumatized,
catatonic, and almost frozen to death.

03:07:23.880 --> 03:07:28.920
When he was finally able to speak again, he
spoke of a monster having killed his family.

03:07:28.920 --> 03:07:33.960
When the police investigated his home, and
found the remains, they deemed it the work

03:07:33.960 --> 03:07:39.779
of an all too human serial killer, and poor
Siegfried Geise was entered into the foster

03:07:39.779 --> 03:07:40.080
system.

03:07:40.080 --> 03:07:45.120
Years of work with psychiatric professionals
tried to convince young Siegfried that the

03:07:45.120 --> 03:07:50.640
monster he claimed to see - the unnaturally
tall, gnarled, bony freak with those shimmering

03:07:50.640 --> 03:07:54.000
black eyes - was merely a product of his trauma.

03:07:54.000 --> 03:07:55.859
But he knew the truth.

03:07:55.859 --> 03:07:57.299
He always knew.

03:07:57.300 --> 03:08:03.060
What he encountered that day, the thing that
took his family, was nothing less than a true

03:08:03.060 --> 03:08:03.540
monster.

03:08:03.540 --> 03:08:10.200
And at nine years old, Siegfried made a vow:
He would one day kill the monster, no matter

03:08:10.200 --> 03:08:11.220
what it took.

03:08:11.220 --> 03:08:16.680
When Siegfried was finally adopted and returned
to school, he did everything he could to apply

03:08:16.680 --> 03:08:17.940
himself academically.

03:08:17.939 --> 03:08:24.000
He had no interest in friends, or romantic
relationships, even as he grew into his teenage

03:08:24.000 --> 03:08:24.240
years.

03:08:24.240 --> 03:08:29.519
The only thing that drew his interest, in
addition to the perfection of his academic

03:08:29.520 --> 03:08:32.820
life, was his own private research into the
supernatural.

03:08:32.819 --> 03:08:38.939
He became an expert in all things occultic
and anomalous, sightings of ghosts, phantoms,

03:08:38.939 --> 03:08:40.439
aliens, monsters.

03:08:40.439 --> 03:08:44.759
He wanted to know it all, no, needed to know
it all.

03:08:44.760 --> 03:08:49.620
He would arm himself with knowledge, wanting
to know everything he could in advance of

03:08:49.620 --> 03:08:52.740
the confrontation that would be his final
destiny.

03:08:52.740 --> 03:08:57.179
He also began obsessively researching attacks
similar to the ones that killed his family,

03:08:57.180 --> 03:09:01.800
and found an eerie pattern seeming to unfold
all over the Northern hemisphere.

03:09:01.800 --> 03:09:06.900
During the twelve days of Christmas, entire
families would be found dead in their homes,

03:09:06.899 --> 03:09:11.819
mutilated horrifically, often with children
having gone completely missing.

03:09:11.819 --> 03:09:16.620
They happened far enough apart in terms of
time and space that no connection was drawn

03:09:16.620 --> 03:09:21.660
between the events - All just random attacks,
according to the police and media.

03:09:21.660 --> 03:09:23.340
Weren’t the connections obvious?

03:09:23.340 --> 03:09:24.600
He didn’t understand.

03:09:24.600 --> 03:09:30.180
It was almost like there was some kind of
sweeping coverup, obscuring the true culprit

03:09:30.180 --> 03:09:31.680
behind all these killings.

03:09:31.680 --> 03:09:35.280
The same beast that’d destroyed his life.

03:09:35.279 --> 03:09:40.500
As Siegfried - now known to most as Dr. Geise
- ascended through his time at university,

03:09:40.500 --> 03:09:45.660
he became one of Germany’s foremost scholars
on the occult, with several published papers

03:09:45.660 --> 03:09:50.460
and books that drew the fascination of fellow
experts and laymen alike - But most importantly

03:09:50.460 --> 03:09:54.600
of all, Siegfried’s work got the attention
of the SCP Foundation.

03:09:54.600 --> 03:10:00.180
They reached out to the ambitious rising star
of the paranormal world and offered him a

03:10:00.180 --> 03:10:00.840
researcher position.

03:10:00.840 --> 03:10:06.840
Dr. Geise was elated, and said he would happily
take the role if they answered one question

03:10:06.840 --> 03:10:13.200
for him: Did they know anything about a horrific
humanoid monster that attacked families across

03:10:13.200 --> 03:10:15.779
the Northern hemisphere around Christmastime?

03:10:15.779 --> 03:10:21.779
This was the moment when Dr. Siegfried Geise,
after decades of searching, finally came to

03:10:21.779 --> 03:10:24.840
know about SCP-4666.

03:10:24.840 --> 03:10:30.240
He had access to all of the SCP Foundation’s
information on the monster, putting him several

03:10:30.240 --> 03:10:36.120
steps closer to what had always been his ultimate
goal: Killing the godforsaken thing.

03:10:36.120 --> 03:10:39.059
But this would be easier said than done.

03:10:39.060 --> 03:10:44.520
After all, if the Yule Man were easy to track,
the Foundation would have captured and contained

03:10:44.520 --> 03:10:45.720
him long before now.

03:10:45.720 --> 03:10:50.700
His attacks could occur anywhere across the
Northern hemisphere, making catching him the

03:10:50.700 --> 03:10:52.559
world’s largest game of whack a mole.

03:10:52.560 --> 03:10:57.241
But this was a game that Dr. Geise had more
than enough patience to play.

03:10:57.240 --> 03:11:02.880
Over the following years, Dr. Geise worked
as hard as humanly possible - Not just at

03:11:02.880 --> 03:11:08.760
the case of SCP-4666, but at every piece of
work the Foundation had to offer him.

03:11:08.760 --> 03:11:14.820
Much like his ruined childhood, Geise made
no effort to interact, no effort to form social

03:11:14.819 --> 03:11:15.660
connections.

03:11:15.660 --> 03:11:23.279
He just worked and worked and worked with
singleminded passion, soon developing a reputation

03:11:23.279 --> 03:11:28.979
as one of the most hard-working and dependable
figures in the SCP Foundation’s European

03:11:28.979 --> 03:11:29.459
division.

03:11:29.460 --> 03:11:35.460
It was this kind of work ethic that soon saw
him promoted to Senior researcher, and then,

03:11:35.460 --> 03:11:36.600
a site director.

03:11:36.600 --> 03:11:42.180
He had more control over information, resources,
and manpower than ever before.

03:11:42.180 --> 03:11:44.040
It was almost time.

03:11:44.040 --> 03:11:45.840
He could sense it.

03:11:45.840 --> 03:11:51.060
Delegating other tasks to his subordinates,
Dr. Geise began to work on his masterpiece:

03:11:51.060 --> 03:11:56.760
An advanced algorithm connecting police, social
media, and surveillance databases across the

03:11:56.760 --> 03:11:57.540
Northern Hemisphere.

03:11:57.540 --> 03:12:02.760
You see, while Dr. Geise had never faltered
in his quest, neither had the Yule Man.

03:12:02.760 --> 03:12:07.920
Every year, more families disappeared, or
were horrifically murdered, and every death

03:12:07.920 --> 03:12:08.940
weighed on Geise.

03:12:08.939 --> 03:12:14.819
He saw them as a personal failing of his,
of his inability to fulfil his destiny.

03:12:14.819 --> 03:12:20.519
But the Yule Man was a creature of habit - and
it would be that very adherence to habit that

03:12:20.520 --> 03:12:22.320
Geise used to destroy it.

03:12:22.319 --> 03:12:27.179
Using the algorithm he created, they would
perfectly track and triangulate the home most

03:12:27.180 --> 03:12:32.280
likely to be the subject of a dreaded Weissnacht
event, with greater accuracy and precision

03:12:32.279 --> 03:12:33.240
than ever before.

03:12:33.240 --> 03:12:35.700
The year was 2022.

03:12:35.700 --> 03:12:41.519
Dr. Geise was convinced that this time, he
would be the one to claim victory, and vengeance.

03:12:41.520 --> 03:12:46.680
There was one thing he didn’t tell his Foundation
compatriots, though, as he put the pieces

03:12:46.680 --> 03:12:51.241
in place for the final showdown: He had no
intention of containing the Yule Man.

03:12:51.240 --> 03:12:54.540
This time, he would destroy it.

03:12:54.540 --> 03:13:00.060
Using his newfound influence as a well-respected
site director, he requested the transfer of

03:13:00.060 --> 03:13:05.220
a few high-profile anomalies, as well as the
services of a crack mobile task force death

03:13:05.220 --> 03:13:05.640
squad.

03:13:05.640 --> 03:13:07.080
It was finally time.

03:13:07.080 --> 03:13:09.600
The Yule Man watched the house from a distance.

03:13:09.600 --> 03:13:13.741
He’d been observing for days, watching the
nice, happy family within.

03:13:13.740 --> 03:13:18.899
What pleasure it would bring him to destroy
them, and take what’s left to his workshop

03:13:18.899 --> 03:13:20.040
for further fun.

03:13:20.040 --> 03:13:22.740
Tonight would be the night that he claimed
them.

03:13:22.740 --> 03:13:28.439
They were all blissfully unaware, and lived
in an isolated, rural area where nobody but

03:13:28.439 --> 03:13:31.019
him would hear all the wonderful screaming.

03:13:31.020 --> 03:13:36.840
The second the lights went out inside the
house, he would strike, and bring about some

03:13:36.840 --> 03:13:37.920
Christmas fear.

03:13:37.920 --> 03:13:42.960
When the last window went dark, the Yule Man
drew a long, rusty blade out of his bag of

03:13:42.960 --> 03:13:44.580
tricks, and approached the house.

03:13:44.580 --> 03:13:49.080
He crept in through one of the windows - locks
had never been a problem for him.

03:13:49.080 --> 03:13:53.700
This whole process had played out hundreds
of times, he barely even needed to think about

03:13:53.700 --> 03:13:54.179
it now.

03:13:54.180 --> 03:13:59.820
He crept in past the presents around the Christmas
tree, found the stairs, and began to climb.

03:13:59.819 --> 03:14:04.559
He’d find them in their beds, one by one,
and take his time.

03:14:04.560 --> 03:14:08.161
Savoring the pain, savoring the fear.

03:14:08.160 --> 03:14:13.740
He went into the youngest one’s bedroom
first, his lips pulled back into a manic grin.

03:14:13.740 --> 03:14:20.040
There was the little boy, tucked in under
his quilt, like a present waiting to be unwrapped.

03:14:20.040 --> 03:14:23.399
He grabbed the sheet with his long, gnarled
fingers, and pulled it back...

03:14:23.399 --> 03:14:28.379
Only to see a plastic dummy underneath, with
a stun grenade fixed to its chest.

03:14:28.380 --> 03:14:33.240
Before the Yule Man could even react, the
stun grenade exploded into his face, blinding

03:14:33.240 --> 03:14:36.960
him with the sudden white light and ringing
in his ancient ears.

03:14:36.960 --> 03:14:41.640
The next thing he heard was a rugged, military
voice yelling, “Squad Alpha, move in!”

03:14:41.640 --> 03:14:46.920
Seconds later, the upstairs landing was filled
with Mobile Task Force Operatives with assault

03:14:46.920 --> 03:14:47.399
rifles.

03:14:47.399 --> 03:14:52.019
They aimed and opened fire into the Yule Man,
peppering him with painful bullets.

03:14:52.020 --> 03:14:54.780
He wasn’t used to his victims fighting back
like this.

03:14:54.779 --> 03:14:57.120
Truthfully, it even caught him off guard.

03:14:57.120 --> 03:15:02.580
But sadly for those brave Task Force operatives,
it wouldn’t be enough to kill him.

03:15:02.580 --> 03:15:06.059
The Yule Man gripped his rusty knife and lunged
at Squad Alpha.

03:15:06.060 --> 03:15:11.161
Moments later, they were all dead, and the
furious Yule Man was trudging back down the

03:15:11.160 --> 03:15:11.639
stairs.

03:15:11.640 --> 03:15:14.520
There had been no satisfaction in this slaughter.

03:15:14.520 --> 03:15:16.380
How had they caught him like this?

03:15:16.380 --> 03:15:20.760
Perhaps he’d go eat some of his servants
in his workshop to make himself feel better...

03:15:20.760 --> 03:15:26.280
As he was walking back across the living room,
past the Christmas tree, past all the presents,

03:15:26.279 --> 03:15:28.380
the television flickered into life.

03:15:28.380 --> 03:15:34.260
Staring at him from the screen was the grinning
face of Dr. Siegfried Geise, now fifty years

03:15:34.260 --> 03:15:34.560
old.

03:15:34.560 --> 03:15:40.201
And the most insane part was that the Yule
Man somehow recognized him: The one who got

03:15:40.200 --> 03:15:42.779
away, 41 years prior.

03:15:42.779 --> 03:15:48.779
“Hello, old friend, you’re looking a little
glum,” Siegfried said on the screen.

03:15:48.779 --> 03:15:53.099
“I take it that you didn’t enjoy the surprise
party I just threw for you.

03:15:53.100 --> 03:15:56.040
Don’t worry, the night isn’t over yet.

03:15:56.040 --> 03:16:00.479
After all, you haven’t even opened your
presents.”

03:16:00.479 --> 03:16:05.399
With that, the presents under the tree - which
were actually packed with C4 plastic explosives

03:16:05.399 --> 03:16:09.779
- detonated, leveling the entire house with
the Yule Man still inside it.

03:16:09.780 --> 03:16:15.120
The explosion could be heard for miles, as
a great pillar of smoke rose up into the air.

03:16:15.120 --> 03:16:20.460
Several hundred meters away, Dr. Siegfried
Geise waited and watched, flanked by Foundation

03:16:20.460 --> 03:16:25.380
agents and more Mobile Task Force soldiers,
taking in the majesty of the explosion.

03:16:25.380 --> 03:16:28.859
Dr. Geise remained steely-eyed and watched
the blaze.

03:16:28.859 --> 03:16:33.120
Somehow, he knew that even that would not
be enough to end the monster permanently.

03:16:33.120 --> 03:16:35.580
He nodded to the lieutenant he’d brought
with him.

03:16:36.600 --> 03:16:38.580
“Squad Bravo, time to engage!”

03:16:38.580 --> 03:16:40.559
the man barked in response.

03:16:40.560 --> 03:16:46.140
The remaining agents and MTF Operatives, armed
with assault rifles and submachine guns, descended

03:16:46.140 --> 03:16:47.760
on the burning wreckage of the house.

03:16:47.760 --> 03:16:54.600
Moments later, a tall, dark figure - the Yule
Man, injured, but alive - crawled out of the

03:16:54.600 --> 03:16:55.140
blaze.

03:16:55.140 --> 03:16:59.340
Immediately, the team opened fire, giving
the monster everything they had.

03:16:59.340 --> 03:17:02.160
But once again, everything was not enough.

03:17:02.160 --> 03:17:07.260
The creature pulled out two long, rusty knives,
and leaped into the fray, making short work

03:17:07.260 --> 03:17:08.460
of the Foundation brass.

03:17:08.460 --> 03:17:12.060
Soon enough, it stood alone on bloodstained
snow.

03:17:12.060 --> 03:17:14.701
Until another bullet caught it in the head.

03:17:14.700 --> 03:17:20.460
Dr. Siegfried Geise was standing there, the
last one alive to face the Yule Man, leveling

03:17:20.460 --> 03:17:21.300
a revolver.

03:17:21.300 --> 03:17:22.860
The Yule Man scowled.

03:17:22.859 --> 03:17:25.259
Geise held his steely gaze.

03:17:25.260 --> 03:17:30.840
“We meet again, at long last,” Siegfried
said.

03:17:30.840 --> 03:17:32.400
“You killed my family.

03:17:32.399 --> 03:17:35.519
You’ve killed so many others.

03:17:35.520 --> 03:17:39.121
Tonight, it’s your turn, Yule Man.

03:17:39.120 --> 03:17:42.359
This is your Weissnacht.”

03:17:42.359 --> 03:17:47.219
Geise opened fire as the Yule Man charged
towards him, taking each shot in stride.

03:17:47.220 --> 03:17:51.000
Even as the Yule Man closed the distance,
Geise didn’t flinch.

03:17:51.000 --> 03:17:52.560
His resolve was steely.

03:17:52.560 --> 03:17:57.121
It made the Yule Man feel confused and furious
that he didn’t detect an ounce of fear in

03:17:57.120 --> 03:18:00.660
this man, even when he drove a knife into
the site director’s stomach.

03:18:00.660 --> 03:18:04.859
Siegfried dropped to his knees and collapsed
as the Yule Man ripped the knife back out.

03:18:04.859 --> 03:18:06.479
His gun clattered to the ground.

03:18:06.479 --> 03:18:08.399
It had been a mortal blow.

03:18:08.399 --> 03:18:11.399
And still, he seemed unfazed.

03:18:11.399 --> 03:18:13.319
“Why aren’t you afraid?”

03:18:13.319 --> 03:18:14.939
The Yule Man growled.

03:18:14.939 --> 03:18:20.219
Siegfried spat blood, and said, “You took
my family 41 years ago.

03:18:20.760 --> 03:18:25.440
Everything since then has just been killing
time until I got to see them again.

03:18:26.700 --> 03:18:31.920
Where you’re going, all you’ll ever see
are flames and torment.”

03:18:31.920 --> 03:18:36.660
The Yule Man, furious, stabbed Siegfried again
- this time, through the heart.

03:18:36.660 --> 03:18:41.220
The doctor was dying, his life’s work having
seemingly amounted to nothing.

03:18:41.220 --> 03:18:46.440
But with the last of his strength, he reached
into his coat and pulled out something - A

03:18:46.439 --> 03:18:51.179
little gift he’d requested from one Dr.
Alto Clef before putting his plan into action.

03:18:51.180 --> 03:18:58.500
Dr. Siegfried Geise lifted a photo of SCP-096
right in front of the Yule Man’s eyes.

03:18:58.500 --> 03:19:02.880
“Merry Christmas...” he said with his
last breath.

03:19:02.880 --> 03:19:04.980
The Yule Man was confused.

03:19:04.979 --> 03:19:05.879
A photograph?

03:19:05.880 --> 03:19:06.960
Was that it?

03:19:06.960 --> 03:19:11.279
Was that really this foolish little boy’s
trump card, after all this time?

03:19:11.279 --> 03:19:13.200
What a waste...

03:19:13.200 --> 03:19:19.559
Then, without a moment’s warning, SCP-096
barreled into the Yule Man, sending them both

03:19:19.560 --> 03:19:20.940
rolling across the snow.

03:19:20.939 --> 03:19:26.939
One of Siegfried’s preparations was to have
SCP-096’s containment cube temporarily relocated

03:19:26.939 --> 03:19:30.960
to only a mile away from the ambush site,
to avoid collateral damage.

03:19:30.960 --> 03:19:36.120
If everything else failed, then SCP-096 would
be their Hail Mary pass.

03:19:36.120 --> 03:19:37.859
Their finishing move.

03:19:37.859 --> 03:19:40.200
And it worked marvelously.

03:19:40.200 --> 03:19:45.779
As strong and evil as the Yule Man was, he’d
been weakened by the night’s battle, and

03:19:45.779 --> 03:19:50.340
the Shy Guy was an order of magnitude more
powerful than his sadistic foe.

03:19:50.340 --> 03:19:55.980
The Yule Man screamed in horror as SCP-096
set upon him with claws and teeth.

03:19:55.979 --> 03:20:02.339
After hundreds of years of inflicting it on
others, the Yule Man had died in terror.

03:20:02.340 --> 03:20:07.500
And Dr. Siegfried Geise had died with a smile
on his face.

03:20:07.500 --> 03:20:13.979
There’s never a good day for a supervillain attack&nbsp;
or a tense feud with your closest sibling, and for&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:13.979 --> 03:20:19.319
The Mighty Thor, this day was turning out to be&nbsp;
both. As the self-proclaimed strongest Avenger&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:19.319 --> 03:20:25.080
flung his body across the planet Earth using his&nbsp;
trusty magical hammer, Mjollnir, the Asgardian&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:25.080 --> 03:20:31.080
steeled himself for yet another confrontation with&nbsp;
his evil half-brother Loki: The God of Mischief.

03:20:31.080 --> 03:20:36.660
As per usual, Loki was using terrorizing the&nbsp;
humans of a random city, using his illusions to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:36.660 --> 03:20:42.960
manifest imaginary dragons and other ferocious&nbsp;
beasts. And what was also usual is Thor had no&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:42.960 --> 03:20:48.540
idea what his brother’s motive or endgame was.&nbsp;
All the God of Thunder knew was that this kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:48.540 --> 03:20:53.040
of tricky-dickery would not be stood for.&nbsp;
While these terrible creatures weren’t real,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:53.040 --> 03:20:58.019
they were still very frightening to the&nbsp;
earthlings. Civilians were running around in every&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:20:58.020 --> 03:21:03.661
direction away from Loki, and this was causing&nbsp;
all kinds of chaos and traffic disturbances.

03:21:03.660 --> 03:21:08.819
Regardless of what Loki’s plan was, Thor knew&nbsp;
that his brother had to be stopped before&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:08.819 --> 03:21:13.559
things escalated further. On Earth, humans&nbsp;
needed to rely on vehicles to get around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:13.560 --> 03:21:18.241
and even the slightest inconvenience in&nbsp;
transportation could lead to disaster.

03:21:18.240 --> 03:21:22.859
Thor touched down in the same city square&nbsp;
where Loki was unleashing his horde of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:22.859 --> 03:21:27.120
make-believe monsters: landing in&nbsp;
a classically cool superhero way.

03:21:27.120 --> 03:21:32.160
“Hello, Brother.” Thor proclaimed. “I don’t&nbsp;
suppose you’ll be stopping this without a fight.”

03:21:32.160 --> 03:21:34.979
Loki smiled a mischievous smile and replied.

03:21:34.979 --> 03:21:41.339
“Ah, Thor. We meet again. And once again,&nbsp;
you’re wrong! I can stop this any time I want!”

03:21:41.340 --> 03:21:43.980
Loki swiftly moved his hands through the air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:43.979 --> 03:21:49.679
casting a new spell to dismiss his diabolical&nbsp;
decoys. The illusions dispersed naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:21:49.680 --> 03:21:53.220
and soon the two brothers were&nbsp;
facing each other one on one.

03:21:53.220 --> 03:21:57.420
“See!” Loki chuckled.

03:21:57.420 --> 03:22:01.319
Thor was rightly confused by his&nbsp;
brother’s unpredictable actions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:01.319 --> 03:22:04.859
He pointed Mjollnir towards&nbsp;
Loki in an accusatory manner.

03:22:04.859 --> 03:22:10.439
“No illusions? No sorcery? What are you&nbsp;
really playing at, Loki?” said Thor.

03:22:10.439 --> 03:22:15.599
And then, Loki reached into his robes and&nbsp;
produced an unusual cube-shaped object. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:15.600 --> 03:22:20.040
his good brother was all too aware, this&nbsp;
particular cube was the latest in a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:20.040 --> 03:22:25.080
line of mysterious and powerful cubes that&nbsp;
Loki had used for his various evil plans.

03:22:25.080 --> 03:22:28.439
Loki juggled the unknown cube&nbsp;
from one hand to the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:28.439 --> 03:22:32.580
then waved it in the air in front of&nbsp;
Thor and began to laugh maniacally.

03:22:32.580 --> 03:22:36.540
“All I need to do is open&nbsp;
this ancient box of mysteries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:36.540 --> 03:22:39.000
and I’ll become completely unstoppable!”

03:22:39.000 --> 03:22:45.300
“Don’t you dare do it, Loki!” Thor shouted. “That&nbsp;
sort of power will be misused in your hands! ”

03:22:45.300 --> 03:22:46.860
“That’s precisely the point!”

03:22:46.859 --> 03:22:52.859
Loki continued to laugh, and reached for what&nbsp;
appeared to be the lid of the cube. He grabbed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:52.859 --> 03:22:58.380
top of it and made a show of beginning to twist.&nbsp;
This sent Thor’s heroic instincts into high gear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:22:58.380 --> 03:23:03.720
and he threw Mjollnir at the cube, knocking&nbsp;
it out of Loki’s grasp. As the cube flew&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:03.720 --> 03:23:07.620
away from the grinning villain, Thor dove&nbsp;
to retrieve it before his brother had the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:07.620 --> 03:23:12.059
chance. He soon held the strange item in one&nbsp;
hand while calling Mjollnir back to the other.

03:23:12.060 --> 03:23:17.400
As Thor stared at the cube, pondering its purpose,&nbsp;
Loki stretched out his hands in desperation.

03:23:17.399 --> 03:23:22.559
“Oh no, not my cube! If you open it&nbsp;
now, my plan is doomed!” Loki pleaded.

03:23:22.560 --> 03:23:27.810
Thor responded with a laugh of his own. “Nice&nbsp;
try, brother! But I’m going to open it now!”

03:23:27.810 --> 03:23:32.640
And so The God of Thunder did. Thor popped&nbsp;
the cube’s lid and braced himself for whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:32.640 --> 03:23:38.280
cosmic force was about to be released. But for&nbsp;
some reason, nothing happened. Still curious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:38.279 --> 03:23:42.719
Thor took a cautious look inside. As&nbsp;
it turned out, the only thing within&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:42.720 --> 03:23:47.520
this apparently nonmagical cube was what&nbsp;
appeared to be a fully-developed photograph.

03:23:47.520 --> 03:23:50.161
“What in Odin’s name is this?”

03:23:50.160 --> 03:23:55.800
Thor removed the image and held it close to&nbsp;
his face. It appeared to depict an emaciated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:23:55.800 --> 03:24:02.880
pale humanoid with long, spindly limbs standing&nbsp;
in the corner of a compact room with smooth metal&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:02.880 --> 03:24:08.100
walls. In all his many years traveling the&nbsp;
nine realms, Thor had never seen anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:08.100 --> 03:24:13.320
like it. Whatever the creature actually&nbsp;
was, it certainly did not look happy.

03:24:13.319 --> 03:24:19.620
Of course, what Thor was actually looking at&nbsp;
was a photo taken of SCP-096 inside of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:19.620 --> 03:24:25.380
containment unit at The Foundation. The Shy Guy,&nbsp;
as its nickname suggested, did not like having&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:25.380 --> 03:24:31.680
its face looked at, even via secondary means&nbsp;
such as video recording or photography. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:31.680 --> 03:24:36.780
anomalous humanoid’s sense of shame about its own&nbsp;
appearance was so great that a mere moment after&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:36.779 --> 03:24:44.099
Thor fixed his Asgardian eyes upon its visage,&nbsp;
SCP-096 began to stir in its containment unit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:44.100 --> 03:24:50.040
The creature wailed and covered its face with&nbsp;
both of the hands on its long, sinewy arms.

03:24:50.040 --> 03:24:53.700
It screamed and carried on for&nbsp;
the duration of two minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:24:53.700 --> 03:25:00.179
after which point SCP-096 sprinted towards&nbsp;
the steel walls of its containment unit and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:00.180 --> 03:25:06.300
smashed through. Reaching speeds greater&nbsp;
than most motor vehicles, SCP-096 began to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:06.300 --> 03:25:11.520
zero in on Thor’s location. As was always&nbsp;
the case when another being saw its face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:11.520 --> 03:25:17.100
The Shy Guy was compelled to utterly annihilate&nbsp;
the offending observer with its bare hands.

03:25:17.100 --> 03:25:23.040
Thor himself had no idea that SCP-096 was en&nbsp;
route and continued to squint at the photograph,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:23.040 --> 03:25:28.979
perplexed by the mysterious entity depicted&nbsp;
therein. Loki now resumed his mad laughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:28.979 --> 03:25:33.359
because once more, he had managed&nbsp;
to trick his foolish brother into&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:33.359 --> 03:25:35.880
falling for one of his ingenious schemes.

03:25:35.880 --> 03:25:40.080
“Make sure to get a good look,&nbsp;
brother.” Loki gloated. “Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:40.080 --> 03:25:44.279
look at the fell creature that will&nbsp;
bring about your inevitable destruction!”

03:25:44.279 --> 03:25:49.739
Thor was annoyed. “If you think that showing&nbsp;
me a gross picture of some lowly wretch&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:49.739 --> 03:25:54.059
will strike fear into my heart, you’ve truly&nbsp;
forgotten what it means to be an Asgardian!”

03:25:54.060 --> 03:25:57.420
“Save your sentiments for the Halls of Valhalla!”&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:25:57.420 --> 03:26:01.200
The green-clad evildoer cackled.&nbsp;
“You’ll be there soon enough.”

03:26:01.200 --> 03:26:06.000
And with that, Loki used a charm to turn&nbsp;
himself invisible and vanished completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:06.000 --> 03:26:10.920
from Thor’s sight. Thor stomped his foot&nbsp;
on the ground angrily. He wasn’t sure how,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:10.920 --> 03:26:14.640
but it seemed as though Loki had&nbsp;
gotten the last laugh once again.

03:26:14.640 --> 03:26:19.560
“Loki!” He shouted after his brother.&nbsp;
“Come back here and face me with honor!”

03:26:19.560 --> 03:26:22.860
Then the God of Thunder sighed&nbsp;
pensively and thought to himself:&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:22.859 --> 03:26:25.439
Who am I kidding? He’s never going to change.

03:26:25.439 --> 03:26:28.439
Thor looked at his trusty hammer. “Mjollnir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:28.439 --> 03:26:33.059
old friend. It seems we’ve saved the day yet&nbsp;
again. Shall we away back to Avenger’s HQ?”

03:26:33.060 --> 03:26:36.780
He then shook Mjollnir up and down&nbsp;
like a puppeteer simulating a nod.

03:26:36.779 --> 03:26:41.580
“Splendid idea, Mjollnir. Glad you thought of&nbsp;
it.” Thor chuckled, already in better spirits.

03:26:41.580 --> 03:26:47.399
That’s when he began to hear the footsteps&nbsp;
of SCP-096 rapidly approaching him. Thor&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:47.399 --> 03:26:51.059
turned his head towards the sound and&nbsp;
saw the same disquieting creature from&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:51.060 --> 03:26:56.340
the photograph that was inside Loki’s cube&nbsp;
zooming headlong towards him with reckless&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:26:56.340 --> 03:27:00.779
abandon. The Asgardian blinked. He&nbsp;
almost couldn’t believe his eyes.

03:27:00.779 --> 03:27:04.019
Whatever that thing was, it was faster than Thor&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:04.020 --> 03:27:08.701
could have possibly anticipated. Was it&nbsp;
looking for the photograph? He wondered.

03:27:08.700 --> 03:27:12.479
“Hello there!” Thor called&nbsp;
out as SCP-096 continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:12.479 --> 03:27:14.819
to make a beeline at the caped superhero.

03:27:14.819 --> 03:27:19.259
The God of Thunder waved the&nbsp;
photograph of SCP-096 in the air.

03:27:19.260 --> 03:27:21.960
“Is this your selfie?”

03:27:21.960 --> 03:27:28.020
In all likelihood, Loki had been his usual&nbsp;
conniving self and stole that self-portrait&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:28.020 --> 03:27:32.581
from the miserable creature hoping that it&nbsp;
would mistake Thor for the thief. The God&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:32.580 --> 03:27:36.779
of Mischief had probably even used a glamour&nbsp;
to disguise himself as Thor when it did the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:36.779 --> 03:27:42.300
deed. Thor believed that all he had to do was&nbsp;
clear up this misunderstanding and he and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:42.300 --> 03:27:48.300
strange beast could go their separate ways without&nbsp;
coming to blows. It was a naive, optimistic, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:48.300 --> 03:27:53.580
ultimately incorrect view of the situation, but&nbsp;
at least the hero’s heart was in the right place.

03:27:53.580 --> 03:27:56.460
“I don’t need this so…” Thor said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:27:56.460 --> 03:27:59.399
trying to reason with the creature.&nbsp;
“You can have it back if you like.”

03:27:59.399 --> 03:28:06.420
But SCP-096 showed no signs of stopping and was&nbsp;
seemingly enraged even further by Thor flaunting&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:06.420 --> 03:28:11.700
its decidedly unflattering photograph. The Shy&nbsp;
Guy kicked the ground and shot forward with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:11.700 --> 03:28:16.859
powerful burst of speed. One of its elongated&nbsp;
arms slammed into Thor’s face with enough force&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:16.859 --> 03:28:22.080
to nearly knock the Asgardian off his feet.&nbsp;
Thor was so surprised that he instinctively&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:22.080 --> 03:28:27.359
whacked the creature with Mjollnir, sending&nbsp;
SCP-096 careening backwards down the street.

03:28:27.359 --> 03:28:31.559
It wasn’t launched more than a dozen&nbsp;
feet before its powerful arms and legs&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:31.560 --> 03:28:37.081
dug into the pavement and slowed its velocity&nbsp;
to zero. Even though the thing from the photo&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:37.080 --> 03:28:41.220
had taken a direct hit from Mjollnir,&nbsp;
it barely looked phased on a physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:41.220 --> 03:28:46.440
level. One thing was for sure, The Shy&nbsp;
Guy was still incredibly distraught,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:28:46.439 --> 03:28:50.879
and it was directing all of those negative&nbsp;
emotions towards The God of Thunder.

03:28:50.880 --> 03:28:56.340
What was this thing? Thor wondered to himself.

03:28:56.340 --> 03:29:02.100
It was fast, strong, and durable to an&nbsp;
unearthly degree. If this thing wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:02.100 --> 03:29:07.680
to engage in single combat, Thor wouldn’t&nbsp;
back down. He was an Asgardian warrior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:07.680 --> 03:29:12.780
and sometimes that meant facing challenges&nbsp;
head-on no matter how suddenly they arose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:12.779 --> 03:29:17.040
Still, his time with The Avengers had&nbsp;
taught him that having a clear reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:17.040 --> 03:29:21.300
to fight was sometimes even more&nbsp;
important than the fight itself.

03:29:21.300 --> 03:29:25.680
“Hey, quick question! What are&nbsp;
you?” He said aloud to the creature.

03:29:25.680 --> 03:29:31.320
SCP-096 didn’t answer. Within seconds,&nbsp;
it was on Thor again like a bilgesnipe&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:31.319 --> 03:29:36.239
on another bilgesnipe. The screaming humanoid&nbsp;
swung wildly, pelting its opponent with blow&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:36.239 --> 03:29:41.040
after blow. Thor tried once more to&nbsp;
communicate with his unknown enemy.

03:29:41.040 --> 03:29:46.439
“I have no quarrel with you, weird one,” Thor&nbsp;
spoke. “So take your noodle arms and begone!”

03:29:46.439 --> 03:29:51.000
For all of its relentless ferocity,&nbsp;
the humanoid abomination left countless&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:51.000 --> 03:29:55.319
openings that a seasoned warrior like&nbsp;
Thor could easily exploit. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:55.319 --> 03:29:59.099
most mortal combatants would be&nbsp;
unable to endure the brunt of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:29:59.100 --> 03:30:03.780
the Shy Guy’s attacks for long enough,&nbsp;
but true to his nature: Thor was a god.

03:30:03.779 --> 03:30:06.599
The Avenger struck back with another crushing blow&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:06.600 --> 03:30:10.440
from Mjollnir, aimed straight for&nbsp;
the creature’s sternum. Once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:10.439 --> 03:30:14.879
the monster was pushed back for a moment&nbsp;
and then resumed its maddening onslaught.

03:30:14.880 --> 03:30:18.960
A blow like that should have at least&nbsp;
busted a rib or two. Were the bones of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:18.960 --> 03:30:22.980
this horrible thing made of vibranium or some&nbsp;
other rare metal out of the depths of space?

03:30:22.979 --> 03:30:27.719
Thor realized he was now fighting on the&nbsp;
defensive. While he had faced foes in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:27.720 --> 03:30:32.220
past with much greater physical strength than&nbsp;
The Shy Guy - including his superhero colleague&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:32.220 --> 03:30:37.140
Bruce Banner’s mean, green, everloving alter&nbsp;
ego The Incredible Hulk - the sheer tenacity&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:37.140 --> 03:30:42.120
of SCP-096 meant that being careless&nbsp;
with his own protection could lead to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:42.120 --> 03:30:46.920
Thor taking unnecessary hits that would&nbsp;
eventually wear him down in a prolonged&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:46.920 --> 03:30:51.480
fight. The wiry humanoid certainly didn’t&nbsp;
show any signs of slowing down or tiring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:51.479 --> 03:30:55.139
even though it had to be expending&nbsp;
a lot of energy to move so quickly.

03:30:55.140 --> 03:30:59.100
Between his alternating deflections&nbsp;
and counterblows against the creature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:30:59.100 --> 03:31:01.980
Thor mulled over his brother’s words from earlier.

03:31:01.979 --> 03:31:07.500
Loki was right about one thing. This is a&nbsp;
fell creature if there ever was one. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:07.500 --> 03:31:09.180
as long as there is thunder in my blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:09.180 --> 03:31:14.280
I won’t allow a hairless cur like this&nbsp;
to bring me my own destruction. Think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:14.279 --> 03:31:19.139
Thor. What would Stark do at a time like this?&nbsp;
He says he’s the smart one, after all. Humor him.

03:31:19.140 --> 03:31:24.240
With his highly sophisticated tactical acumen that&nbsp;
was similar to Iron Man except for the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:24.239 --> 03:31:29.580
was much better and stronger, Thor efficiently&nbsp;
assessed his current situation. While the pale&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:29.580 --> 03:31:35.279
beast was undoubtedly violent and far mightier&nbsp;
than most terrestrial creatures, its attention and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:35.279 --> 03:31:40.500
all the brute strength that came with it had been&nbsp;
fixated solely on Thor since it arrived in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:40.500 --> 03:31:46.319
city. The Asgardian’s best guess as to why was the&nbsp;
photograph that Loki had tricked him into looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:46.319 --> 03:31:51.779
at. Devious trickster that he is, Loki must&nbsp;
have known that this thing would hunt down and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:31:51.779 --> 03:31:58.439
attempt to pulverize the latest being to see its&nbsp;
face. Which now, of course, happened to be Thor.

03:31:58.439 --> 03:32:02.219
The revelation of this fact meant&nbsp;
that Thor no longer had to worry&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:02.220 --> 03:32:06.420
about the fate of civilians if he chose to&nbsp;
relocate to a less populated battlefield,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:06.420 --> 03:32:12.000
since the creature was guaranteed to follow him.
Nobody else needed to get hurt. While running&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:12.000 --> 03:32:15.239
from a fight wasn’t exactly the&nbsp;
God of Thunder’s favorite strategy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:15.239 --> 03:32:19.500
it would massively reduce the chance&nbsp;
of collateral damage. He’d be saving&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:19.500 --> 03:32:25.140
lives in classic Avengers fashion. Captain&nbsp;
America would be so proud, not that Thor&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:25.140 --> 03:32:29.580
would have done it any differently even if he&nbsp;
didn't have the star-spangled man’s approval.

03:32:29.580 --> 03:32:35.819
SCP-096 leaped in towards Thor to deliver its&nbsp;
next walloping strike, but the Asgardian quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:35.819 --> 03:32:40.679
grabbed it by the wrist and pulled the furious&nbsp;
creature off of its feet. He lifted it up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:40.680 --> 03:32:45.600
over his head before slamming it down hard into&nbsp;
the pavement. With a bit of space between himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:45.600 --> 03:32:50.640
and his anomalous foe, Thor began to swing&nbsp;
Mjollnir around by its leathery wrist strap.

03:32:50.640 --> 03:32:55.140
The Shy Guy desperately scrambled to its&nbsp;
feet to resume the attack, but by that time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:32:55.140 --> 03:33:00.120
Thor had already thrown himself skyward.&nbsp;
He soared between the buildings using the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:00.120 --> 03:33:04.380
force of Mjollnir’s trajectory, making&nbsp;
it his mission to exit the city as soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:04.380 --> 03:33:09.660
as possible. Thor looked down to see that&nbsp;
SCP-096 was following him along the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:09.660 --> 03:33:13.739
exactly as he had predicted it would.&nbsp;
While the battle with the beast would&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:13.739 --> 03:33:16.979
most certainly continue the second he&nbsp;
dropped back to the earth’s surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:16.979 --> 03:33:22.559
he could rest assured that the place where the&nbsp;
showdown would take place could be chosen by him.

03:33:22.560 --> 03:33:26.280
He saw an evergreen forest on&nbsp;
a nearby mountain and decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:26.279 --> 03:33:28.859
that it would do nicely as a place to finish this.

03:33:28.859 --> 03:33:33.179
I’m sure no one will mind a few stray bolts&nbsp;
of lightning around here. Thor thought.

03:33:33.180 --> 03:33:38.580
The God of Thunder was amused by the prospect of&nbsp;
going all out against this being. Even though it&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:38.580 --> 03:33:41.939
probably had its reasons for wanting&nbsp;
to destroy the one that saw its face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:41.939 --> 03:33:47.279
Thor was past the point of taking chances&nbsp;
on diplomacy. This distressing creature was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:47.279 --> 03:33:52.859
after all, an adversary that Loki had plotted&nbsp;
to send after him through one means or another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:52.859 --> 03:33:59.160
and because of that, it was likely to have power&nbsp;
and guile to spare. Thor breaking a sweat to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:33:59.160 --> 03:34:04.260
vanquish the thing would be a fitting replacement&nbsp;
for the fight that he would have had with Loki.

03:34:04.260 --> 03:34:08.220
Thor’s feet hit the ground on a&nbsp;
stony mountain ledge, and once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:08.220 --> 03:34:13.860
he struck that iconic superhero landing pose.&nbsp;
Remarkably, the Shy Guy had kept pace with&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:13.859 --> 03:34:18.179
Thor almost the entire time the Asgardian&nbsp;
had been flying, smashing through every&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:18.180 --> 03:34:23.640
tree and boulder that stood in its path.&nbsp;
Even so, Thor was more than ready for it.

03:34:23.640 --> 03:34:27.720
Storm clouds rumbled overhead. The&nbsp;
sky above the mountain was trembling&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:27.720 --> 03:34:33.060
as Thor decided that it was time to bring&nbsp;
down the thunder on his unfortunate foe.

03:34:33.060 --> 03:34:34.621
Boom! Crack!

03:34:34.620 --> 03:34:39.479
The heavens flashed with electrical might as&nbsp;
Thor channeled the energy from several lightning&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:39.479 --> 03:34:43.919
bolts through mjollnir before unleashing&nbsp;
a consolidated blast of unbridled power&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:43.920 --> 03:34:49.380
onto SCP-096. The Shy Guy didn’t even&nbsp;
attempt to dodge as Thor’s thundershock&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:49.380 --> 03:34:54.120
treatment bombarded it with light and heat the&nbsp;
likes of which few mortals could withstand.

03:34:54.120 --> 03:34:59.640
Thor released a second blast, then&nbsp;
a third, surrounding SCP-096 with as&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:34:59.640 --> 03:35:04.020
much lightning as these earthly skies would&nbsp;
offer him. After keeping at this for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:04.020 --> 03:35:08.880
Thor decided to let up momentarily to&nbsp;
check on his foe, only to see that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:08.880 --> 03:35:13.680
were no signs of visible damage on the&nbsp;
creature. It wasn’t even slightly singed.

03:35:13.680 --> 03:35:17.640
The God of Thunder could feel his brother&nbsp;
laughing at him from somewhere out of sight&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:17.640 --> 03:35:22.320
and out of reach. The monstrous humanoid in&nbsp;
the forest below was proving to be far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:22.319 --> 03:35:28.319
than Thor had bargained for, but he was nowhere&nbsp;
near ready to give in and accept defeat. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:28.319 --> 03:35:32.399
maybe this wasn’t a fight worth having&nbsp;
on Earth. All Thor’s electrical attacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:32.399 --> 03:35:36.420
were doing was destroying trees, and the&nbsp;
planet was already facing unprecedented&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:36.420 --> 03:35:41.399
levels of deforestation due to the greed&nbsp;
of human CEOs and shady entrepreneurs.

03:35:41.399 --> 03:35:45.839
Thor certainly didn’t want to be part of the&nbsp;
problem, and now that it was occurring to him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:45.840 --> 03:35:50.160
he realized that he should probably try to do&nbsp;
something about that once this situation with&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:50.160 --> 03:35:55.200
The Shy Guy was resolved. It didn’t matter how&nbsp;
close of a friend Elon Musk was to Tony Stark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:55.200 --> 03:35:59.819
these overreaching humans had to be given&nbsp;
a stern talking to by The God of Thunder:&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:35:59.819 --> 03:36:02.639
with or without a hammer in his hand.

03:36:02.640 --> 03:36:06.661
Of course, Thor’s environmentalist&nbsp;
concerns had to go on the backburner&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:06.660 --> 03:36:12.420
for now since SCP-096 was advancing towards&nbsp;
him once again with no hint of slowing down.

03:36:12.420 --> 03:36:17.220
Let’s see if you can follow me back to&nbsp;
Asgard, you son of an elf. Thor thought.

03:36:17.220 --> 03:36:22.860
He held his hammer aloft and counted on Heimdall&nbsp;
to deploy the bifrost. The rainbow bridge arrived&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:22.859 --> 03:36:28.620
seconds before SCP-096’s arms would have reached&nbsp;
the Asgardian, and as expected, it instantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:28.620 --> 03:36:34.080
carried Thor into the interdimensional space&nbsp;
between his destinations. Amidst the swirling&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:34.080 --> 03:36:38.580
sea of multicolored lights, Thor shared a&nbsp;
laugh with nobody but himself and Mjollnir.

03:36:38.580 --> 03:36:44.160
“So much for Shy Guy!” said Thor. “Looks like&nbsp;
it’ll have to resolve those body image issues on&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:44.160 --> 03:36:49.380
its own time. I’ve been through something similar,&nbsp;
so I can relate. Best of luck, pitiful beast!”

03:36:49.380 --> 03:36:54.840
But while Thor was congratulating himself for&nbsp;
his strategic retreat, he almost didn’t notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:54.840 --> 03:36:59.040
that his foe hadn’t actually stopped&nbsp;
in its pursuit. As a matter of fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:36:59.040 --> 03:37:05.220
SCP-096 was currently inside of the Bifrost and&nbsp;
swiftly gaining on The God of Thunder. The Shy&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:05.220 --> 03:37:11.340
Guy was truly running in the 90s. Well, 90 miles&nbsp;
per hour or more, but it made the same difference.

03:37:11.340 --> 03:37:16.560
The creature grabbed Thor by his ankles and&nbsp;
screeched in mortified rage. Thor punched&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:16.560 --> 03:37:22.680
SCP-096 in the face, hoping to shake it off&nbsp;
but to no avail. The two opponents spun through&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:22.680 --> 03:37:27.840
the bifrost locked together in an unbreakable&nbsp;
grapple until they collided with the walls of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:27.840 --> 03:37:32.940
interdimensional space. Thor knew exactly where&nbsp;
this was going, and he wasn’t happy about it.

03:37:32.939 --> 03:37:38.339
Like a sheet of ice composed of photons, the outer&nbsp;
limits of the bifrost broke away, sending both&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:38.340 --> 03:37:45.239
combatants shooting off into the void. Seconds&nbsp;
later, a wormhole spit Thor and SCP-096 out in&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:45.239 --> 03:37:49.739
a vast junkyard filled with the remains of&nbsp;
countless spaceships and alien lifeforms.

03:37:49.739 --> 03:37:54.540
Great. I’m trapped in this garbage world again.&nbsp;
I knew I should have picked somewhere closer.

03:37:54.540 --> 03:37:58.260
Thor shook off the nauseating effects&nbsp;
of the failed interdimensional trip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:37:58.260 --> 03:38:04.560
SCP-096 was already hale and hearty for another&nbsp;
round. Thor grimaced with frustrated anticipation.

03:38:05.279 --> 03:38:10.679
“How about a quick five-minute rest?” Thor&nbsp;
implored The Shy Guy. “Gentleman’s truce?”

03:38:10.680 --> 03:38:16.440
SCP-096 roared and dashed forth, more&nbsp;
crazed than ever. Thor exhaled in futility.

03:38:16.439 --> 03:38:17.879
“I thought not.”

03:38:17.880 --> 03:38:22.980
He whipped Mjollnir around and hurled it&nbsp;
straight for SCP-096’s skull. The reliable&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:22.979 --> 03:38:27.899
uru-steal hammer made contact, knocking the&nbsp;
creature onto its back. At the very least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:27.899 --> 03:38:32.519
Thor could always count on his signature&nbsp;
weapon to help him out. Or could he…?

03:38:32.520 --> 03:38:36.000
When the Asgardian tried to call the&nbsp;
hammer back, he was shocked to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:36.000 --> 03:38:40.680
that SCP-096 had somehow grabbed ahold&nbsp;
of its handle. Even more surprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:40.680 --> 03:38:44.460
it now appeared to be wielding the&nbsp;
hammer as easily as Thor could.

03:38:44.460 --> 03:38:48.359
Son of a… You’re telling me that thing is worthy?

03:38:48.359 --> 03:38:53.700
The Shy Guy, now with Mjollnir in hand, pounced&nbsp;
on Thor, swinging the hammer with no technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:53.700 --> 03:38:58.739
or finesse. It was everything Thor could do&nbsp;
to simply avoid the creature’s range. A blow&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:38:58.739 --> 03:39:03.479
from Mjollnir was enough to cause serious injury&nbsp;
to even the most formidable Asgardian warrior.

03:39:03.479 --> 03:39:08.759
“Damn you, Mjollnir! We talked about this!”&nbsp;
Thor thundered. “We’re friends, aren’t we?”

03:39:08.760 --> 03:39:14.580
But none of his words could get through to the&nbsp;
hammer now that it was in the hand of SCP-096.

03:39:14.580 --> 03:39:19.260
In an act of righteous defiance, Thor&nbsp;
called the fury of lightning into his&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:19.260 --> 03:39:23.940
own body. This strange foe which had come&nbsp;
from Loki’s latest prank was about to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:23.939 --> 03:39:28.500
out what happened when a God of Thunder got&nbsp;
serious. With the speed of a lightning bolt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:28.500 --> 03:39:33.960
Thor flew at SCP-096 and punched it so&nbsp;
hard that its grip loosened on Mjollnir&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:33.960 --> 03:39:38.939
for all but a second. And that second was&nbsp;
all Thor needed to reclaim his weapon.

03:39:38.939 --> 03:39:43.500
His hand clung tightly to Mjollnir’s handle&nbsp;
as he swung the weapon into the Shy Guy’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:43.500 --> 03:39:48.779
body with all his might. The shockwave of this&nbsp;
forceful blow launched piles of debris high into&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:48.779 --> 03:39:54.359
the air and sent SCP-096 tumbling across&nbsp;
the surface of the planet like a thrown&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:54.359 --> 03:39:58.920
piece of discarded food at an Asgardian&nbsp;
feast. With another pulse of electricity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:39:58.920 --> 03:40:03.480
Thor magnetized the portions of the rubble&nbsp;
which contained spaceship parts and used his&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:03.479 --> 03:40:09.179
manipulation of that same energy to bury the Shy&nbsp;
Guy in the deepest heap of trash he could manage.

03:40:09.180 --> 03:40:14.640
And with one last flick of Mjollnir, Thor sent&nbsp;
himself hurtling through the largest wormhole in&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:14.640 --> 03:40:20.100
the sky above and back into space where it would&nbsp;
be far more difficult for his foe to follow him.

03:40:20.100 --> 03:40:23.940
Finally, with some distance,&nbsp;
Thor breathed with relief.

03:40:23.939 --> 03:40:29.219
You almost had me that time, Loki. What&nbsp;
an unusual being. Fought like a berserker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:29.220 --> 03:40:32.700
but was somehow also pure of&nbsp;
heart. A kindred spirit to myself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:40:32.700 --> 03:40:36.179
perhaps? Even so, I truly hope&nbsp;
to never meet that thing again.

03:40:36.180 --> 03:40:42.300
Everyone in the sleepy mountain town had heard
stories about the abandoned mall, and the

03:40:42.300 --> 03:40:45.420
malevolent creature that lurked in the wreckage
there.

03:40:45.420 --> 03:40:52.020
There had been enough missing persons cases,
lost children, teenagers sneaking out to explore

03:40:52.020 --> 03:40:57.121
and never returning home, urban explorers
who stumbled out of the darkness disheveled

03:40:57.120 --> 03:41:01.680
and traumatized, with an eyewitness account
no one wanted to believe.

03:41:01.680 --> 03:41:06.780
There wasn't anyone left in the surrounding
area willing to set foot in that cursed place.

03:41:06.779 --> 03:41:11.700
But that didn't stop opportunistic out-of-towners
from paying the occasional visit.

03:41:11.700 --> 03:41:16.800
Especially if they felt like there was money
to be made in those crumbling corporate walls.

03:41:16.800 --> 03:41:22.620
Grant and Jason, the hosts of up-and-coming
investigative reality show "Ghostly Happenings,"

03:41:22.620 --> 03:41:24.720
were two such visitors.

03:41:24.720 --> 03:41:29.520
When they heard the rumors about an abandoned
mall haunted by some sort of dark presence

03:41:29.520 --> 03:41:34.621
blamed for the disappearance of local children,
they packed up their van, picked up their

03:41:34.620 --> 03:41:39.059
underpaid cameraman Derek, and headed straight
for the infamous building.

03:41:39.060 --> 03:41:41.460
"Are you sure we're allowed to be here?"

03:41:41.460 --> 03:41:43.800
Derek asked shakily as he began filming.

03:41:43.800 --> 03:41:44.640
"Nope!"

03:41:44.640 --> 03:41:46.560
Grant answered cheerfully.

03:41:46.560 --> 03:41:49.140
"It's part of the fun," Jason laughed.

03:41:49.140 --> 03:41:51.600
"Look man, this place has been left to rot
by the city.

03:41:51.600 --> 03:41:53.760
No one cares if we're shooting some footage.

03:41:53.760 --> 03:41:58.200
Besides, worry less about the cops and more
about...the ghost of the mall."

03:41:58.200 --> 03:41:59.040
Grant grinned.

03:41:59.040 --> 03:42:00.240
"OooooOOooOooo."

03:42:00.240 --> 03:42:02.820
"Not funny," Derek grumbled.

03:42:02.819 --> 03:42:04.200
"Come on, serious faces on!

03:42:04.200 --> 03:42:04.739
We're rolling."

03:42:04.739 --> 03:42:09.120
Jason nudged his cohost, and the two snapped
into their on-camera personalities.

03:42:09.120 --> 03:42:15.239
"Once a bustling center of suburban American
life, this shopping mall has transformed into

03:42:15.239 --> 03:42:16.859
a den of terror."

03:42:16.859 --> 03:42:21.239
Grant's voice dropped into a lower register,
his expression intense.

03:42:21.239 --> 03:42:25.679
"Locals swap stories of something inhuman
that calls this place home.

03:42:25.680 --> 03:42:29.220
Something that doesn't take&nbsp;
kindly to trespassers,"&nbsp;

03:42:29.220 --> 03:42:30.540
Jason added.

03:42:30.540 --> 03:42:31.319
"A ghost?

03:42:31.319 --> 03:42:32.340
A demon?

03:42:32.340 --> 03:42:33.480
Just an urban legend?

03:42:33.479 --> 03:42:35.759
That's what we're here to find out."

03:42:35.760 --> 03:42:39.660
Grant pulled out an EMF reader, which began
beeping as he turned it on.

03:42:40.319 --> 03:42:43.200
"There seems to be a lot of activity in here
already, I'm picking up something."

03:42:43.200 --> 03:42:46.679
"Don't know if you folks at home can see this,
but the meter's going crazy!"

03:42:46.680 --> 03:42:48.240
Jason exclaimed.

03:42:48.239 --> 03:42:53.460
It was a fake meter, of course, one they'd
rigged up to beep and react when Grant pressed

03:42:53.460 --> 03:42:54.960
certain hidden buttons.

03:42:54.960 --> 03:42:57.239
But the audience didn't need to know that.

03:42:57.239 --> 03:43:02.099
They were tuning in for story, for spectacle,
not for the truth.

03:43:02.100 --> 03:43:06.300
If they wanted that, they could flip over
to the nature channel and watch some lions

03:43:06.300 --> 03:43:07.440
chasing a gazelle.

03:43:07.439 --> 03:43:10.679
On this show, they were&nbsp;
chasing pure entertainment.

03:43:10.680 --> 03:43:13.260
They continued deeper into the building, taking&nbsp;

03:43:13.260 --> 03:43:16.800
in the massive, echoey&nbsp;
space, the dusty storefronts,

03:43:16.800 --> 03:43:21.540
the beams of rotten wood strewn across the
ground, the rats that skittered across the

03:43:21.540 --> 03:43:21.779
floor.

03:43:21.779 --> 03:43:24.179
It was the perfect creepy atmosphere.

03:43:24.180 --> 03:43:28.440
Maybe a little too perfect, a little too creepy.

03:43:28.439 --> 03:43:29.219
"What was that?!"

03:43:29.220 --> 03:43:32.880
Derek practically jumped out of his skin,
spinning around to look at something behind

03:43:32.880 --> 03:43:33.060
him.

03:43:33.060 --> 03:43:34.320
"Hey, stay on us!"

03:43:34.319 --> 03:43:35.460
Grant snapped.

03:43:35.460 --> 03:43:38.100
"I saw something moving back there," Derek
insisted.

03:43:38.100 --> 03:43:39.360
"It was a rat."

03:43:39.359 --> 03:43:40.859
Jason rolled his eyes.

03:43:40.859 --> 03:43:42.359
"There are tons of them in here."

03:43:42.359 --> 03:43:43.559
Derek shook his head.

03:43:43.560 --> 03:43:45.000
"No, it wasn't a rat.

03:43:45.000 --> 03:43:46.020
It was way too big."

03:43:46.020 --> 03:43:47.581
"Okay, a raccoon then.

03:43:47.580 --> 03:43:48.600
Come on, get it together.

03:43:48.600 --> 03:43:50.640
We've been way worse locations than this.

03:43:50.640 --> 03:43:52.201
Remember the old tuberculosis hospital?"

03:43:52.200 --> 03:43:53.580
Grant shuddered.

03:43:54.420 --> 03:43:55.500
"Now that was scary.

03:43:55.500 --> 03:43:56.580
This is nothing."

03:43:56.580 --> 03:43:58.739
"Well, not nothing."

03:43:58.739 --> 03:44:00.479
Jason elbowed his partner.

03:44:00.479 --> 03:44:02.399
"I did a good job scouting this place, right?"

03:44:02.399 --> 03:44:05.939
Grant was about to answer, when his foot bumped
something on the ground.

03:44:05.939 --> 03:44:10.859
He looked down, and saw a polaroid camera
next to a long-empty water bottle, and a small

03:44:10.859 --> 03:44:11.399
backpack.

03:44:11.399 --> 03:44:13.139
"Oh man, get a shot of this!"

03:44:13.140 --> 03:44:15.720
Derek obliged, while Jason took a closer look.

03:44:15.720 --> 03:44:18.720
"This must've belonged to one of the urban
explorer types who came in here."

03:44:18.720 --> 03:44:20.760
He unzipped the backpack, rummaging around.

03:44:20.760 --> 03:44:21.780
"Hey, look!

03:44:21.779 --> 03:44:23.160
Photos, tons of them."

03:44:23.160 --> 03:44:24.840
He dumped the pictures out onto the ground.

03:44:24.840 --> 03:44:28.319
"Think he got some spirit photography done
before he bit the dust?"

03:44:28.319 --> 03:44:29.099
"Might as well see..."

03:44:29.100 --> 03:44:33.840
Grant joined Jason on the ground, sifting
through the photos as Derek watched anxiously.

03:44:33.840 --> 03:44:37.620
"Boring, boring, deer, dog, sunset- now wait
a minute..."

03:44:37.620 --> 03:44:40.260
He stopped, holding up one specific polaroid.

03:44:40.260 --> 03:44:42.060
"This is the money shot right here.

03:44:42.060 --> 03:44:43.020
Derek, zoom in on this."

03:44:43.020 --> 03:44:46.800
All three men looked at the picture, illuminated
by the camera's light.

03:44:46.800 --> 03:44:52.380
It showed a tall, long-limbed, pale creature,
vaguely humanoid in shape but at the same

03:44:52.380 --> 03:44:54.480
time distinctly not quite human.

03:44:54.479 --> 03:44:59.639
Its mouth was stretched wide in a wordless
screech, its eyes bloodshot and wild.

03:44:59.640 --> 03:45:03.000
Its long, tapered fingers were reaching toward
the camera.

03:45:03.000 --> 03:45:08.040
"Damn, that's spooky," Jason laughed, a bit
of nervousness creeping into his voice.

03:45:08.040 --> 03:45:10.080
"How do you think he set this up?"

03:45:10.080 --> 03:45:11.220
Grant asked.

03:45:11.220 --> 03:45:12.120
"What if it's real?"

03:45:12.120 --> 03:45:14.279
Derek swallowed the lump in his throat.

03:45:14.279 --> 03:45:15.899
"Don't be an idiot."

03:45:15.899 --> 03:45:16.859
Grant scoffed.

03:45:16.859 --> 03:45:21.899
But then, from outside, not one hundred feet
away, they heard a scream.

03:45:21.899 --> 03:45:27.179
Ear-splittingly loud, filled with rage, pain,
and animalistic instinct.

03:45:27.180 --> 03:45:30.240
It was like nothing any of them had ever heard
before.

03:45:30.239 --> 03:45:35.880
The scream was followed by the sound of splitting
wood, branches crashing to the ground, and,

03:45:35.880 --> 03:45:39.600
horrifyingly, the sound of something breaking
down a nearby wall.

03:45:39.600 --> 03:45:40.920
"What did you do?!"

03:45:40.920 --> 03:45:42.540
Jason backed away from the sound.

03:45:42.540 --> 03:45:43.439
"Me?!

03:45:43.439 --> 03:45:44.759
You opened the backpack!"

03:45:44.760 --> 03:45:46.020
Grant shot back.

03:45:46.020 --> 03:45:46.920
"Guys???

03:45:46.920 --> 03:45:48.480
I think we need to get out of here!"

03:45:48.479 --> 03:45:53.580
Derek watched in wide-eyed terror as two pale
hands ripped a hole in the wall in front of

03:45:53.580 --> 03:45:58.620
them, making an opening for that pale, hairless
head from the photograph to poke through.

03:45:58.620 --> 03:46:03.840
All three men prepared to make a break for
it and run for their lives, but as soon as

03:46:03.840 --> 03:46:06.660
the creature saw them, it was already too
late.

03:46:06.660 --> 03:46:12.180
With another scream, SCP-096 sprinted toward
them, grabbing each man and throwing them

03:46:12.180 --> 03:46:12.660
to the ground.

03:46:12.660 --> 03:46:18.059
The camera shattered into pieces from the
impact, and Derek skidded painfully across

03:46:18.060 --> 03:46:18.420
the ground.

03:46:18.420 --> 03:46:23.160
As he rolled onto his side to try and push
back up to his feet, he caught a glimpse of

03:46:23.160 --> 03:46:27.479
the monster opening its mouth impossibly wide,
and descending on his bosses.

03:46:27.479 --> 03:46:32.099
He really didn’t want to focus on what happened
next, but suffice to say, there was nothing

03:46:32.100 --> 03:46:33.720
of them left afterwards.

03:46:33.720 --> 03:46:38.280
Derek tried his best to escape, but he was
no match for the brute strength and speed

03:46:38.279 --> 03:46:40.080
of SCP-096.

03:46:40.080 --> 03:46:44.399
From the moment he saw its face, his fate
was sealed.

03:46:44.399 --> 03:46:45.779
There was no escape.

03:46:45.779 --> 03:46:50.819
Soon, the beeping EMF meter, the scattered
photographs, and the broken pieces of the

03:46:50.819 --> 03:46:54.420
camera were the only thing left of the crew
from "Ghostly Happenings."

03:46:54.420 --> 03:46:57.180
Their devoted fans would never get another
episode.

03:46:57.180 --> 03:47:01.800
But at least one being in the abandoned mall
could rest easy now.

03:47:01.800 --> 03:47:07.380
SCP-096 was safe again, no one was living
who had seen its face.

03:47:07.380 --> 03:47:12.600
It slowly crawled back to the hole it had
made in the wall, ready to return to the forest

03:47:12.600 --> 03:47:14.100
and climb back up the mountain.

03:47:14.100 --> 03:47:18.780
There, it could curl up beneath a tree and
stay there for as long as it wanted.

03:47:18.779 --> 03:47:24.660
But as SCP-096 reached the hole in the wall,
where it had once been able to see night sky,

03:47:24.660 --> 03:47:28.019
stars, and the trees in the distance, there
was only blackness.

03:47:28.020 --> 03:47:33.420
It was as if something dark was stretched
over the opening, blocking the outside world

03:47:33.420 --> 03:47:34.080
from view.

03:47:34.080 --> 03:47:40.380
096 reached out a hand to push the blockage
out of the way, but whatever it was slithered

03:47:40.380 --> 03:47:43.319
away from the touch faster than the pale hand
could move.

03:47:43.319 --> 03:47:48.540
The wriggling darkness crept back into the
building, weaving in and out of the shadows

03:47:48.540 --> 03:47:51.660
as SCP-096 watched it, curiously.

03:47:51.660 --> 03:47:54.300
Whatever it was, it hadn't&nbsp;
looked at the creature's&nbsp;

03:47:54.300 --> 03:47:56.880
face yet...it wasn't sure if the thing even

03:47:56.880 --> 03:47:58.080
had eyes.

03:47:58.080 --> 03:48:04.559
But then, stretching out of the darkness and
into view was a garish face, wide white eyes

03:48:04.560 --> 03:48:11.880
with massive pupils, a round nose, and a wide,
wide mouth stretched into a toothy grimace

03:48:11.880 --> 03:48:14.100
that might have once looked like a smile.

03:48:14.100 --> 03:48:20.340
This was the creature that had made the mall
its home, the apex predator of this biosphere,

03:48:20.340 --> 03:48:24.779
and it was staring directly at SCP-096's face.

03:48:24.779 --> 03:48:26.819
Its FACE!

03:48:26.819 --> 03:48:32.279
With a shriek, SCP-096 flew into a primal
rage, clawing at the catlike creature, but

03:48:32.279 --> 03:48:35.519
before 096 could reach it, the thing was gone.

03:48:35.520 --> 03:48:36.600
Where was it?

03:48:36.600 --> 03:48:42.420
Where did it go? 096 roared, tearing at the
floor, ripping up the tile and gouging long,

03:48:42.420 --> 03:48:45.120
deep scratches in the concrete beneath.

03:48:45.120 --> 03:48:50.760
A sound in the distance drew 096's attention,
and it bolted in the direction of the noise,

03:48:50.760 --> 03:48:54.600
swinging at any obstacle in its path as it
went.

03:48:54.600 --> 03:48:59.640
It ripped down a banner advertising free frozen
yogurt samples, crushed an empty cell phone

03:48:59.640 --> 03:49:03.600
kiosk, and threw a gumball machine to the
floor with a crash.

03:49:03.600 --> 03:49:09.060
It would rip the entire mall apart if it had
to, if that was what it took to destroy the

03:49:09.060 --> 03:49:12.121
being that had caught a glimpse of its face
and disappeared.

03:49:12.120 --> 03:49:15.300
Then, 096 could see it.

03:49:15.300 --> 03:49:19.140
That face, that wide-eyed, grinning face.

03:49:19.140 --> 03:49:21.840
It sprinted toward the face as fast as it
could.

03:49:21.840 --> 03:49:27.960
The Cartoon Cat was looking at 096, not from
a place in the room, but from behind the screen

03:49:27.960 --> 03:49:30.840
of a television in the electronics store window.

03:49:30.840 --> 03:49:36.540
Somehow, defying the laws of physics and reality,
it had made its way into the tv.

03:49:36.540 --> 03:49:39.720
SCP-096 didn't pause to ponder the logic of
this.

03:49:39.720 --> 03:49:41.880
There was no room for critical thought.

03:49:41.880 --> 03:49:46.739
There was only the need to shred, to consume,
to destroy.

03:49:46.739 --> 03:49:51.540
It burst through the window, sending shards
of glass in every direction, and reared back

03:49:51.540 --> 03:49:52.680
to attack the screen.

03:49:52.680 --> 03:49:58.201
It was so focused on its singular task that
it didn't notice the long black arms snaking

03:49:58.200 --> 03:50:00.779
across the floor, winding around its feet.

03:50:00.779 --> 03:50:06.420
All of a sudden, 096 lost its balance as its
legs were pulled right out from under it,

03:50:06.420 --> 03:50:10.260
those stretchy arms dragging it into the tv
screen itself.

03:50:10.260 --> 03:50:15.120
When 096 regained consciousness, it was in
a place it did not recognize.

03:50:15.120 --> 03:50:19.800
Gone was the inside of the mall, or the familiar
forest and mountains outside.

03:50:19.800 --> 03:50:27.540
Instead, SCP-096 was in some sort of hand-drawn
world, a massive house with a hallway that

03:50:27.540 --> 03:50:32.100
seemed to go on forever, lined with infinite
doors leading to who knew where.

03:50:32.100 --> 03:50:37.440
The floor tipped and tilted at odd angles,
and the geometry made no three-dimensional

03:50:37.439 --> 03:50:37.859
sense.

03:50:37.859 --> 03:50:40.979
It was dizzying, and deeply off-putting.

03:50:40.979 --> 03:50:46.919
SCP-096 had no frame of reference to compare
it to, but a human witness might have noticed

03:50:46.920 --> 03:50:51.480
that this place looked like something out
of an old cartoon, from the days of Steamboat

03:50:51.479 --> 03:50:52.500
Willy and the like.

03:50:52.500 --> 03:50:55.979
SCP-096 did not think such a thing.

03:50:55.979 --> 03:51:01.979
It only knew that it was in an unfamiliar
place, one that felt threatening, though there

03:51:01.979 --> 03:51:03.299
were no visible threats.

03:51:03.300 --> 03:51:08.040
And it knew that it still hadn't gotten its
hands on the creature that had seen its face.

03:51:08.040 --> 03:51:13.140
All along the walls, between each door, there
were clocks in the shape of that horrible

03:51:13.140 --> 03:51:20.220
cat, eyes ticking back and forth with each
second, wide smile mocking SCP-096, as if

03:51:20.220 --> 03:51:22.800
to say, "try and find me."

03:51:22.800 --> 03:51:26.160
SCP-096 would not be discouraged.

03:51:26.160 --> 03:51:28.920
There was no room in its mind for that sort
of thing.

03:51:28.920 --> 03:51:34.319
There was only the singular goal of finding
the Cartoon Cat and tearing it limb from rubber

03:51:34.319 --> 03:51:35.279
hose limb.

03:51:35.279 --> 03:51:37.800
Psychological warfare would have no effect.

03:51:37.800 --> 03:51:40.620
Still, Cartoon Cat would give it his best
shot.

03:51:40.620 --> 03:51:44.939
This was his world, and he could do whatever
he wanted here.

03:51:44.939 --> 03:51:51.179
Cartoon Cat poked his head out through a random
doorway, waving to 096 with one of his puffy

03:51:51.180 --> 03:51:52.500
white gloved hands.

03:51:52.500 --> 03:51:58.920
As 096 wailed and began to gave chase, Cartoon
Cat slammed the door shut, only to open another

03:51:58.920 --> 03:52:01.500
door down the hall and poke his head out of
that one.

03:52:01.500 --> 03:52:04.680
He did the same trick over and over, jerking&nbsp;

03:52:04.680 --> 03:52:08.580
SCP-096 around from end to&nbsp;
end of the ever-expanding

03:52:08.580 --> 03:52:09.479
animated hallway.

03:52:09.479 --> 03:52:15.899
After some time passed, however, it appeared
SCP-096 was not going to get tired, and was

03:52:15.899 --> 03:52:18.359
not going to give up hope of catching its
prey.

03:52:18.359 --> 03:52:20.639
It was time to end this.

03:52:20.640 --> 03:52:27.600
Cartoon Cat wrapped one long arm around SCP-096's
neck, restraining it against the wall, and

03:52:27.600 --> 03:52:30.060
began to squeeze tighter and tighter.

03:52:30.060 --> 03:52:31.201
But nothing happened.

03:52:32.190 --> 03:52:37.980
096 did not collapse, did not react at all
except to tear at the arm around its neck

03:52:37.979 --> 03:52:39.059
until it broke free.

03:52:39.060 --> 03:52:44.760
Then, it returned to its relentless pursuit,
tearing doorframes apart, pulling up floorboards,

03:52:44.760 --> 03:52:46.140
punching through walls.

03:52:46.140 --> 03:52:51.420
Every time it dealt damage to the environment,
the space filled itself back in, an unseen

03:52:51.420 --> 03:52:56.160
pen scribbling in the gaps in the floorboards,
sealing up the holes in the walls.

03:52:56.160 --> 03:53:01.680
Two powerful beings going back and forth,
locked in a seemingly equally-matched battle.

03:53:01.680 --> 03:53:06.300
Cartoon Cat tried to destroy SCP-096 in myriad
ways.

03:53:06.300 --> 03:53:11.940
He tried to rip the beast apart, to snap its
neck, to do any manner of unspeakable violent

03:53:11.939 --> 03:53:15.120
acts that it had done before, with great sadistic
glee.

03:53:15.120 --> 03:53:19.500
But this pale abomination would not fold,
would not break.

03:53:19.500 --> 03:53:25.380
This wasn't going to be any fun if there was
no ending, and if 096 kept destroying one

03:53:25.380 --> 03:53:27.840
of Cartoon Cat's favorite pocket worlds.

03:53:27.840 --> 03:53:29.580
That was quite enough.

03:53:29.580 --> 03:53:35.399
Cartoon Cat grabbed hold of 096's arms and
legs, the creature thrashing viciously as

03:53:35.399 --> 03:53:40.200
he did so, and threw it into the blank space
just beyond the illustrated world.

03:53:40.200 --> 03:53:44.279
Back in the mall, SCP-096 crashed back to
earth.

03:53:44.279 --> 03:53:49.139
It was about to resume its search for Cartoon
Cat, when suddenly a bright light illuminated

03:53:49.140 --> 03:53:49.920
its face.

03:53:49.920 --> 03:53:53.640
A police officer stood a few feet away, holding
a flashlight.

03:53:53.640 --> 03:53:56.220
"Scuse me, you can't be in here.

03:53:56.220 --> 03:53:57.420
You're trespassing."

03:53:57.420 --> 03:54:03.120
The officer started to warn, but his words
were interrupted when SCP-096 began to scream.

03:54:03.120 --> 03:54:06.120
Soon, he would be screaming too.

03:54:06.120 --> 03:54:11.640
That’s right. It’s time once again to&nbsp;
activate the famed Anom-O-Tron 600,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:11.640 --> 03:54:15.780
our patented machine that definitely&nbsp;
wasn’t developed in affiliation with&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:15.779 --> 03:54:21.120
Doctor Wondertainment. We swear. After&nbsp;
recalibrating, rebooting, and hitting restart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:21.120 --> 03:54:26.460
our infamous specialized supercomputer is&nbsp;
ready to bring you all manner of improbable&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:26.460 --> 03:54:31.620
scenarios. We’ll simulate encounters between&nbsp;
the SCP Foundation’s various anomalies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:31.620 --> 03:54:35.460
and any other character from any other&nbsp;
universe you could possibly imagine.&nbsp;

03:54:35.460 --> 03:54:42.239
Here’s a question for you: what do you think&nbsp;
would happen if SCP-096, the notorious Shy Guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:42.239 --> 03:54:47.639
ran afoul of someone he couldn’t just kill&nbsp;
for daring to look at it? What if the scourge&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:47.640 --> 03:54:55.740
of the underworld, battler of demons, and the&nbsp;
Devil himself, set his sights on SCP-096? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:54:55.739 --> 03:55:01.800
we’re about to hit the button and discover the&nbsp;
outcome of SCP-096 vs The dreaded Doom Slayer!&nbsp;

03:55:01.800 --> 03:55:06.180
So, you’ve heard the old philosophical debate&nbsp;
about a tree falling down in the woods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:06.180 --> 03:55:10.260
and whether or not nobody being around to&nbsp;
hear it brings into question if it actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:10.260 --> 03:55:15.900
happened at all, right? Well, let’s reframe&nbsp;
that one a little. If an interdimensional rip&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:15.899 --> 03:55:21.479
in the very fabric of the time-space continuum&nbsp;
opens up in an SCP Foundation secure facility&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:21.479 --> 03:55:25.799
and nobody is around to witness it, does a&nbsp;
six-foot-tall, three-hundred-and-sixty-pound&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:26.580 --> 03:55:30.660
hulking killing machine still emerge&nbsp;
to slaughter everything in his path?&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:30.660 --> 03:55:38.099
It turns out, yes; yes, he definitely does.
Horrified screams and the loud bang of shotgun&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:38.100 --> 03:55:44.040
blasts rang out through the hallways as Foundation&nbsp;
researchers ran for their lives. The Doom Marine&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:44.040 --> 03:55:50.220
had just been unceremoniously dropped out of a&nbsp;
portal into a strange and unfamiliar environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:50.220 --> 03:55:55.860
Mere minutes earlier, he had been awakened&nbsp;
inside a sarcophagus within a facility on Mars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:55:55.859 --> 03:56:02.099
in the year 2148. Unfortunately, Doomguy&nbsp;
hadn’t had the best start to his day given&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:02.100 --> 03:56:06.300
that the base he’d woken up in was being&nbsp;
overrun by the forces of Hell itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:06.300 --> 03:56:11.820
thanks to a rogue scientist opening up portals&nbsp;
to the underworld and allowing all manner of&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:11.819 --> 03:56:17.040
demonic abominations to come pouring through.
So, naturally, when another portal brought the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:17.040 --> 03:56:23.220
armor-clad demon slayer to a site run by the SCP&nbsp;
Foundation, the intensity of battle had led to&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:23.220 --> 03:56:29.760
the Doom Slayer believing the unsuspecting staff&nbsp;
were actually all demons in disguise. And to him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:29.760 --> 03:56:36.480
the only good demon is one he got to send back&nbsp;
to hell in the most brutal fashion imaginable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:36.479 --> 03:56:43.139
He wasn’t going to stop until he’d torn through&nbsp;
wave after wave of Foundation personnel, thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:43.140 --> 03:56:48.600
perhaps if he killed enough, he’d be returned&nbsp;
to his previous locale for more demon killing.&nbsp;

03:56:48.600 --> 03:56:53.340
As the Foundation dispatched its security&nbsp;
forces and Mobile Task Forces to take down&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:53.340 --> 03:56:59.160
the unstoppable force of carnage decked out in&nbsp;
head-to-toe green armor, it seemed nothing they&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:56:59.160 --> 03:57:04.080
could throw at the Doom Marine was enough to&nbsp;
take him down. A hail of bullets rained down&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:04.080 --> 03:57:09.420
on him from the MTFs’ weapons, ricocheting&nbsp;
ineffectually off his protective plating as&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:09.420 --> 03:57:15.239
he continued his assault, remaining tireless&nbsp;
thanks to his immense physical conditioning.&nbsp;

03:57:15.239 --> 03:57:21.239
The SCP Foundation tried and failed to contain&nbsp;
Doomguy. They had assumed him to be either a new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:21.239 --> 03:57:28.559
undocumented anomaly or a violent and unhinged&nbsp;
maniac from the Church of the Broken God, given&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:28.560 --> 03:57:34.140
his armor resembling cybernetic augmentations and&nbsp;
machine parts grafted to his body, along with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:34.140 --> 03:57:39.840
arsenal of advanced weaponry. But there were&nbsp;
no enhancements, no augmentations made to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:39.840 --> 03:57:44.640
Doom Slayer; he may have once been mortal,&nbsp;
but centuries of killing demons had allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:44.640 --> 03:57:51.240
him to transcend ordinary human physiology. He&nbsp;
didn’t tire, never lost strength, never needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:51.239 --> 03:57:57.540
to eat or drink, and was near enough immortal.
Yet, among all the chaos that was unfurling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:57:57.540 --> 03:58:03.420
a solitary researcher had just managed to slip&nbsp;
out of the Doom Slayer’s reach. He ran as fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:03.420 --> 03:58:08.100
and far as he was physically able; the Mobile&nbsp;
Task Force troops were getting eviscerated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:08.699 --> 03:58:13.199
the Slayer could easily tear through the&nbsp;
whole Foundation. Then, fleeing for his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:13.199 --> 03:58:20.340
the researcher had an idea: either a stroke of&nbsp;
genius or total idiocy, and a risky move either&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:20.340 --> 03:58:26.940
way. He’d set an anomaly on the Doom Slayer.
He turned to the nearest containment cell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:26.939 --> 03:58:32.279
a windowless room with a heavy, locked door.&nbsp;
Hurriedly inputting his security clearance code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:32.279 --> 03:58:36.840
the researcher heaved the door open and&nbsp;
covered his eyes with his arm. It wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:36.840 --> 03:58:42.960
have been his first choice of SCP to send into&nbsp;
battle, but SCP-096’s cell was the closest.&nbsp;

03:58:42.960 --> 03:58:50.699
Lumbering out of its containment chamber on its&nbsp;
misshapen, elongated legs, SCP-096 scanned the&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:50.699 --> 03:58:56.399
area. Alarms were blaring, it was loud and&nbsp;
frightening to the timid, albeit terrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:58:56.399 --> 03:59:01.259
creature, that reached up to cover its ears in&nbsp;
bewilderment. Not that the researcher who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:01.260 --> 03:59:05.880
freed it could see what it was doing. For one,&nbsp;
his eyes were still covered. And for another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:05.880 --> 03:59:11.340
he was already dead, thanks to the Doom Slayer&nbsp;
barging shoulder-first through a nearby wall and&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:11.340 --> 03:59:15.779
raining debris on the nearby researcher.
Beneath the visor of his helmet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:15.779 --> 03:59:20.939
the Marine breathed heavily, panting&nbsp;
but not tiring as he looked at the pale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:20.939 --> 03:59:25.319
skinny creature in the corridor before him.&nbsp;
It was clutching the sides of its head;&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:25.319 --> 03:59:32.160
its huge jaw hung open. As far the Slayer knew,&nbsp;
he was making progress; his attack was working.&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:32.160 --> 03:59:36.660
He’d killed enough of these demonic imposters&nbsp;
that they were starting to reveal their true&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:36.660 --> 03:59:41.819
forms. Although, in all his years of bloodshed&nbsp;
and being a scourge to the denizens of Hell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:41.819 --> 03:59:47.580
he hadn’t quite ever seen a demon that looked&nbsp;
like the Shy Guy. Not that it mattered to Doomguy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:47.580 --> 03:59:53.519
before long, it’d be dead like all the rest.
The towering, stick-thin creature turned&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:53.520 --> 03:59:58.260
to face the armor-adorned warrior and&nbsp;
shrieked, immediately overcome with anguish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

03:59:58.260 --> 04:00:03.360
having a full-blown breakdown as it often did&nbsp;
whenever someone looked at it. As the demon&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:03.359 --> 04:00:08.759
killer stomped down the corridor towards it,&nbsp;
The Doomblade, a long, sleek razor sharp edge&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:08.760 --> 04:00:12.840
extended from the Slayer’s gauntlet where&nbsp;
it was mounted on his wrist. He reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:12.840 --> 04:00:18.180
down and yanked the rip cord on his chainsaw&nbsp;
too – he was long overdue for a glory kill.&nbsp;

04:00:18.180 --> 04:00:23.520
But before he could strike at SCP-096,&nbsp;
the Shy Guy moved with impossible speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:23.520 --> 04:00:28.980
faster than even the Doom Marine himself. It&nbsp;
zipped behind the encroaching Marine, moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:28.979 --> 04:00:34.080
quicker than he could perceive: one second&nbsp;
SCP-096 was crying and wailing in front of him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:34.080 --> 04:00:39.000
then not even a full second later the creature&nbsp;
was towering over Doomguy from behind.&nbsp;

04:00:39.000 --> 04:00:45.540
SCP-096 gripped the Doomblade and snapped it with&nbsp;
ease, like breaking a twig before reaching its&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:45.540 --> 04:00:50.399
other long arm down and pulling the chainsaw&nbsp;
from Doomguy’s hand. The motorized blade was&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:50.399 --> 04:00:54.899
still running as it clattered to the ground,&nbsp;
cutting a hole through the floor and falling&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:00:54.899 --> 04:01:00.779
down into the lower level of the facility.
Enraged, as he very often was, the Doom Slayer&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:00.779 --> 04:01:07.500
reached both arms back behind his head and gripped&nbsp;
the elongated torso of SCP-096. The virtually&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:07.500 --> 04:01:13.439
immortal killing machine hurled the creature&nbsp;
over his head with ease; thanks to his immense&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:13.439 --> 04:01:19.379
strength, Doomguy could lift objects far heavier&nbsp;
than most ordinary humans, and carry heavy weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:19.380 --> 04:01:25.020
without tiring. He had even grappled hand-to-hand&nbsp;
with demons. Throwing the Shy Guy down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:25.020 --> 04:01:30.780
corridor like hurling a basketball was no trouble&nbsp;
for the spacefaring slaughterer of Satan’s spawn.&nbsp;

04:01:30.779 --> 04:01:36.779
Screaming and howling in pain, SCP-096 crashed&nbsp;
to the ground at the far end of the corridor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:36.779 --> 04:01:42.719
tumbling over itself thanks to its long limbs&nbsp;
before landing in a confused heap. As the Shy&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:42.720 --> 04:01:47.760
Guy reoriented itself, struggling back onto&nbsp;
its feet and standing up at its full height,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:47.760 --> 04:01:51.780
it was instantly knocked back as&nbsp;
the force of something struck it.&nbsp;

04:01:51.779 --> 04:01:58.859
Baring down on SCP-096, the Doom Marine charged,&nbsp;
his signature double-barrelled, sawed-off shotgun&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:01:58.859 --> 04:02:04.799
drawn. It was hardly leaving a scratch on&nbsp;
SCP-096’s pale skin, despite the moderate&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:04.800 --> 04:02:10.500
amount of damage the weapon was capable of. Two&nbsp;
shots would ring out, reeling the creature but&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:10.500 --> 04:02:16.620
not harming it. Then, by the time SCP-096 managed&nbsp;
to recover from the blast, the Slayer had finished&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:16.620 --> 04:02:22.439
reloading in time to open fire yet again. Even as&nbsp;
the Doom Slayer raced nearer, closing the distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:22.439 --> 04:02:27.779
between him and what he thought to be another&nbsp;
demon for him to kill, the shotgun seemed to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:27.779 --> 04:02:33.779
little effect against the Shy guy at close range.
Stowing the weapon, Doomguy put all of his focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:33.779 --> 04:02:38.939
and energy into his charge, heavy boots stomping&nbsp;
against the ground as he raced towards the Shy&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:38.939 --> 04:02:43.979
guy, still stunned by the last shotgun blast.&nbsp;
Before the creature could steady itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:43.979 --> 04:02:49.620
this time it was struck by another force,&nbsp;
a heavy object barrelling towards it. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:49.620 --> 04:02:55.800
Doom Slayer shoulder barged SCP-096, sending the&nbsp;
pair of them crashing through the nearest wall.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:02:55.800 --> 04:03:01.080
They burst into a neighboring room, with the Doom&nbsp;
Slayer keeping a single-armed grip around the Shy&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:01.080 --> 04:03:07.319
Guy while repeatedly delivering vicious punches to&nbsp;
its gut with his other hand, not once relenting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:07.319 --> 04:03:14.939
SCP-096 screeched in pain, clawing at the Slayer&nbsp;
but unable to tear through his impenetrable armor.&nbsp;

04:03:14.939 --> 04:03:21.719
As they crashed through yet another wall, SCP-096&nbsp;
did something it rarely ever did. In fact, maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:21.720 --> 04:03:27.660
something it had never ever done before. It ran&nbsp;
away from someone that had looked at it. While the&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:27.660 --> 04:03:32.639
creature was deeply insecure and so self-conscious&nbsp;
that it would kill anyone who saw so much as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:32.640 --> 04:03:39.000
photograph of it, it didn’t often feel afraid.&nbsp;
But the Doom Slayer, this muscle-bound killing&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:39.000 --> 04:03:45.720
machine that had brutally butchered actual demons&nbsp;
was so imposing, so threatening, and so effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:45.720 --> 04:03:53.220
at dispensing painful punishment, that he scared&nbsp;
the fight out of SCP-096. Sobbing with terror, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:53.220 --> 04:03:58.080
creature zipped away using its anomalous speed,&nbsp;
trying desperately to put as much distance between&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:03:58.080 --> 04:04:04.739
it and the Doom Marine as it possibly could.
But therein lay the problem. No sooner had&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:04.739 --> 04:04:10.800
SCP-096 gotten away from the Scourge of Hell, then&nbsp;
the creature immediately felt the familiar pull.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:10.800 --> 04:04:16.320
Whether it was an inescapable compulsion to kill&nbsp;
everyone who looked at it, or a fact of the Shy&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:16.319 --> 04:04:22.199
Guy’s biology, it could only distance itself from&nbsp;
the Slayer for a few seconds before it reappeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:22.199 --> 04:04:27.960
near him. From Doomguy’s perspective, it seemed&nbsp;
the creature was intentionally disappearing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:27.960 --> 04:04:33.420
reappearing using its teleportation-like&nbsp;
speed to evade a volley of his attacks.&nbsp;

04:04:33.420 --> 04:04:39.239
SCP-096 came at him again, stuck in a loop of&nbsp;
trying to attack the Doom Slayer for looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:39.239 --> 04:04:43.979
at it, to immediately regretting that course&nbsp;
of action and fleeing in fear of its hulking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:43.979 --> 04:04:48.899
heavy weapon-toting target. Doomguy&nbsp;
drew every weapon he had in his arsenal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:48.899 --> 04:04:55.199
launching a barrage of high explosive ordinance&nbsp;
from his rocket launcher to try and kill SCP-096.&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:04:55.199 --> 04:05:00.540
The resultant damage practically reduced an&nbsp;
entire wing of the Foundation site to rubble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:00.540 --> 04:05:05.699
but still couldn’t kill the Slayer’s opponent.
Meanwhile, in the moments that the Shy Guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:05.699 --> 04:05:11.040
temporarily forgot his own fear for the Doomguy,&nbsp;
it managed to rush towards him, swiping the&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:11.040 --> 04:05:15.359
Marine’s various technologically advanced&nbsp;
armaments out of his hands before instantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:15.359 --> 04:05:20.580
remembering why he was so scared of the green&nbsp;
armored force of destruction and dashing away.&nbsp;

04:05:20.580 --> 04:05:26.160
Doomguy’s supply of weapons had all but&nbsp;
been depleted, neither he nor SCP-096&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:26.160 --> 04:05:30.899
were getting tired out by their constant back&nbsp;
and forth. They were locked in a stalemate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:30.899 --> 04:05:36.599
until the Shy Guy tried to get back to its cell.&nbsp;
Zipping away once again, it cowered in the corner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:36.600 --> 04:05:42.000
reaching for an old, worn-out paper bag that it&nbsp;
slipped over its head. It sat down, trembling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:42.000 --> 04:05:47.580
rocking back and forth in a fetal position.&nbsp;
The Shy Guy was terrified, an overwhelming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:47.580 --> 04:05:53.100
debilitating fear gripped it… fear that&nbsp;
became pure dread as the sound of heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:53.100 --> 04:05:58.621
boots began to stomp closer and closer.
Kicking the steel door off its hinges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:05:58.620 --> 04:06:05.460
the Doom Slayer walked into SCP-096’s cell,&nbsp;
carrying something huge in his hands. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:05.460 --> 04:06:10.980
one of the most devastating weapons Doomguy had&nbsp;
at his disposal, capable of unleashing untold&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:10.979 --> 04:06:18.239
destruction: the BFG 9000. Without remorse, not&nbsp;
caring that the creature seemed to have retreated&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:18.239 --> 04:06:23.880
and was cowering away from him, the Slayer raised&nbsp;
the powerful weapon and fired an enormous ball of&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:23.880 --> 04:06:29.880
green energy. The blast engulfed the entire room,&nbsp;
tendrils snaking out from the energy projectile&nbsp;&nbsp;

04:06:29.880 --> 04:06:38.160
causing additional damage to the surrounding&nbsp;
area until… there was nothing left of SCP-096…

